/*
 * OCR Gateway GCSE Biology A (J247) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real OCR Gateway GCSE Biology A (J247) questions are context-
 * led: a student's investigation, a real-world application, a table of results
 * or a described graph opens each question, practicals are examined in context,
 * and maths skills run throughout. Every item here follows that pattern, written
 * at both Foundation-overlap and Higher demand.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: one marking point per line with (1), allow / ignore
 * / do not accept notes, max n; 6-mark extended responses use the board's three-
 * level ladder with indicative content.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const BIOLOGY_OCR_A_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  'B1.1': {
    name: 'Cell Structures',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student looks at onion cells with a light microscope. The eyepiece lens has a magnification of ×10 and the objective lens has a magnification of ×40.\n\nCalculate the total magnification of the microscope.\nTotal magnification = ×______`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `×400 ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW ×50 (lens magnifications added)` },
      { q: `A student wants to look at cells scraped from the inside of their cheek. Cheek cells are almost colourless when viewed with a light microscope.\n\nName a stain the student could add to make the nuclei of the cheek cells easier to see.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `methylene blue ✓\nALLOW other named suitable stain e.g. eosin / crystal violet / toluidine blue\nIGNORE iodine (stains starch, not used for cheek cells)` },
      { q: `A bacterial cell of the species Escherichia coli is about 2 µm long.\n\nGive the length of the bacterial cell in metres, in standard form.\n(1 µm = 1 × 10⁻⁶ m)\nLength = ______ m`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × 10⁻⁶ (m) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.000002 (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `Muscle cells in the legs of a sprinter release a lot of energy during a race.\n\nWhich sub-cellular structure is the site of aerobic respiration in these cells?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nchloroplast / mitochondrion / nucleus / ribosome`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `mitochondrion ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `Some bacteria found in hospitals are resistant to antibiotics. The gene for resistance is not on the bacterial chromosome. It is carried on a small, separate ring of DNA that can be passed from one bacterium to another.\n\nName this small ring of DNA.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `plasmid ✓\nIGNORE spelling if recognisable` },
      { q: `A student prepares a slide of onion epidermis. The teacher tells the student to lower the cover slip onto the drop of water at an angle, using a mounted needle.\n\nSuggest why the cover slip should be lowered at an angle.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `to stop / reduce air bubbles being trapped (under the cover slip) ✓\nIGNORE to stop the cover slip breaking` },
      { q: `A student draws an image of a stoma from a leaf. The drawing is at a magnification of ×1200. The width of the stoma on the drawing is 36 mm.\n\nCalculate the actual width of the stoma. Give your answer in micrometres.\n(1 mm = 1000 micrometres)\nActual width = ______ micrometres`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK THE ANSWER ON THE ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 30 (micrometres) award 2 marks\nactual size = image size ÷ magnification\n36 ÷ 1200 = 0.03 (mm) OR 36 000 ÷ 1200 ✓\n= 30 (micrometres) ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 0.03 mm (correct but not converted)` },
      { q: `A scientist takes a photograph of a chloroplast using an electron microscope. The chloroplast is 24 mm long on the photograph. The actual length of the chloroplast is 6 µm.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the magnification of the photograph.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nMagnification = ×______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = ×4000 award 2 marks\n24 mm = 24 000 µm ✓\nmagnification = image size ÷ actual size = 24 000 ÷ 6 = ×4000 ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 4 (units not converted) only if working shows image ÷ actual` },
      { q: `A mitochondrion from a liver cell is photographed using a transmission electron microscope. The photograph has a magnification of ×30 000. The mitochondrion is 45 mm long on the photograph.\n\nCalculate the actual length of the mitochondrion in µm.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nActual length = ______ µm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 1.5 (µm) award 2 marks\n45 mm = 45 000 µm OR 45 ÷ 30 000 = 0.0015 (mm) ✓\n45 000 ÷ 30 000 = 1.5 (µm) ✓` },
      { q: `The resolution of a microscope is the smallest distance between two points that can still be seen as separate points.\nA good light microscope has a resolution of about 200 nm. A transmission electron microscope can have a resolution of about 0.2 nm.\n\nCalculate how many times better the resolution of the electron microscope is than the light microscope.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 1000 award 2 marks\n200 ÷ 0.2 ✓\n= 1000 (times) ✓\nALLOW 1 × 10³` },
      { q: `A bacterium is 0.8 µm long. A textbook shows a drawing of the bacterium at a magnification of ×2500.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the length of the bacterium on the drawing. Give your answer in mm.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nLength on drawing = ______ mm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 2 (mm) award 2 marks\nimage size = actual size × magnification = 0.8 × 2500 = 2000 (µm) ✓\n= 2 (mm) ✓\nALLOW ECF for correct conversion of an incorrect µm value` },
      { q: `A student uses a light microscope at ×400 to look at a slide of plant cells. The diameter of the field of view at this magnification is 0.4 mm. The student counts 8 cells lying end to end across the diameter of the field of view.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nEstimate the length of one cell in µm.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nLength of one cell = ______ µm`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `If answer = 50 (µm) award 2 marks\n0.4 ÷ 8 = 0.05 (mm) OR 400 µm ÷ 8 ✓\n= 50 (µm) ✓` },
      { q: `The cell membrane of a human cell is about 7.5 nm thick.\n(1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m)\n\nCalculate the thickness of the cell membrane in metres. Give your answer in standard form.\nThickness = ______ m`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 7.5 × 10⁻⁹ (m) award 2 marks\n7.5 × 1 × 10⁻⁹ OR 0.0000000075 (m) ✓\n7.5 × 10⁻⁹ (m) in standard form ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 75 × 10⁻¹⁰ for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student measures the length of five cheek cells using a light microscope. The results are shown below.\n(data written for this practice question)\nCell 1: 42 µm\nCell 2: 45 µm\nCell 3: 39 µm\nCell 4: 44 µm\nCell 5: 40 µm\n\nCalculate the mean length of a cheek cell.\nMean length = ______ µm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 42 (µm) award 2 marks\n(42 + 45 + 39 + 44 + 40) = 210 ✓\n210 ÷ 5 = 42 (µm) ✓` },
      { q: `Onion epidermis cells are a single layer of almost colourless cells. A student adds a few drops of iodine solution to the slide before looking at it with a light microscope.\n\nExplain why the student adds a stain to the slide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(onion cells / structures are) colourless / transparent / hard to see ✓\nstain adds colour / contrast so structures / nucleus / cell wall can be seen (more clearly) ✓\nALLOW stain highlights / binds to particular structures` },
      { q: `A student is using a light microscope for the first time. The teacher tells them to focus on the cells using the lowest power objective lens first, and only then to change to a higher power objective lens.\n\nExplain why the student should start with the lowest power objective lens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any two from:\nlarger field of view / more of the slide can be seen ✓\nso it is easier to find the cells / area of interest ✓\nless chance of the lens hitting / breaking the slide (when focusing) ✓\nALLOW easier to focus` },
      { q: `Amoeba is a single-celled organism. A scientist carefully removed the nucleus from an Amoeba cell. The cell continued to move for a few days but could not grow or divide, and then it died.\n\nExplain why the Amoeba could not survive without its nucleus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `nucleus contains the genetic material / DNA / chromosomes / genes ✓\n(which) controls the activities of the cell / codes for proteins / enzymes (needed for growth or division) ✓\nIGNORE nucleus is the brain of the cell` },
      { q: `The flight muscle cells of insects such as bees contain very large numbers of mitochondria. Bees beat their wings over 200 times every second.\n\nExplain why these flight muscle cells contain so many mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mitochondria are the site of (aerobic) respiration / contain enzymes for respiration ✓\n(respiration) releases (a lot of) energy (for muscle contraction) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW mitochondria make / produce energy` },
      { q: `Insulin is a hormone that travels in the blood. Liver cells respond to insulin, but insulin does not enter the liver cells. The response starts when insulin attaches to the cell membrane.\n\nDescribe two functions of the cell membrane.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `contains receptor molecules (that insulin / hormones bind to) ✓\nselective / partially permeable barrier OR controls which substances enter or leave the cell ✓\nIGNORE holds the cell together\nDO NOT ALLOW fully permeable` },
      { q: `Streptococcus is a type of bacterium that causes sore throats. It is a prokaryotic cell. Cells lining the throat are eukaryotic cells.\n\nGive two differences between the structure of a Streptococcus cell and a throat cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nbacterium has no nucleus / DNA free in the cytoplasm; throat cell has a nucleus ✓\nbacterium has plasmids; throat cell does not ✓\nbacterium has a cell wall; throat cell does not ✓\nbacterium has no mitochondria; throat cell has mitochondria ✓\nbacterium is much smaller ✓\nALLOW single / circular chromosome in bacterium for nucleus point\nAnswers must be comparative or clearly refer to both cells` },
      { q: `A farmer's crop is attacked by bacteria that are resistant to a chemical spray. Scientists find that the resistance gene can be passed between different bacteria very quickly.\n\nSuggest how the structure of a bacterial cell allows the resistance gene to be passed between bacteria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `gene is carried on a plasmid ✓\nplasmids are small / separate from the chromosome so can be copied / transferred to other bacteria ✓` },
      { q: `A student looks at cells from a leaf and cells from the root of the same plant. Leaf cells contain many chloroplasts. Root cells contain no chloroplasts.\n\nExplain why leaf cells contain chloroplasts but root cells do not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `chloroplasts contain chlorophyll / are where photosynthesis happens ✓\nroots are underground / receive no light so cannot photosynthesise ✓\nALLOW chlorophyll absorbs light (for photosynthesis) for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student measures a cell image as 40 mm long. The actual length of the cell is 20 µm. The student calculates the magnification of the image as ×2.\n\nExplain the mistake the student has made and give the correct magnification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `units were not converted to the same unit / mm not converted to µm ✓\ncorrect magnification = 40 000 ÷ 20 = ×2000 ✓` },
      { q: `Specimens for a transmission electron microscope are cut into extremely thin slices, stained with heavy metals and placed in a vacuum inside the microscope.\n\nSuggest why the electron microscope cannot be used to look at living cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\ncells are in a vacuum (so they lose water / cannot survive) ✓\ncells are killed by the preparation / cutting / staining with heavy metals ✓\nthe electron beam damages the cells ✓` },
      { q: `A student looks at a thin slice of animal tissue using a light microscope at ×1000. They can see the nucleus and some mitochondria but cannot see any ribosomes. Ribosomes are about 25 nm in diameter.\n\nExplain why the student cannot see the ribosomes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ribosomes are smaller than the resolution of a light microscope / resolution of light microscope is about 200 nm ✓\nso they cannot be seen as separate objects / cannot be distinguished ✓\nIGNORE magnification is not high enough unless linked to resolution` },
      { q: `A student plans to make a slide of onion cells stained with iodine solution. The method uses a scalpel to cut the onion, forceps to peel the epidermis, and a glass slide and cover slip.\n\nGive two safety precautions the student should take.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\ncut away from the body / on a tile / take care with scalpel ✓\nwear eye protection / goggles (because iodine is an irritant) ✓\nwash iodine off skin / wear gloves ✓\nhandle glass slides and cover slips carefully / report broken glass ✓\nIGNORE lab coat\nIGNORE general statements e.g. be careful` },
      { q: `A textbook shows a drawing of a white blood cell. Next to the drawing is a scale bar. The scale bar is 20 mm long and represents an actual length of 5 µm. The cell is 60 mm wide on the drawing.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the actual width of the white blood cell in µm.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nActual width = ______ µm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 15 (µm) award 3 marks\nmagnification = 20 000 ÷ 5 = ×4000 ✓\nactual width = 60 000 ÷ 4000 ✓\n= 15 (µm) ✓\nALLOW alternative method: 60 ÷ 20 = 3 scale bars ✓ 3 × 5 ✓ = 15 µm ✓` },
      { q: `A student uses an eyepiece graticule to measure cells. At ×400, the student lines the eyepiece graticule up with a stage micrometer. 25 eyepiece divisions line up exactly with 40 µm on the stage micrometer.\nThe student then measures a cheek cell. It is 35 eyepiece divisions wide.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the actual width of the cheek cell in µm.\nActual width = ______ µm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 56 (µm) award 3 marks\n1 eyepiece division = 40 ÷ 25 ✓\n= 1.6 (µm) ✓\n35 × 1.6 = 56 (µm) ✓\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect value for one eyepiece division` },
      { q: `A student is comparing three types of cell: a palisade cell from a leaf, a cheek cell and a bacterial cell.\n\nPut ticks (✓) in the table to show which cells have each structure.\nStructure | Palisade cell | Cheek cell | Bacterial cell\nCell membrane | | |\nCell wall | | |\nNucleus | | |\nChloroplasts | | |\nPlasmids | | |`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Cell membrane: all three ✓\nCell wall: palisade and bacterial only\nNucleus: palisade and cheek only\nChloroplasts: palisade only\nPlasmids: bacterial only\n5 rows correct = 3 marks\n3–4 rows correct = 2 marks\n1–2 rows correct = 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student wants to look at cells from the lining of their cheek using a light microscope.\n\nDescribe how the student should prepare a slide of their cheek cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\ngently scrape the inside of the cheek with a (clean / sterile) cotton bud ✓\nsmear / wipe the cotton bud onto a (clean) glass slide ✓\nadd a drop of stain / methylene blue ✓\nlower a cover slip (at an angle) to avoid air bubbles ✓\ndispose of cotton bud in disinfectant ✓` },
      { q: `Before the electron microscope was developed, scientists using light microscopes could see mitochondria only as tiny dots. Transmission electron microscopes were developed in the 1930s.\n\nExplain how the electron microscope has increased our understanding of sub-cellular structures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\nelectron microscope has a much higher resolution (than a light microscope) ✓\nso can distinguish / see detail of smaller structures (such as ribosomes) ✓\nshowed the internal structure of mitochondria / chloroplasts / folded inner membrane ✓\nhigher magnification ✓\nshowed structures not seen before / e.g. ribosomes, membranes inside cells ✓\nIGNORE electron microscopes are better / clearer unless qualified` },
      { q: `A plant root tip is 1.2 cm long. The cells in the root tip are each about 30 µm long.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate how many cells there are along the length of the root tip. Give your answer in standard form.\n(1 cm = 10 000 µm)\nNumber of cells = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 4 × 10² award 3 marks\n1.2 cm = 12 000 µm ✓\n12 000 ÷ 30 = 400 ✓\n4 × 10² ✓\nALLOW ECF for standard form of an incorrect number` },
      { q: `A scientist wants to study the structure of ribosomes in a cell. She has the choice of two microscopes.\n(data written for this practice question)\nLight microscope: maximum useful magnification ×1500; resolution 200 nm; cost £2000; can view living cells.\nTransmission electron microscope: maximum magnification ×1 000 000; resolution 0.2 nm; cost £500 000; cannot view living cells.\nRibosomes are about 25 nm in diameter.\n\nExplain which microscope the scientist should use.\nUse information from the data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `electron microscope ✓\nribosomes (25 nm) are smaller than the resolution of the light microscope (200 nm) ✓\nelectron microscope resolution (0.2 nm) is small enough to see ribosomes / distinguish detail of ribosomes ✓\nIGNORE cost / living cells as reasons\nNo marks for the choice alone` },
      { q: `A student makes a biological drawing of onion cells seen with a light microscope. The teacher writes these comments:\n• "The lines are faint and sketchy, and you have shaded the nuclei."\n• "There is no title or magnification."\n• "Your label lines cross over each other."\n\nSuggest three ways the student could improve the drawing.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from:\nuse single, clear, continuous lines / sharp pencil ✓\ndo not shade ✓\nadd a title ✓\nstate the magnification ✓\nuse ruled label lines that do not cross ✓\nIGNORE make it bigger (unless linked to filling the space)` },
      { q: `Scientists discover a new single-celled organism in a hot spring. They examine it with an electron microscope. It is about 1 µm long. It has a cell wall and a cell membrane. Its DNA is a single loop in the cytoplasm, and it contains several small rings of DNA. It has no mitochondria.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain whether the organism is prokaryotic or eukaryotic.\nUse evidence from the description.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `prokaryotic ✓\nAny two from:\nDNA is free in the cytoplasm / no nucleus ✓\nhas plasmids / small rings of DNA ✓\nno mitochondria ✓\nvery small / 1 µm (eukaryotic cells usually 10–100 µm) ✓\nIGNORE has a cell wall (plants also have one)` },
      { q: `A scientist photographs a bacterium using an electron microscope at a magnification of ×12 000. The bacterium is 30 mm long on the photograph. A cheek cell has an actual width of 60 µm.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate how many times wider the cheek cell is than the length of the bacterium.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `If answer = 24 award 4 marks\n30 mm = 30 000 µm ✓\nactual length of bacterium = 30 000 ÷ 12 000 ✓\n= 2.5 (µm) ✓\n60 ÷ 2.5 = 24 (times) ✓\nALLOW ECF at each stage` },
      { q: `A student looks at a single onion epidermis cell stained with iodine, using a light microscope. The cell is long and roughly rectangular. It has a thin outer wall and a thin layer of cytoplasm lining the inside of the wall. There is a nucleus pressed against one side, and a large central vacuole fills most of the cell.\n\nDraw a labelled biological drawing of this cell. Label the cell wall, cytoplasm, nucleus and vacuole.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `drawing quality: clear continuous lines, no shading, of a rectangular cell ✓\nnucleus drawn at the edge of the cell, within the cytoplasm ✓\nlarge central vacuole drawn taking up most of the cell ✓\nall four labels correct with ruled label lines that do not cross ✓\nALLOW cell membrane label if shown just inside the wall\nIGNORE chloroplasts not drawn (onion epidermis has none)\nDO NOT ALLOW chloroplasts drawn for the third mark` },
      { q: `A palisade cell from a leaf and a bacterial cell from soil are both examined using an electron microscope.\n\nCompare the structures of the palisade cell and the bacterial cell.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any four from:\nboth have a cell membrane / cytoplasm / ribosomes ✓\nboth have a cell wall ✓\npalisade cell has a nucleus; bacterium has DNA free in cytoplasm / single loop of DNA ✓\nonly the bacterium has plasmids ✓\nonly the palisade cell has chloroplasts ✓\nonly the palisade cell has mitochondria ✓\nonly the palisade cell has a (large / permanent) vacuole ✓\npalisade cell is much larger ✓\nMax 3 if no similarity given` },
      { q: `A student wants to estimate the size of onion epidermis cells.\nThe student is given:\n• an onion, a scalpel, forceps and a white tile\n• a glass slide, a cover slip and a mounted needle\n• iodine solution and a pipette\n• a light microscope\n• a transparent ruler marked in mm\n\nDescribe how the student should prepare a slide of onion cells and use the microscope to estimate the size of one cell.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to prepare the slide\n• how to use the microscope\n• how to estimate the size of one cell.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed description of preparing a stained slide AND correct use of the microscope AND a valid method for estimating cell size, including a calculation.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of preparing a slide AND either correct use of the microscope OR a method for estimating cell size.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple description of preparing a slide OR using the microscope.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nPreparing the slide\n• cut a piece of onion on a tile, using the scalpel safely\n• peel a thin layer of epidermis using forceps\n• place it flat on the slide in a drop of water / iodine\n• add iodine solution to stain the cells\n• lower the cover slip at an angle with a mounted needle to avoid air bubbles\n• wear eye protection when using iodine\nUsing the microscope\n• place slide on the stage, secure with clips\n• start with the lowest power objective lens\n• use the coarse focus, then fine focus\n• switch to a higher power objective and refocus with the fine focus\n• turn the objective towards the slide while looking from the side, then focus by moving away\nEstimating cell size\n• place the ruler on the stage at low power to measure the diameter of the field of view in mm\n• count the number of cells across the diameter\n• cell length = diameter ÷ number of cells\n• convert to µm (× 1000)\n• repeat for several cells / fields of view and calculate a mean\n• ALLOW use of an eyepiece graticule calibrated with a stage micrometer` },
      { q: `Light microscopes were first used to look at cells in the 1600s. Transmission electron microscopes were developed in the 1930s.\nSome information about the two microscopes is shown below.\n(data written for this practice question)\nLight microscope: uses light; maximum useful magnification about ×1500; resolution about 200 nm; specimens can be living; colour images using stains.\nTransmission electron microscope: uses a beam of electrons; maximum magnification about ×1 000 000; resolution about 0.2 nm; specimens must be dead, very thin and in a vacuum; black and white images.\nA mitochondrion is about 1 µm long. A ribosome is about 25 nm across.\n\nCompare the advantages and disadvantages of the two microscopes for studying cells.\nExplain how electron microscopes have improved our understanding of sub-cellular structures.\nUse the information and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed comparison of advantages AND disadvantages of both microscopes AND a clear explanation, linked to resolution, of how electron microscopes improved understanding of named sub-cellular structures. Uses data (e.g. sizes compared with resolution).\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A comparison of some advantages or disadvantages AND an explanation of how electron microscopes improved understanding, OR a detailed comparison only.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements of differences between the microscopes OR a simple statement that electron microscopes show more detail.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nLight microscope\n• can view living cells / processes such as cell division\n• colour images with stains\n• cheap, portable, simple to prepare specimens\n• low resolution (200 nm) and magnification, so cannot see small structures\nElectron microscope\n• much higher resolution (0.2 nm, 1000 times better) and magnification\n• specimens must be dead / in a vacuum / complex preparation\n• expensive, large, needs trained operators\n• black and white images only\nImproved understanding\n• higher resolution allows small structures to be distinguished\n• ribosomes (25 nm) are below light microscope resolution, seen only with electron microscope\n• mitochondria (1 µm) seen only as dots with light microscope; electron microscope shows internal structure / inner folded membrane\n• internal membranes of chloroplasts / structure of cell membrane / nuclear membrane revealed\n• led to new ideas about the functions of sub-cellular structures` }
    ]
  },
  'B1.2': {
    name: 'What Happens in Cells',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about DNA.\n\nDNA is found in the nucleus of human cells. DNA is a polymer.\n\nWhat is meant by a polymer?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `a large / long molecule made of many repeating units / monomers (joined together) ✓\nALLOW made of many nucleotides joined together\nIGNORE large molecule unqualified` },
      { q: `The diagram shows a short section of a DNA molecule. Two strands are twisted around each other, joined across the middle by pairs of bases.\n\nName the shape of a DNA molecule.\n\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\nalpha helix / double helix / single strand / triple helix`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `double helix ✓` },
      { q: `Enzymes are found in all living cells. Catalase is an enzyme found in liver cells that breaks down hydrogen peroxide.\n\nWhich type of biological molecule are all enzymes made of?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `protein ✓\nALLOW polypeptide / amino acids (joined together)\nDO NOT ALLOW carbohydrate / lipid / DNA` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on the activity of the enzyme amylase.\n\nThe student uses a stop clock to time how long it takes for the starch to be broken down at each temperature.\n\nThe student says the results are repeatable.\n\nWhat does repeatable mean?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `when the student repeats the experiment (using the same method and equipment), the results are (very) similar ✓\nIGNORE results are the same / accurate / reliable unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW another person gets the same results (this is reproducible)` },
      { q: `In protein synthesis, a copy of a gene is made in the nucleus. This copy travels out of the nucleus into the cytoplasm.\n\nName the structure in the cytoplasm where the protein is made.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `ribosome ✓\nIGNORE ribosomal RNA\nDO NOT ALLOW mitochondria / nucleus` },
      { q: `Pepsin is a protease enzyme found in the stomach. The stomach contains hydrochloric acid.\n\nThe table shows the rate of a pepsin-controlled reaction at different pH values (data written for this practice question).\n\npH | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nRate (arbitrary units) | 6.5 | 9.8 | 7.1 | 3.2 | 0.4\n\nGive the optimum pH of pepsin, using the table.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(pH) 2 ✓\nALLOW answers in the range 1.8–2.2` },
      { q: `The diagram shows one nucleotide from a DNA molecule. A circle is joined to a pentagon, and the pentagon is joined to a rectangle.\n\nThe circle is a phosphate group and the rectangle is a base.\n\n(i) Name the part of the nucleotide shown by the pentagon.\n(ii) How many different bases are found in DNA nucleotides?`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `(i) sugar ✓\nALLOW deoxyribose\nDO NOT ALLOW ribose / glucose\n(ii) four / 4 ✓` },
      { q: `Bases on the two strands of DNA pair up with each other.\n\nOne strand of a short piece of DNA has the base sequence:\nA T G C C T A G\n\nWrite the base sequence of the complementary strand.\n\nComplementary strand = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `T A C G G A T C\ncorrect sequence ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 6 or 7 bases correct in the correct position\nDO NOT ALLOW U for T` },
      { q: `Scientists analysed a sample of DNA from a bacterium. They found that 31% of the bases in the sample were guanine (G) (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage of bases in the sample that were thymine (T).\n\nPercentage of thymine = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `cytosine = 31% so G + C = 62% / 100 − 62 = 38 ✓\n(thymine =) 38 ÷ 2 = 19 (%) ✓\nALLOW 19 (%) for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 38 (%) as final answer` },
      { q: `This question is about extracting DNA from strawberries.\n\nA student mashes strawberries in a mixture of washing-up liquid and salt solution. The student filters the mixture, then carefully pours ice-cold ethanol down the side of the tube onto the filtrate. White strands of DNA appear.\n\nExplain why the student adds washing-up liquid to the mashed strawberries.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(detergent) breaks down the (cell) membranes / nuclear membrane ✓\n(so) the DNA is released (from the nucleus / cell) ✓\nALLOW breaks down lipids in membranes for first mark\nIGNORE breaks down cell walls` },
      { q: `Human body cells contain 46 chromosomes. Each chromosome contains one very long molecule of DNA.\n\nThe DNA in one set of human chromosomes contains about 3.2 × 10⁹ base pairs.\n\nCalculate the number of nucleotides in this DNA.\n\nGive your answer in standard form.\n\nNumber of nucleotides = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.2 × 10⁹ × 2 ✓\n6.4 × 10⁹ ✓\nALLOW 6 400 000 000 for 1 mark only\nExplanation: each base pair contains two bases and each base is part of one nucleotide` },
      { q: `Insulin is a protein hormone. A molecule of human insulin is made of 51 amino acids.\n\nThe gene for insulin contains a sequence of bases that codes for these amino acids.\n\nCalculate the minimum number of bases in the gene needed to code for the 51 amino acids.\n\nMinimum number of bases = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `idea that 3 bases / a triplet codes for one amino acid or 51 × 3 ✓\n153 ✓\nALLOW 153 for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 306 (both strands counted)` },
      { q: `Proteins are made in the cytoplasm, but genes are found in the nucleus.\n\nThe DNA does not leave the nucleus.\n\nExplain how the information in a gene reaches the cytoplasm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a (complementary) copy of the gene is made as mRNA / messenger RNA ✓\n(the mRNA) leaves the nucleus through (nuclear) pores (to a ribosome) ✓\nALLOW RNA for mRNA\nALLOW transcription for first mark` },
      { q: `Amylase is an enzyme that breaks down starch into sugars. Amylase does not break down proteins.\n\nExplain why amylase cannot break down proteins.\n\nUse ideas about the active site.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the shape of the active site is complementary to / only fits (starch) ✓\nprotein (molecules) do not fit (into) the active site / cannot form an enzyme–substrate complex ✓\nALLOW enzymes are specific for first mark only if linked to active site shape\nIGNORE protein is the wrong substrate unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigates the enzyme catalase. Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.\n\nThe student adds a piece of potato to hydrogen peroxide solution and collects the oxygen in a gas syringe.\n\nThe student collects 48 cm³ of oxygen in 2 minutes.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of oxygen production in cm³ per second.\n\nRate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 minutes = 120 s / 48 ÷ 120 ✓\n0.4 (cm³/s) ✓\nALLOW 0.4 for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 24 (cm³/min)` },
      { q: `A student times how long it takes for amylase to break down starch at different pH values. The student uses iodine solution to test for starch.\n\nThe table shows the results (data written for this practice question).\n\npH | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8\nTime for starch to be broken down (s) | 240 | 150 | 90 | 120 | 210\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at pH 6.\n\nUse the equation: rate = 1000 / time\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nRate = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1000 ÷ 90 ✓\n11.1 ✓\nALLOW 11.1 for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 11.11… / 11 (not 3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of pH on amylase. The student puts one drop of iodine solution into each well of a spotting tile. Every 30 seconds the student adds a drop of the amylase and starch mixture to a new well.\n\nThe iodine solution in the first wells turns blue-black. Later wells stay orange-brown.\n\nExplain what these colour changes show.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `blue-black shows starch is (still) present ✓\norange-brown shows starch has (all) been broken down / is absent (by amylase) ✓\nALLOW starch has been digested / turned into sugar\nIGNORE yellow / brown / red for orange-brown` },
      { q: `In the amylase and starch experiment, the student keeps the test tubes in a water bath at 35 °C.\n\nGive two other variables the student should control.\n\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nvolume of amylase / enzyme ✓\nconcentration of amylase / enzyme ✓\nvolume of starch (solution) ✓\nconcentration of starch (solution) ✓\npH / use of the same buffer ✓\nIGNORE amount for volume\nIGNORE temperature (already given)` },
      { q: `A student investigates how the concentration of hydrogen peroxide affects the rate of the reaction catalysed by catalase.\n\nThe student says:\n'As the concentration of hydrogen peroxide increases, the rate of reaction keeps increasing.'\n\nAt high concentrations of hydrogen peroxide, the rate stops increasing.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `all the active sites are occupied / in use (at any one time) ✓\n(so) the enzyme (concentration) becomes the limiting factor / adding more substrate cannot increase the number of enzyme–substrate complexes ✓\nALLOW enzymes are saturated for first mark\nIGNORE all the enzymes are used up` },
      { q: `The graph shows the volume of oxygen produced by catalase against time. The curve starts at 0 and rises steeply at first, then becomes less steep and levels off at 40 cm³ after about 100 s.\n\nA student draws a tangent to the curve at 0 s. The tangent passes through (0 s, 0 cm³) and (20 s, 15 cm³).\n\nCalculate the initial rate of reaction.\n\nInitial rate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `15 ÷ 20 ✓\n0.75 (cm³/s) ✓\nALLOW 0.75 for 2 marks` },
      { q: `The table shows the rate of a reaction catalysed by a protease enzyme at two temperatures (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | 30 | 40\nRate (arbitrary units) | 0.8 | 1.4\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in rate from 30 °C to 40 °C.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(1.4 − 0.8) ÷ 0.8 (× 100) / 0.6 ÷ 0.8 ✓\n75 (%) ✓\nALLOW 75 for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student investigates catalase using pieces of liver and hydrogen peroxide. The student boils one piece of liver for 5 minutes, cools it and adds it to hydrogen peroxide.\n\nNo bubbles are produced.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(boiling) denatures the catalase / enzyme ✓\nthe active site changes shape (so hydrogen peroxide no longer fits / no enzyme–substrate complexes form) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW enzyme is killed\nIGNORE enzyme is destroyed unqualified` },
      { q: `Each enzyme in the body is made from a specific sequence of amino acids.\n\nA mutation changes one base in the gene for an enzyme. This changes one amino acid in the enzyme.\n\nSuggest why the enzyme may no longer work.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the protein / enzyme folds into a different shape ✓\n(so) the active site changes shape and the substrate no longer fits / cannot bind ✓\nALLOW no enzyme–substrate complex forms for second mark` },
      { q: `A student measures the volume of oxygen produced by catalase in yeast.\n\nThe student first collects the gas by bubbling it into an upturned measuring cylinder full of water. The measuring cylinder has a scale marked every 2 cm³.\n\nThe student then repeats the experiment using a gas syringe marked every 1 cm³.\n\nExplain why using the gas syringe is an improvement.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the gas syringe has a higher resolution / smaller scale divisions ✓\n(so) the measurement is more accurate / precise ✓\nALLOW no gas escapes / no gas dissolves in water for 1 mark\nIGNORE more reliable / fair test` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on the enzyme trypsin. Trypsin breaks down the protein in milk, so a cloudy milk suspension becomes clear.\n\nAt 40 °C, the milk suspension took 80 s to become clear.\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at 40 °C.\n\nUse the equation: rate = 1 / time\n\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\n\nRate = ______ s⁻¹`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 ÷ 80 / 0.0125 ✓\n0.013 (s⁻¹) ✓\nALLOW 1.3 × 10⁻² for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 0.0125 (not 2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `Enzymes are biological catalysts. Without enzymes, the reactions in cells would happen very slowly at body temperature.\n\nThe model in the diagram shows a substrate molecule fitting exactly into the active site of an enzyme, like a key in a lock.\n\nDescribe what happens to the substrate after it enters the active site.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `an enzyme–substrate complex forms ✓\nthe substrate is broken down / converted into products which leave the active site ✓\nALLOW substrate is split / joined together for second mark\nALLOW the enzyme is unchanged / can be used again for second mark` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on amylase. The student times how long it takes for the starch to be broken down. The student repeats the experiment three times at each temperature.\n\nThe table shows some of the results (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | Test 1 (s) | Test 2 (s) | Test 3 (s) | Mean (s)\n30 | 120 | 118 | 125 | 121\n40 | 62 | 96 | 58 | ______\n\nOne result at 40 °C is anomalous.\n\nIdentify the anomalous result and calculate the mean time at 40 °C.\n\nAnomalous result = ______ s\nMean = ______ s`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `anomalous result = 96 (s) ✓\n(62 + 58) ÷ 2 ✓\n60 (s) ✓\nALLOW ECF for mean if wrong anomaly excluded\nmean of 72 (all three included) scores 1 mark (second marking point only if method shown)` },
      { q: `DNA is made of two strands. Each strand is made of many nucleotides joined together.\n\nDescribe how the two strands of a DNA molecule are held together.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `bases on one strand pair with bases on the other strand / complementary base pairing ✓\nA pairs with T ✓\nC pairs with G ✓\nALLOW adenine with thymine, cytosine with guanine\nALLOW (weak) hydrogen bonds between the bases as an alternative to the first mark\nIGNORE sugar-phosphate backbone` },
      { q: `A student investigates catalase in potato. The student cuts discs of potato and places them in hydrogen peroxide solution.\n\nThe student measures the volume of oxygen collected in 4 minutes as 30 cm³.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production in cm³ per second.\n\nGive your answer in standard form to 2 significant figures.\n\nRate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4 minutes = 240 s / 30 ÷ 240 ✓\n0.125 ✓\n1.3 × 10⁻¹ (cm³/s) ✓\nALLOW 1.2 × 10⁻¹ (rounding from 0.125)\nALLOW ECF from incorrect time conversion for the standard form mark` },
      { q: `The table shows how the time taken for trypsin to clear a milk suspension changes with temperature (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60\nTime to clear (s) | 300 | 150 | 80 | 110 | did not clear in 600 s\n\nDescribe and explain the results between 20 °C and 40 °C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `time decreases / rate increases (as temperature increases from 20 °C to 40 °C) ✓\nuse of data, e.g. time halves from 300 s to 150 s between 20 °C and 30 °C ✓\nmolecules have more (kinetic) energy / move faster so there are more collisions between enzyme and substrate / more enzyme–substrate complexes form ✓\nIGNORE particles for molecules` },
      { q: `The table shows the results in the trypsin investigation (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60\nTime to clear (s) | 300 | 150 | 80 | 110 | did not clear in 600 s\n\nExplain the results at 50 °C and 60 °C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(above the optimum / 40 °C) the enzyme starts to denature ✓\nthe active site changes shape (so the substrate no longer fits) ✓\nat 60 °C (all) the enzyme is denatured so no / very few enzyme–substrate complexes form and the milk does not clear ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW enzyme is killed` },
      { q: `Lactose is a sugar found in milk. Some people cannot digest lactose because they do not make enough of the enzyme lactase.\n\nLactose-free milk is made by adding lactase to milk. The lactase breaks lactose down into glucose and galactose.\n\nA company finds that the lactase works faster at 37 °C than at 5 °C but stops working if the milk is heated to 70 °C.\n\nExplain these observations.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `at 37 °C molecules have more energy / move faster than at 5 °C ✓\n(so) more collisions / more enzyme–substrate complexes form per second ✓\nat 70 °C the lactase is denatured / its active site changes shape (so lactose no longer fits) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW enzyme killed` },
      { q: `Scientists studied an enzyme found in bacteria that live in hot springs at 80 °C. They compared it with the same enzyme from human cells (data written for this practice question).\n\nEnzyme source | Optimum temperature (°C) | Optimum pH\nHot spring bacteria | 75 | 7.0\nHuman cells | 37 | 7.0\n\nSuggest why the enzyme from the bacteria has a different optimum temperature from the human enzyme.\n\nUse ideas about the structure of enzymes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the bacterial enzyme has a different sequence of amino acids ✓\n(so) it has a different (more stable) shape / bonds that hold its shape are not broken at high temperature ✓\n(so) the active site does not change shape / the enzyme does not denature until higher temperatures ✓\nALLOW the bacterial gene has a different base sequence for first mark` },
      { q: `This question is about protein synthesis.\n\nA gene contains the code for making a protein.\n\nThe sentences describe how a protein is made. They are in the wrong order.\n\nA  Amino acids are joined together in a chain.\nB  A copy of the gene is made in the nucleus.\nC  The copy of the gene attaches to a ribosome.\nD  The protein chain folds into its shape.\nE  The copy of the gene moves out of the nucleus.\n\nWrite the letters in the correct order.\n\n______ → ______ → ______ → ______ → ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `B → E → C → A → D\nall five correct ✓✓✓\n3 in the correct position ✓✓\n2 in the correct position ✓` },
      { q: `Amylase is produced in the salivary glands and in the pancreas. Amylase from saliva works in the mouth.\n\nWhen food is swallowed it enters the stomach, where the pH is about 2. Amylase stops working in the stomach.\n\nExplain why amylase stops working in the stomach.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the pH (2) is far from the optimum pH (of amylase, about 7) / stomach is too acidic ✓\n(acid) changes the shape of the active site / amylase is denatured ✓\nstarch no longer fits the active site / no enzyme–substrate complexes form ✓\nIGNORE enzyme dies` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of hydrogen peroxide concentration on the rate of the reaction catalysed by catalase.\n\nThe table shows the volume of oxygen collected in 60 seconds at each concentration (data written for this practice question).\n\nConcentration of hydrogen peroxide (%) | 0.5 | 1.0 | 1.5 | 2.0 | 2.5\nVolume of oxygen in 60 s (cm³) | 6 | 12 | 17 | 20 | 21\n\nPlot a graph of the results on a grid.\n\nDraw a line of best fit.`, marks: 4, command: 'Plot', diagram: true, markScheme: `axes labelled with units: concentration of hydrogen peroxide (%) on x-axis and volume of oxygen (cm³) on y-axis ✓\nsuitable linear scales using more than half the grid ✓\nall 5 points plotted correctly (± half a small square) ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 3 or 4 points correctly plotted\nIGNORE line of best fit if curve plotted smoothly through or close to points\nDO NOT ALLOW points joined dot to dot with a ruler for full marks (max 3)\nnote: a smooth curve that rises steeply then levels off is expected` },
      { q: `Sickle cell anaemia is caused by a change in the gene for one of the proteins in haemoglobin.\n\nPart of the base sequence of the normal gene and the sickle cell gene is:\nNormal gene:      CCT GAG GAG\nSickle cell gene: CCT GTG GAG\n\nThe table shows which amino acid is coded for by some base triplets.\n\nBase triplet | Amino acid\nCCT | proline\nGAG | glutamic acid\nGTG | valine\n\n(i) How many amino acids are coded for by the part of the gene shown?\n(ii) Name the amino acid in the sickle cell protein that is different from the normal protein.\n(iii) Explain how this change could affect the haemoglobin protein.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(i) 3 ✓\n(ii) valine (replaces glutamic acid) ✓\n(iii) the sequence of amino acids in the protein changes ✓\n(so) the protein folds into a different shape / has a different structure (and does not work properly) ✓\nALLOW the haemoglobin molecules stick together / cause red blood cells to change shape for the final mark\nIGNORE references to a different gene / whole protein changing unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigates how temperature affects the activity of amylase. The student uses amylase and starch solution and a spotting tile with iodine.\n\nThe student's method is:\n1. Put 5 cm³ of starch solution into a test tube.\n2. Add 2 cm³ of amylase solution.\n3. Put the test tube in a water bath at 20 °C.\n4. Every minute, add a drop of the mixture to iodine in a spotting tile.\n5. Repeat at 30 °C, 40 °C, 50 °C and 60 °C.\n\nThe teacher says the method has errors which will make the results less valid.\n\nSuggest two improvements to the method and explain why each one would improve the results.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `improvement 1:\nput the starch and the amylase solutions in the water bath separately (for a few minutes) before mixing them ✓\nso that the reaction happens at the correct temperature from the start ✓\nimprovement 2:\ntest every 30 s / 10 s (instead of every minute) ✓\nso the end point / time is found more precisely ✓\nALTERNATIVE improvements:\nadd a buffer (solution) to keep the pH the same ✓ because pH affects enzyme activity ✓\nrepeat at each temperature and calculate a mean ✓ to identify anomalies / improve repeatability ✓\nuse a thermometer to check the water bath temperature ✓ so temperature is kept constant ✓\nmax 2 for improvements with no explanation` },
      { q: `* A student wants to investigate the effect of pH on the activity of amylase.\n\nThe student is given:\n• amylase solution\n• starch solution\n• buffer solutions of pH 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8\n• iodine solution\n• a spotting tile, test tubes and pipettes\n• a water bath set at 35 °C\n• a stop clock.\n\nDescribe how the student could carry out this investigation and use the results to find the optimum pH of amylase.\n\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how the measurements are made\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• how the results are processed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nDescribes a detailed method that would produce valid results, including how the end point is found, control of variables AND how the rate is calculated and used to find the optimum pH.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes a method with some detail that would produce some results, and either identifies some control variables OR describes how the results are processed.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives a simple description of a method OR names some control variables.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nmethod / measurements\n• put a drop of iodine into each well of the spotting tile\n• add buffer of one pH to the starch solution\n• place amylase and starch (with buffer) in the water bath before mixing\n• mix and start the stop clock\n• every 30 s / 10 s, add a drop of the mixture to a new well of iodine\n• record the time when the iodine stays orange-brown (no starch left)\n• repeat for each pH\ncontrol variables\n• temperature (35 °C water bath)\n• volume and concentration of amylase\n• volume and concentration of starch\n• volume of buffer / iodine\n• time interval between samples\nprocessing\n• repeat at each pH and calculate a mean time, ignoring anomalies\n• calculate rate = 1 / time or 1000 / time\n• plot a graph of rate against pH\n• the optimum pH is the pH with the highest rate / shortest time\n• repeat with smaller pH intervals around the optimum to find it more precisely` },
      { q: `* Genes are sections of DNA found in the nucleus. Each gene codes for a protein, such as an enzyme.\n\nThe enzyme amylase is made in cells of the salivary glands.\n\nExplain how the cells use the gene for amylase to make an amylase molecule with an active site of the correct shape.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nGives a detailed explanation of both transcription and translation AND links the base sequence to the amino acid sequence and the shape of the active site.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes both stages of protein synthesis in outline OR describes one stage in detail and links the amino acid sequence to the shape of the protein.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives a simple statement about making a copy of the gene OR about amino acids being joined at a ribosome.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\ntranscription (in the nucleus)\n• the two DNA strands separate / unzip at the gene\n• one strand is used as a template\n• a complementary copy is made as mRNA (messenger RNA)\n• mRNA leaves the nucleus through a nuclear pore\ntranslation (in the cytoplasm)\n• mRNA attaches to a ribosome\n• the ribosome reads the bases in triplets / three bases at a time\n• each triplet codes for one amino acid\n• tRNA molecules bring specific amino acids to the ribosome\n• amino acids are joined in the order set by the mRNA / gene\nshape of the active site\n• the sequence of bases in the gene determines the sequence of amino acids\n• the chain of amino acids folds into a specific 3D shape\n• this shape includes the active site, which is complementary to starch\n• a change in the base sequence could change the amino acid sequence and the shape of the active site` }
    ]
  },
  'B1.3': {
    name: 'Respiration',
    questions: [
      { q: `A baker mixes flour, water, sugar and yeast to make bread dough. The dough is left in a warm place for one hour and it doubles in size.\n\nName the gas made by the yeast that makes the dough rise.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide / CO₂ (1)\nDo not allow: oxygen / air / ethanol` },
      { q: `A sprinter runs a 200 m race. During the race some of her muscle cells respire anaerobically.\n\nName the product of anaerobic respiration in human muscle cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• lactic acid (1)\nAllow: lactate\nDo not allow: ethanol / alcohol / carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Yeast cells can respire aerobically and anaerobically.\n\nWhere in a yeast cell does anaerobic respiration take place?\n\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\ncell wall        cytoplasm        mitochondria        nucleus`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• cytoplasm (1)\nIf more than one option is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `A student puts some germinating peas into a vacuum flask with a thermometer. Over three days the temperature inside the flask rises from 20 °C to 24 °C.\n\nWhich word describes respiration?\n\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\ndiffusion        endothermic        exothermic        osmosis`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• exothermic (1)\nIf more than one option is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `A student sets up a simple respirometer. A tube contains some woodlice resting on a wire gauze. Below the gauze is some soda lime. A coloured liquid in a capillary tube is connected to the tube.\n\nState the purpose of the soda lime.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• to absorb carbon dioxide (given out by the woodlice) (1)\nAllow: removes CO₂\nIgnore: absorbs gases / absorbs oxygen\nDo not allow: absorbs oxygen as the only answer` },
      { q: `A scientist counts the yeast cells in 1 cm³ of a culture used to brew beer. There are 360 000 000 yeast cells in 1 cm³.\n\nGive this number in standard form.\n\nNumber of yeast cells = ______ per cm³`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• 3.6 × 10⁸ (1)\nDo not allow: 36 × 10⁷` },
      { q: `A marathon runner's leg muscle cells respire aerobically for most of the race.\n\nComplete the word equation for aerobic respiration.\n\nglucose + ______ → ______ + water`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• oxygen (1)\n• carbon dioxide (1)\nAllow: correct formulae O₂ and CO₂\nIgnore: energy / ATP written as a product` },
      { q: `Aerobic respiration in a plant root cell uses glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆).\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation for aerobic respiration.\n\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + ___O₂ → ___CO₂ + ___H₂O`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• 6 in front of O₂ (1)\n• 6 in front of CO₂ AND 6 in front of H₂O (1)` },
      { q: `A brewer adds yeast to a sugar solution in a sealed tank. No air can get into the tank.\n\nWrite the word equation for the respiration that takes place in the yeast in the tank.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• glucose → (1)\n• ethanol + carbon dioxide (1)\nAllow: sugar for glucose; alcohol for ethanol\nIgnore: energy / ATP\nDo not allow: oxygen as a reactant for either mark\nDo not allow: lactic acid` },
      { q: `A student uses a gas syringe to collect the carbon dioxide given off by a mixture of yeast and sugar solution at 30 °C. The student collects 37.5 cm³ of carbon dioxide in 2.5 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of carbon dioxide production. Give the unit.\n\nRate = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 37.5 ÷ 2.5 = 15 (1)\n• cm³ per minute / cm³/min (1)\nAllow: 0.25 cm³ per second for both marks` },
      { q: `A student investigates the rate of respiration of yeast at 35 °C. The student counts the number of bubbles of gas released in one minute. The student repeats the count four times. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCount 1: 26\nCount 2: 28\nCount 3: 19\nCount 4: 27\n\nCalculate the mean number of bubbles per minute. Do not include the anomalous result.\n\nMean = ______ bubbles per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• identifies 19 as anomalous / (26 + 28 + 27) ÷ 3 (1)\n• 27 (1)\nAllow: 1 mark for 25 (all four counts used)` },
      { q: `During a 100 m sprint, a runner's leg muscles contract very quickly. Some muscle cells start to respire anaerobically.\n\nExplain why the muscle cells start to respire anaerobically.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• muscles need (a lot of) energy / ATP quickly (1)\n• not enough oxygen is delivered to the muscle cells (for aerobic respiration) (1)\nAllow: oxygen cannot reach the muscles fast enough\nIgnore: no oxygen at all` },
      { q: `After heavy rain a field becomes waterlogged. The spaces between the soil particles fill with water instead of air. The roots of the crop plants start to respire anaerobically.\n\nExplain why the roots respire anaerobically and name the products made.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is little / no oxygen in the (waterlogged) soil (for aerobic respiration) (1)\n• ethanol and carbon dioxide (1)\nBoth products needed for the second mark\nDo not allow: lactic acid` },
      { q: `A student says: "Our cells only respire when we are exercising. When we are asleep, respiration stops."\n\nExplain why the student is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• respiration happens continuously / all the time in all living cells (1)\n• to supply ATP / energy for processes such as (any one) active transport / muscle contraction for breathing or heartbeat / building large molecules / keeping body temperature (1)\nIgnore: "to make energy"` },
      { q: `Bread dough contains yeast. While the dough rises, the yeast makes ethanol as well as carbon dioxide. Freshly baked bread does not contain any ethanol.\n\nSuggest why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the high temperature in the oven makes the ethanol evaporate (1)\n• the yeast is killed / its enzymes are denatured so no more ethanol is made (1)\nAllow: ethanol has a low boiling point / boils off` },
      { q: `Yoghurt is made by adding bacteria to warm milk. The bacteria respire anaerobically using the sugar in the milk. The milk becomes thick and tastes sour.\n\nExplain why the milk tastes sour.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the bacteria produce lactic acid (1)\n• which lowers the pH / makes the milk acidic (1)\nDo not allow: ethanol / alcohol for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student uses a respirometer to measure the respiration of germinating seeds. The student sets up a second, identical respirometer. In this second respirometer, the seeds are replaced with glass beads of the same volume.\n\nExplain why the student sets up the second respirometer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it is a control (1)\n• to show that any movement of the liquid is caused by the (respiring) seeds / not by changes in temperature or pressure (1)\nAllow: so that the results can be compared to show the seeds cause the change` },
      { q: `A baker measures the height of some bread dough in a tin. At the start the dough is 4.0 cm high. After 40 minutes in a warm place the dough is 6.8 cm high.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the height of the dough.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (6.8 − 4.0) ÷ 4.0 × 100 / 2.8 ÷ 4.0 × 100 (1)\n• 70 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student sets up two vacuum flasks, each with a thermometer.\nFlask A contains germinating peas.\nFlask B contains peas that have been boiled and cooled.\n\nAfter two days the temperature in flask A has risen. The temperature in flask B has not changed.\n\nExplain the difference in the results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• peas in A are respiring / the boiled peas in B are dead and do not respire (1)\n• respiration is exothermic / transfers energy to the surroundings as heat (1)\nAllow: enzymes in boiled peas are denatured for the first mark` },
      { q: `The table shows the number of molecules of ATP made from one molecule of glucose. (data written for this practice question)\n\nType of respiration | ATP made per glucose\naerobic | 32\nanaerobic (in muscle cells) | 2\n\nA group of muscle cells needs 640 molecules of ATP.\n\nCalculate how many more glucose molecules would be needed if all the ATP were made by anaerobic respiration instead of aerobic respiration.\n\nNumber of extra glucose molecules = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 640 ÷ 2 = 320 AND 640 ÷ 32 = 20 (1)\n• 300 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Grizzly bears hibernate through the winter. During hibernation they do not eat. They survive by using the large store of fat (lipid) built up in their bodies during the autumn.\n\nExplain how the fat store provides the bear with energy during hibernation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• lipids / fats are broken down into fatty acids and glycerol (1)\n• which are used in respiration to make ATP / release energy (1)\nIgnore: fat is turned into energy` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out if a sports drink contains protein.\n\nDescribe how the student could test the drink for protein. Give the result if protein is present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• add biuret reagent / add sodium hydroxide then copper sulfate solution (1)\n• colour changes from blue to purple / lilac (1)\nAllow: mauve / violet for purple\nDo not allow: second mark if heating is described as needed` },
      { q: `A student tests some crushed germinating seeds for reducing sugars using Benedict's reagent.\n\nDescribe how the test is carried out and give the result if a reducing sugar is present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• add Benedict's reagent and heat in a (hot) water bath (1)\n• colour changes from blue to green / yellow / orange / brick-red (1)\nAllow: red precipitate for the second mark` },
      { q: `A respirometer contains some maggots. Soda lime is placed in the tube to absorb carbon dioxide. The tube is sealed and connected to a capillary tube containing a drop of coloured liquid.\n\nThe drop of coloured liquid moves towards the maggots.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the maggots use / take in oxygen (for aerobic respiration) and the carbon dioxide they release is absorbed (1)\n• so the volume of gas / pressure in the tube decreases, pulling the liquid towards the maggots (1)` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of carbon dioxide production by yeast at different temperatures. The rate at 35 °C is much higher than the rate at 55 °C.\n\nExplain why the rate is lower at 55 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (respiratory) enzymes in the yeast are denatured (1)\n• the shape of the active site changes so substrate no longer fits / fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form (1)\nDo not allow: enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `In Brazil, sugar cane juice is fermented by yeast in large tanks. The ethanol produced is used as a fuel for cars.\n\nSuggest why the tanks are kept sealed so that air cannot get in.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• to keep oxygen out so the yeast respires anaerobically (1)\n• so that ethanol is produced (yeast respiring aerobically would make carbon dioxide and water instead) (1)\nAllow: to stop other microorganisms contaminating the tank for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Respiration can be aerobic or anaerobic.\n\nPut ticks (✓) in the table to show which type of respiration in a human muscle cell has each feature.\n\nFeature | Aerobic | Anaerobic\nUses oxygen | ☐ | ☐\nProduces carbon dioxide | ☐ | ☐\nTakes place in the cytoplasm only | ☐ | ☐`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• uses oxygen: aerobic only (1)\n• produces carbon dioxide: aerobic only (1)\n• takes place in the cytoplasm only: anaerobic only (1)\nA row with both boxes ticked scores 0 for that row.` },
      { q: `A student uses a respirometer to measure the rate of oxygen uptake by germinating seeds at 25 °C. The capillary tube has a radius of 0.5 mm. In 10 minutes the drop of coloured liquid moves 24 mm along the tube.\n\nThe volume of liquid moved is given by: volume = πr²l, where l is the distance moved.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen uptake in mm³ per minute. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.\n\nRate = ______ mm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• volume = π × 0.5² × 24 (1)\n• = 18.8 / 18.85 mm³ (1)\n• rate = 18.85 ÷ 10 = 1.9 (mm³ per minute) (1)\nAllow: π = 3.14 giving 18.84 mm³\nECF from incorrect volume for the third mark\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student counts the bubbles of gas given off by yeast in sugar solution at two temperatures. Each count lasts 3 minutes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nTemperature (°C) | Number of bubbles in 3 minutes\n20 | 36\n40 | 123\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the rate of bubbling per minute from 20 °C to 40 °C. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• rates: 36 ÷ 3 = 12 AND 123 ÷ 3 = 41 (bubbles per minute) (1)\n• (41 − 12) ÷ 12 × 100 (1)\n• 242 (%) (1)\nAllow: (123 − 36) ÷ 36 × 100 for the first two marks\nAllow: 241.7 for 2 marks only (not 3 s.f.)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `Yeast is used both in bread making and in making wine. Yeast can respire aerobically or anaerobically depending on the conditions.\n\nCompare aerobic respiration and anaerobic respiration in yeast.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• aerobic needs oxygen; anaerobic does not (1)\n• both produce carbon dioxide (1)\n• aerobic produces water; anaerobic produces ethanol (1)\n• aerobic releases more energy / makes more ATP per glucose (than anaerobic) (1)\n• aerobic takes place (mainly) in mitochondria; anaerobic in the cytoplasm (1)\n• both use glucose / both are exothermic (1)\nA comparative statement is needed for each mark; a list of features of only one type scores max 1` },
      { q: `A pea seed contains stores of starch and protein. When the seed germinates it grows a root and a shoot before it has any green leaves.\n\nExplain how the seed uses its stores of starch and protein to grow.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• starch is broken down into glucose / sugars (1)\n• glucose is used in respiration to release energy / make ATP (for growth) (1)\n• protein is broken down into amino acids (1)\n• amino acids are used to build / synthesise new proteins (e.g. enzymes) for new cells (1)\nMax 3\nAllow: glucose used to make cellulose for new cell walls (1)` },
      { q: `A student plans an investigation into the effect of sugar concentration on the rate of respiration in yeast. The student will measure the volume of carbon dioxide given off in 10 minutes using a gas syringe.\n\nGive three variables that the student must control so that the results are valid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• temperature (of the yeast mixture / water bath) (1)\n• mass / volume / concentration of yeast (1)\n• volume of sugar solution (1)\n• type of sugar (1)\n• pH (1)\n• time yeast is left to settle before measuring (1)\nIgnore: amount without further detail\nDo not allow: sugar concentration (this is the independent variable)` },
      { q: `A student writes the balanced symbol equation for aerobic respiration:\n\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O\n\nIn one minute a group of cells respires 5 molecules of glucose aerobically.\n\nComplete the table to show the number of molecules used and produced.\n\nMolecule | Number\noxygen used | ______\ncarbon dioxide produced | ______\nwater produced | ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• oxygen used = 30 (1)\n• carbon dioxide produced = 30 (1)\n• water produced = 30 (1)` },
      { q: `A student puts germinating mung beans into a vacuum flask and records the temperature each day. (data written for this practice question)\n\nDay | Temperature (°C)\n0 | 20.0\n4 | 23.6\n\nCalculate the mean temperature rise per day and explain why the temperature rises.\n\nMean temperature rise = ______ °C per day`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (23.6 − 20.0) ÷ 4 = 0.9 (°C per day) (1)\n• the beans respire (1)\n• respiration is exothermic / releases energy as heat which is kept in by the vacuum flask (1)` },
      { q: `A sprinter runs 400 m as fast as possible. A marathon runner runs at a steady pace for over two hours. At the end of the race, the sprinter's leg muscles contain much more lactic acid than the marathon runner's.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the sprinter's muscles need energy faster than oxygen can be supplied (1)\n• so the sprinter's muscle cells respire anaerobically (1)\n• anaerobic respiration produces lactic acid; the marathon runner respires mainly aerobically, which does not (1)\nAllow: marathon runner's muscles receive enough oxygen` },
      { q: `A student measures the total volume of carbon dioxide collected from yeast in sugar solution every 2 minutes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nTime (minutes) | Total volume of carbon dioxide (cm³)\n0 | 0.0\n2 | 3.0\n4 | 6.5\n6 | 10.0\n8 | 13.0\n10 | 14.5\n\nPlot a graph of the results on graph paper. Draw a line of best fit.`, marks: 4, command: 'Plot', diagram: true, markScheme: `• time on the x-axis and volume of carbon dioxide on the y-axis, both labelled with units (1)\n• linear scales that use more than half of the grid (1)\n• all points plotted correctly to within ± half a small square (1)\n• smooth curve / line of best fit through or close to the points, levelling off after 8 minutes (1)\nAllow: 1 mark for plotting if 5 points correct\nDo not allow: dot-to-dot ruled lines for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `A student sets up four boiling tubes containing hydrogencarbonate indicator. Each tube is sealed with a bung. The indicator is red at the start. It turns yellow when the carbon dioxide concentration increases.\n\nTube | Contents (on a gauze above the indicator) | Colour after 1 hour\nA | germinating seeds | yellow\nB | boiled seeds | red\nC | nothing | red\nD | woodlice | yellow\n\nAll tubes are kept in the dark.\n\nExplain the results.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• A and D: germinating seeds / woodlice are respiring (1)\n• respiration releases carbon dioxide which turns the indicator yellow (1)\n• B: boiled seeds are dead / enzymes denatured so they do not respire / no carbon dioxide produced (1)\n• C is a control to show the indicator does not change on its own / change is caused by living organisms (1)\nAllow: tubes kept in the dark so seeds do not photosynthesise (and take up CO₂) for 1 mark\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A respirometer is used to measure the rate of oxygen uptake by some woodlice. The woodlice are in a sealed tube with soda lime. The tube is connected to a capillary tube containing coloured liquid. The tube is kept in a water bath at 20 °C.\n\nA student says: "The distance the liquid moves only shows how much oxygen the woodlice use, not how much carbon dioxide they release."\n\nExplain why the student is correct and why the water bath is needed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• soda lime absorbs the carbon dioxide released by the woodlice (1)\n• so the change in gas volume / pressure is due only to oxygen taken in (1)\n• the water bath keeps the temperature constant (1)\n• because temperature changes would change the gas volume / pressure in the tube OR would change the rate of respiration (of the woodlice) (1)` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration in yeast.\n\nThe student has this apparatus: a conical flask fitted with a bung and delivery tube, a gas syringe, a mixture of yeast and sugar solution, water baths set at different temperatures, a thermometer and a stop clock.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration in yeast.\n\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to change the temperature\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• the measurements that should be taken.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nDescribes a clear method using a range of at least five temperatures AND names at least two control variables AND describes how to measure the volume of gas and time, with repeats and how to calculate the rate.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes a method with at least three temperatures AND gives at least one control variable OR a correct measurement of gas volume in a fixed time.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives a simple description of changing the temperature OR measuring the gas given off.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nChanging temperature\n• place the flask in a water bath at e.g. 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 °C\n• check the temperature of the mixture with the thermometer\n• leave the mixture to reach the temperature of the water bath before starting\nControl variables\n• same mass / volume / concentration of yeast\n• same volume and concentration of sugar solution\n• same type of sugar\n• same pH\nMeasurements\n• connect the delivery tube to the gas syringe and seal with the bung\n• start the stop clock and record the volume of carbon dioxide in the syringe after a fixed time (e.g. 5 minutes)\n• rate = volume ÷ time\n• repeat at each temperature and calculate a mean\n• ignore anomalous results` },
      { q: `Before winter, a brown bear eats large amounts of food containing carbohydrates, proteins and lipids. During winter the bear hibernates for several months and does not eat or drink. Its body temperature stays only a few degrees below normal.\n\nExplain how the bear uses the carbohydrates, proteins and lipids in its food before hibernation, and how it survives during hibernation.\n\nUse ideas about the breakdown and synthesis of large molecules and about respiration.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nExplains the breakdown of carbohydrates, proteins AND lipids into the correct subunits AND explains how subunits are used to synthesise stored molecules AND explains how stored lipid is used in respiration during hibernation to supply ATP and keep the bear warm.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nExplains the breakdown of at least two types of large molecule into the correct subunits AND links stored fat or glycogen to respiration during hibernation.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nDescribes the breakdown of at least one type of large molecule OR states that stored fat is used for respiration / energy.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nBreakdown (digestion)\n• carbohydrates (e.g. starch) broken down into sugars / glucose by carbohydrase / amylase\n• proteins broken down into amino acids by protease\n• lipids broken down into fatty acids and glycerol by lipase\n• small molecules can be absorbed into the blood\nSynthesis\n• glucose joined to make glycogen (stored in liver / muscle)\n• fatty acids and glycerol joined to make lipids / fat stored under the skin\n• extra glucose can be converted into fat\n• amino acids joined to make new proteins, e.g. enzymes, muscle\nDuring hibernation\n• stored fat broken down into fatty acids and glycerol\n• these are used in (aerobic) respiration in cells\n• respiration supplies ATP for processes such as heartbeat, breathing, active transport\n• respiration is exothermic so the energy released as heat keeps the body temperature up\n• respiration continues all the time, even when the bear is inactive, but at a lower rate\n• lipids are a large energy store, so the bear can survive months without food` }
    ]
  },
  'B1.4': {
    name: 'Photosynthesis',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student puts a piece of pondweed upside down in a test tube of water and places a lamp next to it. Bubbles of gas are released from the cut end of the stem.\n\nName the gas that is mostly in the bubbles.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `oxygen ✓\nALLOW O₂\nDO NOT ALLOW O OR carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Palisade mesophyll cells near the top of a leaf carry out most of the photosynthesis in the plant.\n\nName the sub-cellular structure in a palisade cell where photosynthesis takes place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `chloroplast ✓\nIGNORE chlorophyll\nDO NOT ALLOW chromosome` },
      { q: `Plants make their own food by photosynthesis.\n\nComplete the word equation for photosynthesis.\ncarbon dioxide + water → glucose + ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `oxygen ✓\nIGNORE light / energy written above the arrow\nDO NOT ALLOW O` },
      { q: `A student counts the bubbles given off by a piece of pondweed. She counts 36 bubbles in 3 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of photosynthesis in bubbles per minute.\nRate = ______ bubbles per minute`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `12 ✓\n(36 ÷ 3 = 12)` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis. Light intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the lamp.\n\nThe student moves the lamp from 15 cm to 30 cm away from the pondweed.\n\nWhat fraction of the original light intensity reaches the pondweed now?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n1/2 / 1/4 / 1/8 / 1/16`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `1/4 ✓\n(distance doubled, so intensity = 1/2² of the original)` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate photosynthesis using pondweed in a beaker of water. Her teacher tells her to dissolve some sodium hydrogen carbonate in the water before she starts.\n\nWhy does the teacher tell her to add sodium hydrogen carbonate?`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `to supply / release carbon dioxide (for photosynthesis) ✓\nALLOW so that carbon dioxide is not a limiting factor\nIGNORE to make the water alkaline / oxygen` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis is described as an endothermic reaction.\n\nExplain what is meant by an endothermic reaction, using photosynthesis as an example.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `energy is taken in / absorbed (from the surroundings) ✓\nin photosynthesis, light energy is absorbed (by chlorophyll / chloroplasts) to make glucose ✓\nALLOW energy transferred from the environment to the chloroplasts\nDO NOT ALLOW 'energy is made' OR 'heat is given out'` },
      { q: `A teacher writes part of the balanced symbol equation for photosynthesis on the board.\n\n6CO₂ + ______ → C₆H₁₂O₆ + ______\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `6H₂O ✓\n6O₂ ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for H₂O and O₂ with incorrect / missing balancing numbers\nDO NOT ALLOW O₆ OR 6O` },
      { q: `Leaves have two layers of mesophyll cells. The cells in the upper (palisade) layer contain many more chloroplasts than the cells in the lower (spongy) layer.\n\nExplain why it is an advantage for the upper layer of cells to contain many chloroplasts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the upper layer receives the most light ✓\nchloroplasts contain chlorophyll which absorbs light (energy) for photosynthesis ✓\nALLOW more chloroplasts so more photosynthesis / more glucose made\nIGNORE 'to make food' unqualified` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis takes place in two stages inside the chloroplast. The first stage needs light.\n\nDescribe what happens in the first stage of photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `light energy (absorbed by chlorophyll) is used to split water ✓\nreleasing oxygen gas and hydrogen ions ✓\nALLOW hydrogen for hydrogen ions\nDO NOT ALLOW carbon dioxide is split\nDO NOT ALLOW oxygen is made from carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis takes place in two stages inside the chloroplast. In the first stage water is split using light energy.\n\nDescribe what happens in the second stage of photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `carbon dioxide (gas) is combined with hydrogen (ions) ✓\nto make glucose ✓\nALLOW hydrogen from the first stage / from water\nIGNORE references to light being needed or not needed` },
      { q: `A student counts the number of bubbles released by pondweed in one minute. She repeats the count four times with the lamp at the same distance. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCount 1: 24   Count 2: 26   Count 3: 15   Count 4: 25\n\nCalculate the mean number of bubbles per minute. Do not include the anomalous result.\nMean = ______ bubbles per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(24 + 26 + 25) ÷ 3 ✓\n= 25 ✓\nALLOW 22.5 (mean including 15) for 1 mark only\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `A light meter shows a light intensity of 1000 lux when it is 20 cm from a lamp. Light intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the lamp.\n\nCalculate the light intensity when the light meter is 40 cm from the lamp.\nLight intensity = ______ lux`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1000 × (20 ÷ 40)² OR 1000 ÷ 2² OR 1000 ÷ 4 ✓\n= 250 (lux) ✓\nALLOW 500 for 1 mark only if the student has halved the intensity` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed by counting the bubbles of gas released in one minute.\n\nGive one reason why counting bubbles may not give an accurate measure of the rate of photosynthesis, and describe a better way to measure the gas given off.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `bubbles may be different sizes / bubbles are released too quickly to count / some gas dissolves in the water ✓\ncollect the gas in a measuring cylinder / gas syringe / capillary tube and measure the volume (in a set time) ✓\nALLOW bubbles may be missed\nIGNORE 'human error' unqualified` },
      { q: `A student uses a filament lamp placed close to a beaker of pondweed. The lamp gets hot.\n\nExplain why this could make the results of the investigation invalid, and suggest how the student could prevent it.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the lamp changes the temperature of the water, so temperature is not controlled / temperature also affects the rate (of photosynthesis) ✓\nuse an LED (bulb) / place a heat shield (or a beaker of water) between the lamp and the pondweed / use a water bath / measure the temperature and keep it constant ✓\nALLOW enzymes work faster at higher temperature` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed at different light intensities. The temperature is 20 °C and the water contains no added sodium hydrogen carbonate.\n\nA graph of the results shows that the rate increases as light intensity increases and then levels off at high light intensity.\n\nExplain why the rate levels off.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `light intensity is no longer the limiting factor ✓\nanother factor, e.g. carbon dioxide concentration / temperature, is now limiting (the rate) ✓\nALLOW chlorophyll / number of chloroplasts is limiting\nIGNORE the plant has enough light` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different temperatures. The rate was highest at 35 °C. Above 40 °C the rate decreased rapidly.\n\nExplain why the rate decreased rapidly above 40 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `enzymes (that control photosynthesis) are denatured ✓\nthe active site changes shape so the substrate no longer fits ✓\nALLOW fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form\nDO NOT ALLOW enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `In winter, a farmer who grows lettuces in a greenhouse burns a paraffin heater inside the greenhouse. Burning paraffin releases heat and carbon dioxide.\n\nSuggest two ways the paraffin heater increases the rate of photosynthesis of the lettuces.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `raises the temperature, so enzymes work faster / temperature is less limiting ✓\nincreases the carbon dioxide concentration, so carbon dioxide is less limiting ✓\nIGNORE 'more light'` },
      { q: `A student collects the oxygen given off by a piece of pondweed in a measuring cylinder. She collects 2.4 cm³ of oxygen in 8 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production in cm³ per hour.\nRate = ______ cm³ per hour`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.4 ÷ 8 = 0.3 (cm³ per minute) OR 60 ÷ 8 = 7.5 ✓\n0.3 × 60 = 18 (cm³ per hour) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 0.3 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `In a pond, the only organisms that photosynthesise are algae and water plants. All the animals in the pond depend on them.\n\nExplain why all the animals in the pond depend on photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `algae / plants are producers / make glucose / biomass / food ✓\nanimals get their food / energy by eating producers or by eating animals that eat producers ✓\nALLOW photosynthesis releases oxygen which animals need for (aerobic) respiration\nIGNORE 'food chain' unqualified` },
      { q: `A student cuts small discs from a spinach leaf. She removes the air from the discs so that they sink in a syringe of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution. She then shines a lamp on the syringe. After a few minutes the discs float to the top.\n\nExplain why the leaf discs float to the top.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the discs photosynthesise and produce oxygen ✓\noxygen fills the air spaces in the leaf (so the discs become less dense) ✓\nALLOW gas builds up inside the disc\nDO NOT ALLOW carbon dioxide is produced` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of light on the rate of photosynthesis using leaf discs in a syringe of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution. The student calculates a rate for each light intensity using the formula:\n\nrate = 1000 ÷ time taken for the discs to float (s)\n\nAt one light intensity, the discs took 320 s to float.\n\nCalculate the rate. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.\nRate = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1000 ÷ 320 = 3.125 ✓\n= 3.1 ✓\nALLOW 3.125 / 3.13 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A variegated leaf has green parts and white parts. The white parts have no chlorophyll. A student leaves a variegated plant in sunlight for 24 hours. She then removes the leaf, boils it in ethanol and adds iodine solution.\n\nThe green parts turn blue-black but the white parts stay orange-brown.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `green parts contain starch / white parts contain no starch ✓\n(only) the green parts photosynthesise because chlorophyll is needed to absorb light ✓\nALLOW glucose (from photosynthesis) is stored as starch in the green parts\nDO NOT ALLOW the white parts photosynthesise slower` },
      { q: `A scientist measures the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at two carbon dioxide concentrations, 0.04% and 0.10%. At very low light intensity the rate is the same at both carbon dioxide concentrations.\n\nExplain why increasing the carbon dioxide concentration has no effect on the rate at very low light intensity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `light intensity is the limiting factor ✓\nso not enough light energy is available to split water / not enough hydrogen is made to combine with the extra carbon dioxide ✓\nALLOW the rate is limited by the first stage (of photosynthesis)\nIGNORE carbon dioxide is not needed` },
      { q: `A student moves a lamp closer to some pondweed. The rate of photosynthesis increases from 12 bubbles per minute to 30 bubbles per minute.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the rate of photosynthesis.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(30 − 12) ÷ 12 × 100 OR 18 ÷ 12 × 100 ✓\n= 150 (%) ✓\nALLOW 250 for 1 mark (30 ÷ 12 × 100)` },
      { q: `A farmer grows tomatoes in a greenhouse. He raises the carbon dioxide concentration in the greenhouse from 0.04% to 0.10%. This increases his tomato yield.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the farmer does not raise the carbon dioxide concentration any higher than 0.10%.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nanother factor such as light intensity / temperature becomes limiting (so rate does not increase further) ✓\nthe cost of extra carbon dioxide is more than the value of the extra tomatoes / not cost effective ✓\nhigh carbon dioxide concentration may be harmful to workers ✓\nIGNORE carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas` },
      { q: `A student moves a lamp from 40 cm to 20 cm away from some pondweed. The number of bubbles released per minute increases.\n\nExplain why the rate of photosynthesis increases. Use ideas about light intensity and the stages of photosynthesis in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `light intensity is inversely proportional to distance squared, so light intensity is four times greater ✓\nmore light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll (in chloroplasts) ✓\nso more water is split / more hydrogen ions produced (in the first stage), so more glucose can be made / more oxygen released ✓\nALLOW light intensity increases for the first marking point if 'four times' not stated\nALLOW light was the limiting factor` },
      { q: `A student measures the oxygen produced by pondweed by collecting it in a capillary tube. The oxygen forms a column 18 mm long in 5 minutes. The radius of the capillary tube is 0.5 mm. (data written for this practice question)\n\nThe volume of a cylinder = πr²l\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production in mm³ per minute. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.\nRate = ______ mm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `volume = π × 0.5² × 18 = 14.1 (mm³) ✓\n14.1 ÷ 5 = 2.83 ✓\n= 2.8 (mm³ per minute) to 2 sig figs ✓\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect volume\nALLOW 3.14 for π\nUse of 1 mm as the radius (π × 1² × 18 = 56.5; 56.5 ÷ 5 = 11) = max 2 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the rate of photosynthesis of a pondweed at different temperatures, keeping light intensity and carbon dioxide concentration high. (data written for this practice question)\n\nTemperature (°C): 10, 20, 30, 40, 50\nRate (arbitrary units): 8, 18, 34, 22, 2\n\nDescribe and explain the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `rate increases from 10 °C to 30 °C AND decreases above 30 °C ✓\n(up to 30 °C) more kinetic energy so more collisions between enzymes and substrates / more enzyme–substrate complexes ✓\n(above 30 °C) enzymes denature / active site changes shape ✓\nALLOW optimum at (about) 30 °C for the first marking point\nDO NOT ALLOW enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `A scientist measures the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at a range of light intensities. She does this at two carbon dioxide concentrations, 0.04% and 0.10%. Temperature is kept at 25 °C.\n\nSketch a graph to show the results you would expect. Label the axes and label each line with its carbon dioxide concentration.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `x-axis labelled light intensity AND y-axis labelled rate of photosynthesis ✓\nboth lines rise from (near) the origin and then level off / plateau ✓\n0.10% line plateaus higher than the 0.04% line AND lines are the same / overlap at low light intensity ✓\nIGNORE units\nDO NOT ALLOW lines that fall after levelling off` },
      { q: `A student wrote this method to investigate the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis:\n• Put some pondweed in a beaker of tap water.\n• Hold a lamp close to the beaker and count the bubbles for 30 seconds.\n• Move the lamp further away and count the bubbles again.\n• Do this for four distances.\n\nSuggest three improvements to the student's method.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any three from:\nadd sodium hydrogen carbonate (to the water) so that carbon dioxide is not limiting ✓\nuse a ruler to measure / set the distances (e.g. 10, 20, 30, 40 cm) ✓\nleave the pondweed for a few minutes at each distance before counting (to acclimatise) ✓\ncount for a longer time, e.g. 1 / 5 minutes ✓\nrepeat each distance (at least) three times and calculate a mean ✓\ncontrol temperature, e.g. use a water bath / heat shield / LED ✓\ncarry out in a darkened room / reduce background light ✓\ncollect the gas and measure its volume instead of counting bubbles ✓\nuse the same piece / length of pondweed ✓` },
      { q: `A student sets up four sealed tubes containing hydrogencarbonate indicator. The indicator is red at normal air carbon dioxide levels, yellow when carbon dioxide increases and purple when carbon dioxide decreases. (data written for this practice question)\n\nTube A: leaf, in bright light → indicator turns purple\nTube B: leaf, wrapped in foil (dark) → indicator turns yellow\nTube C: no leaf, in bright light → indicator stays red\nTube D: leaf, covered in thin gauze (dim light) → indicator stays red\n\nExplain the results in tubes A, B and D.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `A: rate of photosynthesis is greater than rate of respiration, so carbon dioxide is taken up / decreases ✓\nB: no photosynthesis (only respiration), so carbon dioxide is released / increases ✓\nD: rate of photosynthesis equals rate of respiration, so carbon dioxide level does not change ✓\nALLOW A: photosynthesis uses up carbon dioxide\nIGNORE references to tube C (it is the control)` },
      { q: `A student places pondweed 20 cm from a lamp. She then moves the lamp to 8 cm from the pondweed. Light intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the lamp.\n\nCalculate how many times greater the light intensity is at 8 cm than at 20 cm.\nNumber of times greater = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `uses 1/d²: 1 ÷ 8² AND 1 ÷ 20² OR (20 ÷ 8) ✓\n(20 ÷ 8)² OR 0.015625 ÷ 0.0025 ✓\n= 6.25 ✓\nALLOW 6.3\nALLOW 2.5 for 1 mark (ratio not squared)` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed at six light intensities. (data written for this practice question)\n\nLight intensity (arbitrary units): 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\nRate (bubbles per minute): 10, 20, 30, 36, 38, 38\n\nThe student concludes: "The rate of photosynthesis is directly proportional to light intensity."\n\nEvaluate the student's conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `conclusion is correct for light intensities 1 to 3 ✓\ne.g. doubling light intensity from 1 to 2 doubles the rate from 10 to 20 / rate increases by 10 for every unit ✓\nabove 3 (units) the rate is not proportional / rate increases less and levels off at 38, because another factor (e.g. carbon dioxide / temperature) is limiting ✓\nALLOW conclusion only partly correct for first marking point if supported` },
      { q: `A grower uses a carbon dioxide generator in a 500 m² tomato greenhouse. Without extra carbon dioxide the yield is 18 kg per m². With extra carbon dioxide the yield is 22.5 kg per m². Tomatoes sell for £1.60 per kg. Running the carbon dioxide generator for the season costs £2100. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the extra profit the grower makes from using the carbon dioxide generator.\nExtra profit = £ ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `extra yield = 22.5 − 18 = 4.5 kg per m² AND 4.5 × 500 = 2250 kg ✓\n2250 × 1.60 = £3600 (extra income) ✓\n3600 − 2100 = £1500 ✓\nALLOW ECF at each step\nCorrect answer with no working = 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of distance from a lamp on the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed. She counts the bubbles released in one minute, three times at each distance. (data written for this practice question)\n\n10 cm: 52, 49, 55\n20 cm: 30, 18, 33\n30 cm: 14, 16, 15\n40 cm: 9, 7, 8\n\nIdentify the anomalous result, calculate the mean rate at 20 cm without it, and suggest one reason why the anomalous result happened.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `anomalous result = 18 (at 20 cm) ✓\n(30 + 33) ÷ 2 ✓\n= 31.5 (bubbles per minute) ✓\nsuggestion, e.g. counted too soon after moving the lamp / pondweed had not acclimatised / some bubbles missed / bubbles merged / lamp moved during count ✓\nALLOW 27 for the mean including 18 for 1 mark of the two calculation marks\nIGNORE 'human error' unqualified` },
      { q: `A scientist measures the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different light intensities under three conditions.\n• Line X (0.10% carbon dioxide, 25 °C): rises and levels off at 60 units.\n• Line Y (0.04% carbon dioxide, 25 °C): rises and levels off at 35 units.\n• Line Z (0.04% carbon dioxide, 15 °C): rises and levels off at 20 units.\nAt low light intensities all three lines are the same.\n\nExplain the results. Use ideas about limiting factors.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `at low light intensity, light is the limiting factor (so all lines are the same) ✓\non line Y, carbon dioxide (concentration) is limiting at high light intensity because raising carbon dioxide to 0.10% (line X) increases the rate ✓\non line Z, temperature is limiting at high light intensity because raising temperature to 25 °C (line Y) increases the rate ✓\nthe factor in shortest supply limits the rate / raising a factor that is not limiting has no effect ✓\nALLOW for the third marking point: at 15 °C enzymes have less kinetic energy / fewer collisions` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed with the lamp at different distances. (data written for this practice question)\n\nDistance from lamp (cm): 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 40\nMean rate (bubbles per minute): 48, 30, 19, 12, 8, 5\n\nPlot a graph of the results on a grid and draw a line of best fit.`, marks: 4, command: 'Plot', diagram: true, markScheme: `distance on the x-axis AND rate on the y-axis, both labelled with units ✓\nsensible linear scales using more than half of the grid ✓\nall six points plotted correctly (± half a small square) ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 4 or 5 points correct\nsmooth curve of best fit, falling steeply then levelling off ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW points joined dot to dot / a straight line of best fit` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis using pondweed. She has a water bath, a thermometer, a lamp, sodium hydrogen carbonate solution, a boiling tube, a stopwatch and a ruler.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to change the temperature\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• the measurements that should be taken.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that describes how to change temperature, identifies several control variables with how they are controlled, and describes the measurements to be taken, including repeats. The method could be followed to give valid results.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method that describes how to change temperature and either the control variables or the measurements in some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple description of changing the temperature OR a measurement OR a control variable.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nChanging temperature\n• place the boiling tube of pondweed in a water bath\n• use at least five temperatures, e.g. 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 °C\n• check the temperature with a thermometer; allow time for the pondweed to acclimatise\nControl variables\n• light intensity: keep the lamp at the same distance, measured with the ruler; use the same lamp / LED\n• carbon dioxide: same concentration / volume of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution\n• same piece / length / species of pondweed\n• carry out in a darkened room\nMeasurements\n• count bubbles (or collect gas volume) for a set time, e.g. 1 minute, using the stopwatch\n• repeat at least three times at each temperature and calculate a mean\n• calculate rate = number of bubbles ÷ time; plot rate against temperature` },
      { q: `A grower wants to increase the yield of tomatoes in her greenhouse. She measures the rate of photosynthesis of her tomato plants under four sets of conditions. Light intensity is high in all four. (data written for this practice question)\n\nA: 15 °C, 0.04% carbon dioxide, rate 20 units\nB: 15 °C, 0.10% carbon dioxide, rate 26 units\nC: 25 °C, 0.04% carbon dioxide, rate 30 units\nD: 25 °C, 0.10% carbon dioxide, rate 52 units\n\nHeating the greenhouse to 25 °C costs more than adding carbon dioxide.\n\nExplain the results and advise the grower on the best conditions to use.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains the results using limiting factors, including the interaction between temperature and carbon dioxide, using data AND gives reasoned advice that considers cost against the increase in rate / yield.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains some results using limiting factors with some use of data AND gives some advice.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple description of the results OR simple advice without justification.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nExplanation\n• increasing carbon dioxide at 15 °C (A to B) increases the rate only by 6 units, because temperature is limiting\n• increasing temperature at 0.04% (A to C) increases the rate by 10 units, because carbon dioxide then becomes limiting\n• increasing both (A to D) increases the rate by 32 units / rate is more than doubled (2.6 times)\n• the factors interact: raising one factor only has a big effect if the other is not limiting\n• higher temperature means more kinetic energy / more enzyme–substrate collisions\n• more carbon dioxide combines with hydrogen to make more glucose\nAdvice\n• condition D gives the highest rate, so the most glucose / growth / yield\n• adding carbon dioxide alone is cheap but gives only a small increase\n• heating is expensive; the grower should compare the cost of heating and carbon dioxide with the value of the extra tomatoes\n• check that light does not become limiting at night / in winter; temperature should not be raised so high that enzymes denature` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.1': {
    name: 'Supplying the Cell',
    questions: [
      { q: `A root hair cell takes in water from the soil.\n\nWhich process moves water into the root hair cell?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nactive transport / diffusion of glucose / osmosis / respiration`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `osmosis (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `A body cell from a dog contains 78 chromosomes. The cell divides by mitosis.\n\nHow many chromosomes are in each of the new cells?\nNumber of chromosomes = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `78 (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 39` },
      { q: `A student weighs potato cylinders before and after leaving them in sugar solutions for 30 minutes. Before weighing them at the end, the student gently rolls each cylinder on a paper towel.\n\nGive one reason why the student rolls each cylinder on a paper towel.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to remove (excess) solution / water from the surface (so that only water taken in or lost by the cells is measured) (1)\nIGNORE to dry it unqualified\nALLOW so the mass is not increased by liquid on the outside` },
      { q: `A gardener notices that a plant grows new cells at the tips of its roots and shoots. Cells in these regions divide many times and are not yet specialised.\n\nName the type of tissue found at the tips of roots and shoots where these cells are produced.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `meristem / meristematic tissue (1)\nALLOW plant stem cells\nIGNORE xylem / phloem` },
      { q: `A leukaemia patient is given a bone marrow transplant. The bone marrow from a donor contains stem cells that can make new blood cells.\n\nWhat type of stem cell is found in bone marrow?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `adult (stem cell) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW embryonic` },
      { q: `A scientist grows human skin cells in the laboratory for a skin graft (data written for this practice question). Starting from one cell, the number of cells doubles with each cell cycle. After 20 cell cycles there are 1 048 576 cells.\n\nGive the number of cells after 20 cell cycles in standard form to 3 significant figures.\nNumber of cells = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1.05 × 10⁶ (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 10.5 × 10⁵` },
      { q: `A student investigates osmosis in potato. A potato cylinder has a mass of 2.50 g at the start. After 30 minutes in distilled water its mass is 2.85 g (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in mass of the potato cylinder.\nPercentage change in mass = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `change in mass = 2.85 − 2.50 = 0.35 (g) (1)\n(0.35 ÷ 2.50) × 100 = (+)14 (%) (1)\nALLOW 14 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW ECF for an incorrect change in mass` },
      { q: `A student places a potato cylinder into a concentrated sugar solution (1.0 mol/dm³). After 30 minutes the cylinder has lost mass and feels soft and floppy.\n\nExplain why the potato cylinder lost mass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water concentration is higher inside the potato cells than in the solution / solution is more concentrated than the cell contents (1)\nso water leaves the cells by osmosis (through the partially permeable membrane) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW sugar leaves the cells\nIGNORE water potential if used correctly` },
      { q: `Two students do an osmosis investigation. Student A uses potato cylinders that each start at about 1 g. Student B uses cylinders that each start at about 5 g. They compare their results as percentage change in mass, not change in mass.\n\nExplain why they use percentage change in mass to compare their results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the starting masses of the cylinders are different (1)\npercentage change allows a fair comparison / shows the change relative to the starting mass (1)\nALLOW a larger cylinder would change more in mass even if the effect is the same` },
      { q: `A student repeats an osmosis experiment four times using potato cylinders in 0.8 mol/dm³ sugar solution. The percentage changes in mass are:\n−11.2 %, −10.8 %, −4.1 %, −11.5 %\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean percentage change in mass. Do not include the anomalous result.\nMean = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `identifies −4.1 as the anomaly / (−11.2 + −10.8 + −11.5) ÷ 3 (1)\n= −11.2 (%) (1)\nALLOW −11.17 / −11.167\nALLOW 11.2 without the minus sign for 1 mark only\nIf anomaly included: −9.4 scores 0` },
      { q: `Red blood cells are placed in a test tube of distilled water. After a few minutes the cells have burst.\n\nExplain why the red blood cells burst.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water moves into the cells by osmosis / water moves from a dilute solution to a more concentrated solution (1)\nanimal cells have no cell wall so the cell (membrane) swells and bursts (1)\nIGNORE cell membrane is weak unqualified` },
      { q: `A student puts a piece of red onion skin into a strong salt solution. After 20 minutes the student looks at the cells under a light microscope.\n\nDescribe two changes the student would see in the cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\nthe vacuole gets smaller (1)\nthe cytoplasm / cell membrane pulls away from the cell wall (1)\nthe red colour becomes darker / more concentrated (1)\nthe cell wall stays the same shape / size (1)\nALLOW cells become plasmolysed for one mark\nIGNORE cell shrinks unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of potassium ions in the cells of a root and in the soil around the root (data written for this practice question).\nConcentration of potassium ions in root hair cells: 20 mmol/dm³\nConcentration of potassium ions in soil water: 0.5 mmol/dm³\n\nExplain why the root hair cells must absorb potassium ions by active transport.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `concentration in the cells is (40 times) higher than in the soil / ions must move against the concentration gradient / from low to high concentration (1)\nso diffusion would move ions out of the cell / active transport uses energy (from respiration) to move them in (1)\nALLOW ions cannot diffuse in` },
      { q: `Cyanide is a poison that stops aerobic respiration in cells. A scientist adds a small amount of cyanide to the water around the roots of a plant.\n\nSuggest and explain the effect of cyanide on the uptake of nitrate ions by the roots.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `nitrate uptake decreases / stops (1)\nbecause active transport needs energy from respiration / less energy (ATP) is released (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is produced / made` },
      { q: `A single-celled organism called Amoeba lives in pond water. Oxygen diffuses into the cell from the water. The cell uses the oxygen in respiration.\n\nExplain why oxygen keeps diffusing into the cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `oxygen is used up in respiration so the concentration inside the cell stays low (1)\nso a concentration gradient is maintained / oxygen concentration is higher in the water than in the cell (1)\nALLOW net movement from high to low concentration` },
      { q: `A student puts a drop of purple dye into beakers of still water at different temperatures. The student times how long it takes for the colour to spread evenly through the water.\nAt 20 °C it takes 240 seconds. At 40 °C it takes 120 seconds (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain the effect of temperature on the rate of diffusion shown by these results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `higher temperature increases the rate of diffusion / rate doubles from 20 °C to 40 °C / time halves (1)\nparticles have more (kinetic) energy / move faster at higher temperature (1)\nIGNORE particles vibrate more` },
      { q: `Before a cell divides by mitosis it goes through a stage of growth called interphase.\n\nDescribe two things that happen to the cell during interphase.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\nDNA is copied / replicated / chromosomes are copied (1)\nnumber of organelles increases / mitochondria / ribosomes are copied (1)\ncell grows / increases in size / makes more proteins (1)\nIGNORE chromosomes separate / line up` },
      { q: `When a skin cell divides by mitosis, two new cells are produced. Scientists say the two new cells are genetically identical to each other and to the parent cell.\n\nExplain why the two new cells are genetically identical.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the DNA / chromosomes are copied exactly before division (1)\none copy of each chromosome moves to each new cell / each new cell gets a full identical set of chromosomes (1)\nALLOW chromatids separate to opposite ends of the cell` },
      { q: `An early human embryo develops from a single cell. Each cell divides by mitosis every 12 hours (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate how many cells there will be after 3 days, starting from one cell.\nNumber of cells = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3 days = 72 hours so 72 ÷ 12 = 6 divisions (1)\n2⁶ = 64 (cells) (1)\nALLOW 64 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 12 (6 × 2)` },
      { q: `A human embryo starts as a single cell. An adult human has more than 200 different types of specialised cell, such as nerve cells, red blood cells and muscle cells.\n\nExplain why cell differentiation is important in a multicellular organism such as a human.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `cells become specialised / develop different structures to carry out a particular function (1)\nso different tissues / organs can be formed / functions can be carried out efficiently (1)\nALLOW example e.g. red blood cells to carry oxygen` },
      { q: `Scientists can obtain stem cells from early human embryos or from the bone marrow of adults.\n\nGive two differences between embryonic stem cells and adult stem cells.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nembryonic can differentiate into any type of cell / adult can only differentiate into a limited range of cell types (1)\nobtaining embryonic stem cells destroys an embryo / adult can be obtained without harming the donor (1)\nadult stem cells can come from the patient so less risk of rejection (1)\nembryonic stem cells are easier to obtain in large numbers / divide more (1)\nALLOW pluripotent / multipotent if used correctly` },
      { q: `A gardener cuts a short piece of stem from a geranium plant and puts it into compost. After two weeks the cutting has grown new roots.\n\nExplain how the cutting is able to grow new roots.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the stem contains meristem tissue / unspecialised plant stem cells (1)\nwhich divide by mitosis and differentiate into root cells / any type of plant cell (1)\nIGNORE grows from the cut end unqualified` },
      { q: `Doctors are testing whether stem cells can be used to treat damage to the spinal cord.\n\nSuggest two risks of injecting stem cells into a patient.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nthe cells may be rejected by the immune system (1)\nthe cells may divide uncontrollably / cause tumours / cancer (1)\nthe cells may be infected with viruses / transfer infection (1)\nthe cells may differentiate into the wrong type of cell (1)\nIGNORE side effects / it may not work` },
      { q: `In a study, scientists used stem cells to treat 24 mice that had damage to the retina in one eye (data written for this practice question). After 8 weeks, the vision of 15 of the mice had improved.\n\nCalculate the percentage of mice whose vision improved.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(15 ÷ 24) × 100 (1)\n= 62.5 (%) (1)\nALLOW 63 / 62\nALLOW 62.5 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student makes a model cell. A bag made of Visking tubing (a partially permeable membrane) is filled with concentrated sugar solution. The bag is tied to a glass tube and placed in a beaker of pure water. After one hour the liquid has risen up the glass tube.\n\nExplain why the liquid rises up the glass tube.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water moves into the bag by osmosis / from the pure water (higher water concentration) to the sugar solution (lower water concentration) (1)\nsugar molecules are too large to pass through the membrane / cannot move out, so the volume in the bag increases (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW sugar moves out` },
      { q: `A student leaves a potato cylinder in distilled water. The student weighs it every hour. The mass increases for the first three hours and then stays the same, even though the cylinder is still in distilled water.\n\nExplain why the mass of the potato cylinder stops increasing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the cells have become turgid / full of water (1)\nthe (strong) cell wall resists further expansion / pressure of the cell wall stops more water entering, so there is no net movement of water (1)\nALLOW the cells cannot take in any more water because of the cell wall\nDO NOT ALLOW the water concentration is equal on both sides` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of sugar solution concentration on potato cylinders. The table shows the results (data written for this practice question).\nConcentration (mol/dm³) | Percentage change in mass (%)\n0.0 | +15.0\n0.2 | +7.9\n0.4 | +0.8\n0.6 | −8.0\n0.8 | −14.1\n1.0 | −14.9\n\nEstimate the concentration of sugar solution that is the same concentration as the potato cell contents. Explain your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `estimate in range 0.40–0.45 mol/dm³ (1)\nALLOW 0.42\nthis is where percentage change in mass is zero / where the line crosses the x-axis (1)\nso there is no net movement of water by osmosis / water concentration is the same inside and outside the cells (1)\nALLOW estimate between 0.4 and 0.6 for the first mark only if a reason is given` },
      { q: `After a meal there is a high concentration of glucose in the small intestine. Glucose is absorbed into the blood by diffusion. Later, when the concentration of glucose in the intestine is lower than in the blood, glucose is still absorbed into the cells lining the intestine by active transport.\n\nCompare diffusion and active transport.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `diffusion moves substances down a concentration gradient / from high to low, active transport moves substances against a concentration gradient / from low to high (1)\ndiffusion is passive / needs no energy, active transport needs energy from respiration (1)\nactive transport uses carrier proteins in the membrane, diffusion does not (1)\nALLOW both move substances across cell membranes for one mark if no other mark given\nComparative statements required for each mark` },
      { q: `Root hair cells absorb mineral ions from the soil by active transport. A root hair cell has a long thin extension and contains many mitochondria.\n\nExplain how these features help the root hair cell to absorb mineral ions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `long extension gives a large surface area (1)\nso more ions / more carrier proteins can be taken up at once / faster (1)\nmany mitochondria release (more) energy by respiration for active transport (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW mitochondria produce / make energy` },
      { q: `On a hot sunny day, a plant in a garden loses a lot of water from its leaves and the soil becomes dry. By the afternoon the leaves of the plant are drooping (wilting).\n\nExplain, in terms of osmosis, why the plant wilts.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water moves out of / is not replaced in the plant cells by osmosis (1)\nthe cells lose turgor / become flaccid / vacuole shrinks (1)\nso the cells no longer press against the cell wall / no longer support the leaf (1)\nALLOW less pressure on the cell wall` },
      { q: `A student looks at cells from an onion root tip under a microscope. Some of the cells are dividing by mitosis.\n\nDescribe what happens to the chromosomes during mitosis.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\nchromosomes (become shorter and thicker and) become visible (1)\nchromosomes line up in the middle / along the equator of the cell (1)\ncopies of chromosomes / chromatids are pulled apart / separated (by fibres / spindle) (1)\nmove to opposite ends / poles of the cell (1)\na new nucleus / membrane forms around each set (1)\nALLOW correct names of stages for credit only if linked to the correct event\nIGNORE references to interphase` },
      { q: `A plant nursery wants to produce thousands of copies of a rare orchid that has very attractive flowers. Scientists take small pieces of meristem tissue from the orchid and grow them on sterile agar jelly containing nutrients and plant hormones.\n\nExplain why using meristem tissue is a good way for the nursery to produce these orchids.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `meristem cells are undifferentiated / can divide by mitosis and differentiate into any type of plant cell (1)\nso whole new plants can grow from a small piece of tissue / many plants produced quickly (1)\nthe new plants are genetically identical / clones so all have the attractive flowers (1)\nALLOW rare plants can be saved from extinction / plants can be grown free from disease` },
      { q: `A patient needs new nerve cells to treat a disease of the brain. Doctors could use stem cells from a donor, or they could use stem cells taken from the patient's own body.\n\nExplain why using the patient's own stem cells could be better than using stem cells from a donor.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the cells have the same antigens / are genetically identical to the patient's cells (1)\nso the immune system will not recognise them as foreign / less likely to be rejected (1)\nso the patient will not need (long-term) drugs to suppress the immune system / less risk of infection from a donor (1)\nIGNORE better match unqualified` },
      { q: `A student does an osmosis investigation with potato cylinders in five different concentrations of sugar solution. For each concentration the student does three repeats. The results for 0.4 mol/dm³ were +0.9 %, +0.7 % and +6.2 % (data written for this practice question).\n\nIdentify the anomalous result, suggest one possible cause of it, and state how the student should deal with it.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `+6.2 (%) (1)\npossible cause, e.g. cylinder not blotted / extra solution left on surface / cylinder left in for longer / mass recorded incorrectly / cylinder cut from a different potato (1)\nignore it when calculating the mean / repeat that measurement (1)\nIGNORE human error unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the rate of uptake of nitrate ions by roots at different oxygen concentrations. The graph shows that at 0 % oxygen the uptake is very low. As the oxygen concentration increases from 0 % to 10 %, uptake increases steeply. Above 10 % oxygen, uptake stays the same (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `more oxygen means more aerobic respiration in root cells (1)\nso more energy is released for active transport (of nitrate ions) (1)\nabove 10 % another factor is limiting / number of carrier proteins limits uptake / respiration rate is at a maximum (1)\nALLOW a small uptake at 0 % due to diffusion / anaerobic respiration\nDO NOT ALLOW energy produced` },
      { q: `A student investigates osmosis using this method:\n• cut cylinders of potato of different lengths using a knife\n• weigh each cylinder\n• place one cylinder into each of five concentrations of sugar solution\n• leave the cylinders in the solutions for different times, between 15 and 40 minutes\n• take each cylinder out and weigh it straight away.\nThe student's results do not show a clear pattern.\n\nSuggest two improvements to the method. Explain how each improvement would make the results more valid or repeatable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two improvements, each with a matching explanation (max 4):\nuse a cork borer / ruler to cut cylinders of the same size / length (1) so the surface area for osmosis is the same (1)\nleave all cylinders for the same time (1) so each has the same time for water to move (1)\nblot / roll each cylinder dry before weighing (1) so solution on the surface does not add to the mass (1)\ndo repeats at each concentration and calculate a mean (1) so anomalies can be identified / results are more repeatable (1)\nuse the same volume of solution / same temperature (1) so only concentration affects the result (1)\nExplanation must match the improvement` },
      { q: `A scientist grows onion root tip cells. The complete cell cycle of these cells takes 18 hours. The scientist looks at 200 cells under a microscope and counts 22 cells that are in mitosis. The rest are in interphase (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe proportion of cells in mitosis is equal to the proportion of the cell cycle spent in mitosis.\n\nCalculate how long, in minutes, these cells spend in mitosis in each cell cycle. Explain why most of the cells seen were in interphase.\nTime in mitosis = ______ minutes`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `22 ÷ 200 = 0.11 / 11 % (1)\n0.11 × 18 = 1.98 hours (1)\n1.98 × 60 = 118.8 / 119 minutes (1)\nALLOW 118.8 or 119 or 120 with no working for 3 marks\ninterphase is the longest stage of the cycle / cells spend most time growing and copying DNA / organelles (1)` },
      { q: `Type 1 diabetes happens when the cells in the pancreas that make insulin are destroyed. Scientists are investigating whether stem cells could be used to treat type 1 diabetes.\n\nExplain how stem cells could be used to treat type 1 diabetes, and describe one problem that would need to be overcome.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `stem cells are undifferentiated / can divide and differentiate into other cell types (1)\nthey are made to differentiate into insulin-producing (pancreas) cells (1)\nthe cells are transplanted into the patient / pancreas so the patient can make their own insulin (1)\none problem, e.g. rejection by the immune system / the immune system may destroy the new cells again / risk of tumours / risk of infection / ethical issues if embryonic cells are used (1)\nIGNORE stem cells cure diabetes unqualified` },
      { q: `* A student wants to find the concentration of the cell contents of a sweet potato. The student has:\n• a sweet potato, a cork borer, a knife and a ruler\n• sugar solutions with concentrations 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and 1.0 mol/dm³\n• boiling tubes, a balance and paper towels.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the effect of sugar concentration on the mass of sweet potato cylinders, and how the results could be used to find the concentration of the cell contents.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• the measurements that should be taken\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• how to process the results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that would produce valid results, including control variables, AND clearly describes how the results are processed (percentage change, graph) to find the concentration where there is no change in mass.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method that would produce results with some control variables, AND some processing of results, e.g. calculates change in mass or plots a graph.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple method, OR a description of how results could be used. The method may lack detail.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod / measurements\n• cut cylinders with cork borer; trim to the same length with knife and ruler\n• blot and weigh each cylinder on a balance; record initial mass\n• place one cylinder in each concentration (same volume of solution)\n• leave for the same time, e.g. 30 minutes / overnight\n• remove, blot dry with paper towel, reweigh; record final mass\n• repeat (at least 3 times) at each concentration\nControl variables\n• same size / length / diameter / surface area of cylinder\n• same volume of solution; same time; same temperature; same sweet potato\nProcessing\n• calculate percentage change in mass = (final − initial) ÷ initial × 100\n• calculate the mean for each concentration, ignoring anomalies\n• plot percentage change in mass (y) against concentration (x); draw a line / curve of best fit\n• read off the concentration where the line crosses 0 % change\n• at this concentration there is no net movement of water by osmosis, so it equals the concentration of the cell contents` },
      { q: `* Stem cells could be used to treat many conditions, for example paralysis caused by spinal cord damage, type 1 diabetes and some types of blindness. Stem cells can be taken from early embryos that are left over from fertility treatment, or from the bone marrow of adults. Plants also contain stem cells in their meristems.\n\nDiscuss the potential benefits and risks of using stem cells in medicine.\nIn your answer you should compare embryonic and adult stem cells.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Discusses benefits AND risks (scientific and ethical) of using stem cells in medicine AND makes clear comparisons between embryonic and adult stem cells.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes some benefits AND some risks of using stem cells, OR gives a clear comparison of embryonic and adult stem cells with some benefits or risks.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a benefit OR a risk of using stem cells, OR a simple difference between embryonic and adult stem cells.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nBenefits\n• stem cells are undifferentiated and can differentiate into specialised cells\n• can replace damaged / destroyed cells, e.g. nerve cells, insulin-producing cells, retina cells\n• could treat conditions with no current cure / reduce the need for organ donors\n• bone marrow (adult) stem cells already used to treat leukaemia\n• can be used to test new drugs / research how diseases develop\nRisks\n• rejection by the immune system (donor cells); patient may need immunosuppressant drugs\n• stem cells may divide uncontrollably and form tumours / cancer\n• risk of transferring viral infection from donor cells\n• treatment is new; long-term effects unknown; expensive\nEthical issues\n• obtaining embryonic stem cells destroys an embryo; some people believe an embryo has a right to life\n• embryos used are spare from IVF and would otherwise be destroyed\n• donors must give consent\nComparison\n• embryonic stem cells can differentiate into any type of cell; adult stem cells into a limited range\n• embryonic stem cells easier to grow in large numbers\n• adult stem cells can come from the patient so less risk of rejection and fewer ethical issues\n• removing bone marrow can be painful / carries some risk to the donor\nALLOW reference to meristems: plant stem cells used to clone plants, but are not used in human medicine` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.2': {
    name: 'The Challenges of Size',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student spins a sample of blood in a centrifuge. The blood separates into layers. The bottom layer is red, there is a thin pale layer in the middle and the top layer is a straw-coloured liquid.\n\nName the straw-coloured liquid in the top layer.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `plasma (1)\nIGNORE serum\nDO NOT ALLOW water` },
      { q: `This question is about the heart and circulation.\nBlood leaves the right ventricle and travels to the lungs, where it picks up oxygen.\n\nWhich blood vessel carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\naorta / pulmonary artery / pulmonary vein / vena cava`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `pulmonary artery (1)\nIf more than one option ringed, do not award the mark` },
      { q: `An adult human has about 30 000 000 000 000 red blood cells in their blood.\n\nGive this number in standard form.\nNumber of red blood cells = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `3 × 10¹³ (1)\nALLOW 3.0 × 10¹³\nDO NOT ALLOW 30 × 10¹² (not standard form)` },
      { q: `Root hair cells absorb nitrate ions from the soil. The concentration of nitrate ions inside the root hair cell is higher than the concentration in the soil water.\n\nName the process root hair cells use to absorb the nitrate ions.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `active transport (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW diffusion / osmosis` },
      { q: `A student sets up a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. The student cuts the shoot from the plant and fits it into the potometer while the cut end of the stem is under water.\n\nWhy does the student cut the shoot and fit it into the potometer under water?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `to stop air (bubbles) entering the xylem / stem / potometer (1)\nALLOW to stop the xylem being blocked by air\nIGNORE to keep the plant alive / to stop it drying out` },
      { q: `Xylem vessels carry water from the roots to the leaves of a tree. The walls of xylem vessels are thickened with a strong substance so that they do not collapse.\n\nName the substance that thickens and strengthens the walls of xylem vessels.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `lignin (1)\nALLOW phonetic spelling, e.g. lignen\nDO NOT ALLOW cellulose / cuticle / wax` },
      { q: `Biologists use cubes to model the shape of living organisms.\nA student models a small organism as a cube with sides of 2 cm.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the cube.\nSurface area : volume = ______ : 1`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `surface area = 6 × 2 × 2 = 24 (cm²) AND volume = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 (cm³) (1)\n3 (: 1) (1)\nALLOW 24 : 8 for 1 mark only\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Fish have a single circulatory system. Blood is pumped from the heart to the gills, then flows on to the rest of the body before returning to the heart.\nHumans have a double circulatory system.\n\nExplain how the human circulatory system is different from the fish circulatory system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(in humans) blood passes through the heart twice for each complete circuit (of the body) (1)\none circuit goes to the lungs and one circuit goes to the rest of the body (1)\nALLOW in fish blood passes through the heart once per circuit\nALLOW pulmonary circulation and systemic circulation\nIGNORE references to the number of chambers unless linked to circuits` },
      { q: `A student looks at a blood smear using a light microscope. In a drawing of the smear, one red blood cell has a diameter of 30 mm. The real diameter of a red blood cell is 7.5 µm.\n(1 mm = 1000 µm)\n\nCalculate the magnification of the drawing.\nMagnification = × ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `30 mm = 30 000 µm OR 30 000 ÷ 7.5 (1)\n(×) 4000 (1)\nALLOW 4 × 10³\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\n0.0075 mm used correctly also gains both marks` },
      { q: `Capillaries are the smallest blood vessels. They carry blood close to every cell in the body, including muscle cells that need a supply of oxygen and glucose.\n\nExplain how the wall of a capillary is adapted for its function.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `wall is one cell thick / very thin (1)\nso short diffusion distance (for oxygen / glucose / carbon dioxide to move between blood and cells) (1)\nALLOW wall is permeable / has gaps so substances can pass through for 1 mark\nIGNORE thin cell wall` },
      { q: `A nurse presses a finger on a vein in the back of a patient's hand and slides the finger towards the wrist. The vein empties behind the finger, but blood does not flow back into the emptied section from the direction of the heart.\n\nExplain why blood does not flow back into the emptied section of the vein.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `veins have valves (1)\n(valves) prevent the backflow of blood / make sure blood only flows towards the heart (1)\nALLOW blood in veins is at low pressure so valves are needed\nIGNORE valves pump blood` },
      { q: `Thrombocytopenia is a condition in which a person has fewer platelets in their blood than normal. People with this condition may bruise easily and small cuts bleed for a long time.\n\nExplain why people with thrombocytopenia bleed for a long time from small cuts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `platelets are needed for blood clotting (1)\nfewer platelets so the blood clots more slowly / a clot / scab does not form (quickly) to seal the wound (1)\nALLOW platelets are cell fragments that help form a clot\nIGNORE platelets fight infection` },
      { q: `White blood cells are part of the blood. There are different types of white blood cell, including phagocytes and lymphocytes.\n\nDescribe how phagocytes and lymphocytes help to protect the body against pathogens.\nPhagocytes: ______\nLymphocytes: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `phagocytes: engulf / ingest / digest pathogens (1)\nALLOW phagocytosis\nlymphocytes: produce antibodies (1)\nALLOW produce antitoxins\nIGNORE kill / fight pathogens unqualified` },
      { q: `A student paints clear nail varnish onto the lower surface of a leaf, lets it dry and peels it off. The peel shows the pattern of stomata. Under the microscope the student counts 24 stomata in a field of view. The area of the field of view is 0.12 mm².\n\nCalculate the number of stomata per mm² of leaf surface.\nNumber of stomata = ______ per mm²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `24 ÷ 0.12 (1)\n200 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student uses a bubble potometer to measure the water uptake by a leafy shoot. The student measures how far the air bubble moves in 10 minutes. The student repeats the measurement three times.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nRepeat 1: 4.2 cm\nRepeat 2: 4.6 cm\nRepeat 3: 3.9 cm\n\nCalculate the mean rate of movement of the bubble in cm per minute.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nMean rate = ______ cm per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean distance = (4.2 + 4.6 + 3.9) ÷ 3 = 4.23 (cm) (1)\n4.23 ÷ 10 = 0.42 (cm per minute) (1)\nALLOW 0.423 for 1 mark if not rounded to 2 s.f.\nECF from an incorrect mean divided by 10` },
      { q: `Scientists supplied a leaf with carbon dioxide containing radioactive carbon. The leaf made radioactive sugars in photosynthesis. The scientists detected the radioactive sugar in the phloem of the stem 45 cm away from the leaf after 30 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of movement of the sugar in the phloem in cm per hour.\nRate = ______ cm per hour`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `45 ÷ 30 = 1.5 (cm per minute) OR 30 minutes = 0.5 hours (1)\n90 (cm per hour) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `On a hot, dry, sunny afternoon the guard cells in a leaf lose water and become flaccid.\n\nExplain how this affects the stomata and why this is useful to the plant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the stomata close (1)\nso less water (vapour) is lost from the leaf / reduces transpiration / stops the plant wilting (1)\nALLOW guard cells straighten / become less curved so the gap closes\nIGNORE stops water getting in` },
      { q: `Root hair cells are found on the surface of plant roots. Each root hair cell has a long, thin extension that grows out into the soil.\n\nExplain how this shape helps the root hair cell to absorb water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(gives a) large surface area (1)\nso more water is absorbed / water is absorbed faster by osmosis (1)\nALLOW increases surface area to volume ratio\nIGNORE thin cell wall` },
      { q: `A farmer waters a field of lettuces. The soil water is a dilute solution. The cytoplasm and vacuole of the root hair cells contain a more concentrated solution of sugars and mineral ions.\n\nExplain how water moves from the soil into the root hair cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(by) osmosis (1)\n(water moves) from a dilute solution to a more concentrated solution / from a high water concentration to a low water concentration across a partially permeable membrane (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW active transport\nDO NOT ALLOW water moves from low concentration to high concentration unless qualified as solute concentration` },
      { q: `A healthy adult has about 5 000 000 red blood cells in each mm³ of blood. The adult has a blood volume of 5 dm³.\n(1 dm³ = 1 000 000 mm³)\n\nCalculate the total number of red blood cells in the adult's blood.\nGive your answer in standard form.\nNumber of red blood cells = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `5 dm³ = 5 000 000 mm³ OR 5 000 000 × 5 000 000 (1)\n2.5 × 10¹³ (1)\nALLOW 25 000 000 000 000 for 1 mark only (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `A doctor measures the heart of a student at rest.\nThe student's heart rate is 68 beats per minute.\nThe volume of blood pumped out of the left ventricle in each beat is 75 cm³.\nCardiac output = heart rate × volume of blood pumped per beat\n\nCalculate the student's cardiac output in dm³ per minute.\n(1 dm³ = 1000 cm³)\nCardiac output = ______ dm³ per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `68 × 75 = 5100 (cm³ per minute) (1)\n5.1 (dm³ per minute) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A potometer measures how quickly a leafy shoot takes up water. Students often use the results as a measure of the rate of transpiration.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the rate of water uptake is not exactly the same as the rate of transpiration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nsome water is used in photosynthesis (1)\nsome water is used to keep cells turgid / is stored in cells / vacuoles (1)\nsome water is used in other reactions / for growth (1)\nsome water may leak from the apparatus / evaporate from the capillary tube (1)\nIGNORE some water is lost through the roots` },
      { q: `This question is about transport in plants.\nThe table shows three substances that are transported in plants.\n\nPut a tick (✓) in each row to show whether the substance is transported in the xylem, the phloem or both.\nSubstance | Xylem | Phloem\nwater | | \nmineral ions | | \nsucrose | | `, marks: 2, command: 'Put ticks', markScheme: `water – xylem; mineral ions – xylem; sucrose – phloem\nall three rows correct (2)\ntwo rows correct (1)\nA row with ticks in both columns scores 0 for that row\nALLOW water in both columns (water is also in phloem sap) if every other row is correct` },
      { q: `A student dissects a sheep's heart. The student measures the thickness of the wall of each ventricle.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nLeft ventricle wall: 15 mm\nRight ventricle wall: 5 mm\n\nExplain why the left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right ventricle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `left ventricle pumps blood to the (whole) body / right ventricle only pumps blood to the lungs (1)\nso the left ventricle must produce a higher pressure / pump blood further (so needs more muscle) (1)\nALLOW lungs are close to the heart / lung tissue could be damaged by high pressure\nIGNORE stronger blood` },
      { q: `Amoeba is a single-celled organism that lives in ponds. It takes in oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide across its cell membrane. It does not have a transport system.\n\nExplain why Amoeba does not need a transport system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(it is small so) it has a large surface area to volume ratio (1)\n(so) diffusion (across the membrane) is fast enough to supply its needs / short diffusion distance to all parts of the cell (1)\nALLOW enough oxygen can diffuse in to meet its needs\nIGNORE it is small unqualified` },
      { q: `A student makes three cubes of agar jelly containing an indicator. The jelly is pink. The cubes have sides of 1 cm, 2 cm and 3 cm. The student puts all three cubes into acid at the same time. The jelly turns colourless as the acid diffuses into it.\n\nPredict which cube will become completely colourless first.\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `1 cm cube (1)\nlargest surface area to volume ratio / shortest distance for the acid to diffuse to the centre (1)\nALLOW SA:V of 6 : 1 compared with 3 : 1 and 2 : 1\nThe reason mark is dependent on the correct cube` },
      { q: `Flatworms are thin, flat animals that live in water. They have no transport system. A student models a flatworm as a cuboid 4 cm long, 2 cm wide and 1 cm thick.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the model.\nUse your answer to explain why the flatworm can survive without a transport system, given that a cube with sides of 2 cm has a ratio of 3 : 1.\nSurface area : volume = ______ : 1`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `surface area = 2 × [(4 × 2) + (4 × 1) + (2 × 1)] = 28 (cm²) AND volume = 4 × 2 × 1 = 8 (cm³) (1)\n3.5 (: 1) (1)\nflat shape gives a larger SA:V than the cube of the same volume so (oxygen) can diffuse to all cells quickly enough / short diffusion distance (1)\nECF from an incorrect ratio for the third mark if the comparison is correct` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a shoot. The air bubble moves along a capillary tube with a radius of 0.5 mm. The bubble moves 36 mm in 5 minutes.\nVolume of water in the tube = π × radius² × distance moved\n\nCalculate the rate of water uptake in mm³ per minute.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nRate of water uptake = ______ mm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `volume = π × 0.5² × 36 (1)\n= 28.3 (mm³) (1)\n28.3 ÷ 5 = 5.7 (mm³ per minute) (1)\nALLOW use of π = 3.14 giving 28.26 and 5.7\nALLOW 5.65 for 2 marks (not to 2 s.f.)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student investigates where water is lost from leaves. The student takes three similar leaves and treats them in different ways. Petroleum jelly is waterproof. The student weighs each leaf, hangs them up for 24 hours, then weighs them again.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nLeaf A, no petroleum jelly: 5.00 g at start, 4.20 g after 24 hours\nLeaf B, petroleum jelly on upper surface: 5.00 g at start, 4.40 g after 24 hours\nLeaf C, petroleum jelly on lower surface: 5.00 g at start, 4.85 g after 24 hours\n\nCalculate the percentage loss in mass of leaf C, and use the results to explain where most water is lost from a leaf.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(5.00 − 4.85) ÷ 5.00 × 100 = 3 (%) (1)\nleaf C (lower surface covered) lost the least mass / leaf A lost 16% and leaf B lost 12% (1)\n(so most water is lost from the lower surface because) the lower surface has more stomata (1)\nALLOW the upper surface has a waxy cuticle / fewer stomata\nIGNORE water lost by osmosis` },
      { q: `Arteries and veins both carry blood. Blood in the arteries is at a much higher pressure than blood in the veins.\n\nCompare the structure of arteries and veins.\nRelate each difference to the function of the vessel.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any three from:\narteries have thicker walls to withstand high pressure (1)\narteries have more muscle / elastic tissue (in the wall) to stretch and recoil / maintain pressure (1)\nveins have a wider lumen to reduce resistance to (low pressure) blood flow (1)\nveins have valves to prevent backflow (because pressure is low) (1)\narteries have a narrow lumen to maintain high pressure (1)\nEach marking point must include a comparison and a link to function\nIGNORE arteries carry oxygenated blood` },
      { q: `Xylem vessels transport water and mineral ions up a plant. Xylem vessels are made from dead cells joined end to end, with no end walls between them, and their walls contain lignin.\n\nExplain how the structure of xylem vessels is adapted to their function.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\nno end walls / continuous tube so water can flow freely / without obstruction (1)\ndead cells / no cytoplasm / no contents so more space for water flow (1)\nlignin strengthens the walls / stops vessels collapsing (1)\nlignin makes walls waterproof (1)\nnarrow tubes help water to move up (in a continuous column) (1)\nEach structure must be linked to its function for the mark` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot, first in still air and then with an electric fan blowing air across the leaves. All other conditions are kept the same.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nStill air: 1.2 cm³ per hour\nWith fan: 3.0 cm³ per hour\n\nCalculate how many times faster the rate of water uptake is with the fan.\nExplain the effect of the fan.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.0 ÷ 1.2 = 2.5 (times) (1)\nfan moves / blows water vapour away from the leaf surface (1)\nso a steeper concentration gradient (of water vapour) between the air spaces in the leaf and the air / faster diffusion (of water vapour) out of the stomata (1)\nIGNORE plant gets more air / more oxygen` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on the rate of water uptake by a plant shoot.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\n10 °C: 0.4 mm per minute\n20 °C: 0.9 mm per minute\n30 °C: 1.6 mm per minute\n\nDescribe and explain the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `as temperature increases, the rate of water uptake increases (1)\nALLOW quoted data, e.g. rate increases four times between 10 °C and 30 °C\nwater molecules have more (kinetic) energy / move faster (1)\nso faster evaporation (from cell surfaces) / faster diffusion of water vapour out of the stomata (so more water is pulled up the xylem) (1)\nIGNORE enzymes work faster` },
      { q: `Some babies are born with a hole in the wall (septum) between the left and right ventricles. When the ventricles contract, some blood flows through the hole from the left ventricle into the right ventricle.\n\nSuggest why a baby with this condition may get tired easily and become breathless when feeding.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any three from:\nless blood is pumped (from the left ventricle) to the body through the aorta (1)\nless oxygen delivered to the (muscle) cells / tissues (1)\nless (aerobic) respiration / less energy released (in muscle cells) (1)\nmore blood is sent to the lungs / the heart has to work harder (to circulate enough blood) (1)\nALLOW oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix\nIGNORE less energy produced / made` },
      { q: `Aphids are small insects that feed on plants. An aphid pushes its mouthparts into the stem of a plant and feeds on the liquid in one type of transport tissue. Aphids are found on young leaves at the top of a plant and also on the stems near the roots.\n\nName the tissue that aphids feed from, and explain why aphids can find food both above and below the leaves.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `phloem (1)\nphloem contains dissolved sugars / sucrose (made in the leaves) (1)\nsugars are moved (by translocation) in both directions / up and down the plant to growing parts and to storage organs / roots (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW xylem\nALLOW from source to sink for the third mark` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot with a potometer. The student places a lamp at different distances from the shoot. A heat filter is placed between the lamp and the shoot.\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nlamp switched off (dark): 2 mm per minute\nlamp at 40 cm: 6 mm per minute\nlamp at 20 cm: 11 mm per minute\nlamp at 10 cm: 12 mm per minute\n\nDescribe and explain the results.\nUse ideas about stomata in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `as the lamp gets closer / light intensity increases, the rate of water uptake increases (1)\nthe rate levels off / only increases a little between 20 cm and 10 cm (1)\nALLOW quoted data for either of the first two marks\nmore light causes more stomata to open / stomata open wider (1)\nso more water vapour diffuses out of the leaf / more transpiration so more water is pulled up (1)\nALLOW rate levels off because all stomata are fully open / another factor is limiting\nIGNORE the heat filter keeps the temperature constant` },
      { q: `This question is about the heart and circulation.\nComplete the sentences to describe the path of blood through the heart.\nUse words from the list. Each word may be used once, more than once or not at all.\naorta / left atrium / pulmonary artery / pulmonary vein / right atrium / vena cava\n\nDeoxygenated blood from the body enters the heart through the ______.\nIt is pumped from the right ventricle to the lungs through the ______.\nOxygenated blood returns to the heart in the pulmonary vein and enters the ______.\nIt is then pumped from the left ventricle to the body through the ______.`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `vena cava (1)\npulmonary artery (1)\nleft atrium (1)\naorta (1)` },
      { q: `Biologists model three organisms of different sizes as cubes.\nOrganism model with sides of 0.1 mm: surface area to volume ratio = 60 : 1\nOrganism model with sides of 1 mm: surface area to volume ratio = 6 : 1\nOrganism model with sides of 10 mm: surface area to volume ratio = ______\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the cube with sides of 10 mm.\nUse the data to explain why large multicellular organisms need specialised exchange surfaces and a transport system.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `600 ÷ 1000 = 0.6 (: 1) (1)\nas size increases, the surface area to volume ratio decreases (1)\n(in large organisms) diffusion across the body surface cannot supply enough oxygen / food for the volume of cells / remove enough carbon dioxide / waste (1)\ndiffusion distance (to inner cells) is too long so a transport system is needed / exchange surfaces increase the surface area (e.g. alveoli, villi, gills, root hairs) (1)\nIGNORE large organisms need more energy unqualified` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate the effect of air movement on the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. The student has this apparatus: a bubble potometer with a scale beside the capillary tube, a leafy shoot, a stopwatch, and an electric fan that has three speed settings.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the effect of air movement on the rate of water uptake.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to set up the potometer\n• how to change the air movement\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• the measurements that should be taken.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that covers setting up the potometer, changing air movement, at least two control variables and the measurements needed to calculate a rate, including repeats.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method that covers changing air movement and measuring the bubble, with some control variables OR some detail of setting up.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple statement about using the fan and measuring the bubble.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nsetting up\n• cut the shoot under water (at an angle)\n• fit the shoot into the potometer under water / make sure it is airtight (e.g. seal with petroleum jelly)\n• make sure there are no air bubbles other than the measuring bubble\n• dry the leaves\n• leave the shoot to acclimatise for a few minutes\nindependent variable\n• use fan at off / low / medium / high setting\n• keep the fan at the same distance from the shoot\ncontrol variables\n• same shoot / same number or area of leaves\n• same temperature (room)\n• same light intensity (same distance from window / lamp)\n• same humidity\nmeasurements\n• measure the distance the bubble moves on the scale\n• in a fixed time, e.g. 5 minutes, timed with the stopwatch\n• reset the bubble (using the reservoir / tap) between readings\n• repeat at each setting and calculate a mean\n• calculate rate = distance ÷ time` },
      { q: `On a warm, sunny day, a sunflower plant loses a large volume of water from its leaves. The water is replaced by water taken in from the soil. Each root of the sunflower has many root hair cells. The stem contains xylem vessels and the leaves have many stomata on the lower surface.\n\nExplain how water moves from the soil, through the sunflower plant and into the air.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of water uptake by root hair cells by osmosis, movement up the xylem in the transpiration stream, and evaporation and diffusion of water vapour out through the stomata, linking structures to processes.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of the route of water (root, xylem, leaf, stomata) with some explanation of at least one process, e.g. osmosis into root hair cells OR evaporation/diffusion from the leaf.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple description of water entering the roots and leaving through the leaves.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nroot\n• water enters root hair cells by osmosis\n• from a dilute solution (soil water) to a more concentrated solution in the cell / across a partially permeable membrane\n• root hair cells have a large surface area\n• water moves across the root (cell to cell) to the xylem\nstem\n• water moves up the xylem vessels\n• xylem is a continuous tube of dead cells with no end walls, strengthened by lignin\n• water moves as a continuous column / transpiration stream\n• water is pulled up as water is lost from the leaves\nleaf\n• water evaporates from the surfaces of cells inside the leaf (into the air spaces)\n• water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata\n• stomata are open in the light / opened by turgid guard cells\n• warm, sunny conditions increase the rate of evaporation / transpiration\nDO NOT ALLOW water moves by active transport\nDO NOT ALLOW water moves in the phloem` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.1': {
    name: 'Coordination — Nervous System',
    questions: [
      { q: `A boy treads on a drawing pin. His leg moves away from the pin before he is aware of the pain. This response is a reflex.\n\nWhich part of the nervous system coordinates this reflex?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nbrain stem / cerebrum / spinal cord / motor neurone`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `spinal cord ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `Scientists estimate that the human brain contains about 86 000 000 000 neurones.\n\nGive this number in standard form.\nNumber of neurones = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `8.6 × 10¹⁰ ✓\nALLOW 8.6 x 10^10\nDO NOT ALLOW 86 × 10⁹` },
      { q: `An optician uses a bright light to look at the back of a patient's eye. The optician is checking the layer that contains the light-sensitive receptor cells.\n\nName this layer of the eye.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `retina ✓\nIGNORE rods / cones\nDO NOT ALLOW optic nerve` },
      { q: `After a stroke, a patient finds it difficult to remember new information and to make decisions. A brain scan shows that blood flow to the largest, folded outer region of the brain has been reduced.\n\nWhich region of the brain is responsible for memory and conscious thought?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\ncerebellum / cerebrum / medulla / pituitary gland`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `cerebrum ✓` },
      { q: `A man is red-green colour blind. He finds it difficult to tell the difference between red and green traffic lights by colour alone.\n\nWhich type of receptor cell in the retina does not work properly in a person with colour blindness?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `cone (cells) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW rods` },
      { q: `A student investigates whether drinking an energy drink containing caffeine changes reaction time. The student measures her reaction time with a ruler drop test before the drink and again 20 minutes after the drink.\n\nWhat is the independent variable in this investigation?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `whether or not the energy drink / caffeine has been drunk ✓\nALLOW caffeine (intake) / energy drink\nDO NOT ALLOW reaction time` },
      { q: `A student uses a ruler drop test to measure reaction time. A partner holds a 30 cm ruler vertically with the zero mark level with the student's open finger and thumb, then drops it without warning. The student catches the ruler and records the distance it fell.\nThe table shows the results (data written for this practice question).\nTrial 1: 18 cm\nTrial 2: 21 cm\nTrial 3: 16 cm\nTrial 4: 19 cm\nTrial 5: 21 cm\n\nCalculate the mean distance the ruler fell.\nMean distance = ______ cm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `18 + 21 + 16 + 19 + 21 = 95 ✓\n95 ÷ 5 = 19 (cm) ✓\nALLOW 19.0\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `In a ruler drop test, the reaction time can be calculated from the distance the ruler falls using the equation:\nreaction time (s) = √(2 × distance fallen (m) ÷ 9.8)\nA student's mean distance was 19 cm.\n\nCalculate the student's reaction time.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nReaction time = ______ s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `correct conversion and substitution: √(2 × 0.19 ÷ 9.8) ✓\n= 0.20 (s) ✓\nALLOW 0.197 for 1 mark only if not given to 2 s.f.\nIf distance not converted to m (answer 1.97 / 2.0 s) award 1 mark max` },
      { q: `A sensory neurone carries impulses from a touch receptor in the big toe to the spinal cord. The neurone is 0.90 m long. An impulse takes 0.015 s to travel the whole length of the neurone (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the speed of the impulse.\nUse the equation: speed = distance ÷ time\nSpeed = ______ m/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.90 ÷ 0.015 ✓\n= 60 (m/s) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student measures his reaction time using an online test. His mean reaction time is 0.24 s. After a week of practising every day, his mean reaction time is 0.21 s (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in his reaction time.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(0.24 − 0.21) ÷ 0.24 (× 100) ✓\n= 12.5 (%) ✓\nALLOW 12 or 13\nALLOW 0.03 ÷ 0.24 × 100 for first mark` },
      { q: `In a reflex arc, the end of a sensory neurone is separated from a relay neurone by a synapse. Nerve impulses cannot jump across the gap.\n\nDescribe how the impulse is passed across the synapse.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `neurotransmitter / chemical is released (from the first neurone) ✓\n(neurotransmitter) diffuses across the gap and binds to receptors on the next neurone / triggers an impulse in the next neurone ✓\nIGNORE named neurotransmitter\nDO NOT ALLOW electrical impulse crosses the gap` },
      { q: `Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a disease in which the fatty myelin sheath around the axons of neurones is damaged. People with MS often have slower and less coordinated movements.\n\nExplain why damage to the myelin sheath causes these symptoms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `myelin (sheath) insulates the axon / speeds up the impulse ✓\n(so damage means) impulses travel more slowly / do not reach the muscles (effectors) properly ✓\nALLOW impulses may leak / are lost for second mark\nIGNORE nerves are damaged unqualified` },
      { q: `A chef accidentally touches a hot baking tray. Her hand moves away from the tray within a fraction of a second, before she has thought about it.\n\nExplain why it is an advantage for this response to be a reflex.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `response is (very) fast / automatic / does not involve the conscious brain ✓\n(so) less damage to the skin / tissue / reduces burns ✓\nIGNORE prevents pain` },
      { q: `When the chef touches the hot tray, an impulse travels along a reflex arc.\n\nComplete the sentences to describe the pathway.\nThe heat is detected by a ______ in the skin.\nThe impulse travels along a sensory neurone to a ______ neurone in the spinal cord.\nThe impulse then travels along a ______ neurone to a muscle in the arm, which contracts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `receptor ✓ (ALLOW sensory receptor / temperature receptor)\nrelay AND motor (in that order) ✓\nAll three correct = 2 marks; two correct = 1 mark` },
      { q: `Motor neurones carry impulses from the spinal cord to muscles. Some motor neurones are over one metre long and connect with many other neurones in the spinal cord.\n\nGive two ways in which the structure of a motor neurone is adapted to its function.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nlong axon to carry impulses over long distances / to the effector ✓\nmany dendrites / branched endings to connect with many other neurones ✓\nmyelin sheath to insulate / speed up impulses ✓\nnerve endings / synapses with muscle (fibres) to pass on the impulse ✓\nIGNORE has a nucleus` },
      { q: `Light passes through several structures before it reaches the back of the eye.\nThe table shows two of these structures.\ncornea: ______\niris: ______\n\nDescribe the function of each structure.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `cornea: refracts / bends / focuses light (entering the eye) ✓\nALLOW transparent layer that lets light in / protects the front of the eye\niris: controls the size of the pupil / controls how much light enters the eye ✓\nIGNORE coloured part of the eye` },
      { q: `A student looks up from reading a book and focuses on a bird in a distant tree.\n\nComplete the sentences to describe how the student's eye changes to focus on the distant bird.\nThe ciliary muscles ______.\nThe suspensory ligaments become ______.\nThe lens becomes ______ so light is refracted less.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `ciliary muscles relax\nsuspensory ligaments become tight / taut / tense\nlens becomes thinner / flatter / less convex\nAll three correct ✓✓; two correct ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW lens becomes smaller` },
      { q: `An older man finds it difficult to read a newspaper because the print looks blurred. Distant objects are clear. In his eyes, light from near objects is focused behind the retina.\n\nExplain how wearing glasses with convex lenses corrects his vision.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `convex lens refracts / bends light inwards / makes light rays converge (before they enter the eye) ✓\n(so) light is focused on the retina ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW lens focuses light on the lens` },
      { q: `A short-sighted woman is deciding whether to have laser eye surgery or to keep wearing glasses. In laser eye surgery the cornea is reshaped.\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage of laser eye surgery compared with wearing glasses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `advantage: any one from: permanent correction / no need to wear or buy glasses or contact lenses / does not get lost or broken ✓\ndisadvantage: any one from: risk of damage to the eye / infection / surgery cannot be easily reversed / expensive (at once) / side effects e.g. dry eyes, glare ✓\nIGNORE it hurts unqualified` },
      { q: `About 8% of males in the UK are red-green colour blind. A school has 600 male students.\n\nCalculate how many of the male students are likely to be red-green colour blind.\nNumber of students = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `600 × 8 ÷ 100 / 600 × 0.08 ✓\n= 48 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A footballer receives a blow to the back of the head. Afterwards he finds it difficult to walk in a straight line and to balance on one foot. His memory and speech are not affected.\n\nSuggest which region of the brain has been damaged. Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `cerebellum ✓\n(because) it controls balance / coordination of (muscular) movement ✓\nSecond mark is dependent on the cerebellum being named\nDO NOT ALLOW cerebrum` },
      { q: `A patient has a brain injury that has damaged the medulla. Doctors have connected the patient to a ventilator, which pumps air into and out of the lungs.\n\nExplain why the patient needs the ventilator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `medulla controls unconscious activities / breathing (rate) ✓\n(so) patient may not breathe without help / ventilator supplies oxygen to the body ✓\nALLOW controls heart rate for first mark` },
      { q: `A young man has damaged his spinal cord in a diving accident. He cannot move his legs. The neurones in his legs and the muscles in his legs are undamaged.\n\nSuggest two reasons why damage to the spinal cord is very difficult to treat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nneurones / nerve tissue do not repair / regenerate (easily) ✓\nspinal cord is difficult to reach / protected by the vertebrae / backbone ✓\nsurgery may damage other neurones / make the injury worse ✓\nmany neurones / connections are involved and it is hard to reconnect them correctly ✓\nALLOW stem cell treatments are still being researched / not yet available ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists use MRI scans to study the brain. An MRI scan can show which areas of the brain are active when a person carries out a task, such as listening to music.\n\nSuggest two reasons why it is difficult for scientists to find out what each region of the brain does.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nbrain is complex / has billions of neurones / many connections ✓\nmany regions are active at the same time / a task may use more than one region ✓\nbrain is protected by the skull / hard to access ✓\nunethical / dangerous to damage or experiment on living human brains ✓\neach person's brain may be slightly different ✓` },
      { q: `A student measures her reaction time five times with a ruler drop test and calculates a mean.\n\nExplain why the student repeats the test and calculates a mean.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(to) identify / remove anomalies / reduce the effect of random errors ✓\n(so the) result is more accurate / closer to true value / check results are repeatable ✓\nIGNORE fair test\nIGNORE to make it reliable unqualified` },
      { q: `Two students compare ways of measuring reaction time. Student A uses a ruler drop test. Student B uses a computer program which shows a coloured square at a random time; the student presses a key as soon as the square appears and the computer records the reaction time to 0.001 s.\n\nGive two advantages of the computer method compared with the ruler drop test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nmore precise / higher resolution / measures to 0.001 s ✓\nno human error in reading the ruler / no need to convert distance to time ✓\ntiming of the stimulus is truly random / the catcher cannot predict the drop from the dropper's movement ✓\nsame stimulus every time / easier to repeat many times ✓\nIGNORE more reliable / more accurate unqualified` },
      { q: `A man walking barefoot steps on a sharp stone. His leg muscles contract and his foot is lifted off the stone.\n\nDescribe the pathway of nerve impulses in this reflex.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(pain / pressure) receptor (in the skin) detects the stimulus ✓\nimpulse travels along sensory neurone to the spinal cord / CNS ✓\n(passes across synapse to) relay neurone then (across synapse to) motor neurone ✓\n(motor neurone carries impulse) to effector / (leg) muscle, which contracts ✓\nMax 3\nIGNORE brain` },
      { q: `A student is reading a text message on her phone. She then looks up to watch a bus approaching from the far end of the road.\n\nExplain how her eye changes to focus light from the distant bus on the retina.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ciliary muscles relax ✓\nsuspensory ligaments become tight / taut / pulled ✓\nlens is pulled thinner / flatter ✓\n(so) light is refracted less (to focus on the retina) ✓\nMax 3\nDO NOT ALLOW ciliary muscles contract / suspensory ligaments relax` },
      { q: `A boy cannot see the board clearly from the back of the classroom, but he can read his book clearly. An optician tells him his eyeballs are slightly too long.\n\nExplain why he cannot see distant objects clearly and how this can be corrected.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `he is short-sighted / has myopia ✓\n(light from distant objects) is focused in front of the retina ✓\ncorrected with concave / diverging lenses (glasses or contact lenses) ✓\nALLOW laser surgery to reshape the cornea as correction\nDO NOT ALLOW convex lenses` },
      { q: `Curare is a poison that was used on arrow tips by hunters in South America. Curare binds to the receptors on muscle cells where motor neurones end. It does not affect the release of neurotransmitter from the motor neurone.\nAn animal hit by a curare-tipped arrow becomes paralysed.\n\nExplain why curare causes paralysis.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `neurotransmitter is still released from the motor neurone / diffuses across the gap ✓\nneurotransmitter cannot bind to the receptors (because curare is bound / blocking them) ✓\n(so) muscle (cell) is not stimulated / no impulse passes to the muscle / muscle cannot contract ✓\nALLOW curare has a similar shape to the neurotransmitter` },
      { q: `The knee-jerk reflex arc has only two neurones: a sensory neurone that connects directly to a motor neurone in the spinal cord. The reflex that withdraws a hand from a hot object has three neurones.\nThe table shows the time taken for each reflex in one person (data written for this practice question).\nknee-jerk reflex: 0.03 s\nhand withdrawal reflex: 0.05 s\n\nSuggest reasons why the knee-jerk reflex is faster than the hand withdrawal reflex.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `knee-jerk reflex has no relay neurone / fewer neurones ✓\n(so) fewer synapses / only one synapse (in the spinal cord) ✓\ntransmission across a synapse is slow(er) / involves diffusion of neurotransmitter ✓\nALLOW the pathway is shorter / impulses travel a shorter distance ✓\nMax 3` },
      { q: `A student tests the claim that caffeine reduces reaction time. She measures the reaction time of four volunteers before and 20 minutes after drinking a cup of coffee (data written for this practice question).\nMean reaction time before coffee: 0.26 s, 0.24 s, 0.27 s, 0.25 s\nMean reaction time after coffee: 0.21 s, 0.22 s, 0.20 s, 0.21 s\nShe concludes: 'Caffeine reduces reaction time in everyone.'\n\nEvaluate her conclusion.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `supports: reaction time decreased for all four volunteers / mean fell from 0.255 s to 0.21 s ✓\nALLOW a decrease of about 18%\nagainst: (only) four volunteers / small sample so cannot conclude about everyone ✓\nagainst: any one from: no control group / did not test a drink without caffeine / volunteers may have improved with practice / other variables (e.g. tiredness) not controlled ✓` },
      { q: `A patient has a tumour deep inside the brain. Doctors are considering surgery to remove it.\n\nExplain why removing a tumour from deep inside the brain is difficult and risky.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\ndifficult to reach / brain is protected by the skull ✓\nsurgery may damage (healthy) neurones / other regions of the brain on the way ✓\ndamaged neurones do not regenerate / damage is permanent ✓\nmay lose functions such as movement / memory / speech ✓\nrisk of infection / bleeding in the brain ✓\nhard to know exactly which functions the area controls ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists have used different methods to find out what regions of the brain do.\nMethod 1: studying patients who have damage to one region of the brain.\nMethod 2: electrically stimulating small regions of the brain during surgery while the patient is awake.\nMethod 3: MRI scanning while a person carries out tasks.\n\nChoose one method. Describe how it shows the function of a region of the brain, and give one limitation of the method.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Method 1: observe which functions / behaviours are lost or changed ✓ link the loss to the damaged region ✓ limitation: damage often affects more than one region / few patients / cannot choose where the damage is ✓\nMethod 2: stimulate region and observe response, e.g. a muscle moves / patient reports a sensation or memory ✓ links region to that function ✓ limitation: invasive / only possible during surgery / risk to patient / ethical issues ✓\nMethod 3: active areas show increased blood flow ✓ identifies which region is used for that task ✓ limitation: expensive / many regions active at once / only shows activity, not cause ✓\nMarks must come from one method only` },
      { q: `Different parts of the eye have different functions.\nThe table lists three parts of the eye.\nsclera: ______\noptic nerve: ______\nsuspensory ligaments: ______\n\nComplete the table to describe the function of each part.`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `sclera: tough outer layer that protects the eye / maintains shape of eyeball ✓\noptic nerve: carries impulses from the retina to the brain ✓\nALLOW sensory neurones to the brain\nsuspensory ligaments: hold the lens in place / connect lens to ciliary muscles / pull on the lens to change its shape ✓` },
      { q: `Some drugs that are used to treat depression work at synapses in the brain. One type of drug stops the neurotransmitter serotonin from being taken back up into the neurone that released it. This means more serotonin stays in the synaptic gap for longer.\n\nDescribe how an impulse is transmitted across a synapse, and suggest the effect of this drug on the next neurone.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `impulse arrives at the end of the (first) neurone and causes release of neurotransmitter (from vesicles) ✓\nneurotransmitter diffuses across the synapse / gap ✓\nbinds to receptors on the next neurone, triggering a new impulse ✓\neffect: neurotransmitter keeps binding to receptors / next neurone is stimulated more / for longer / more impulses are sent ✓\nIGNORE electrical signal crosses the gap` },
      { q: `A student measures his reaction time with a ruler drop test. He uses a table to convert each distance to a time (data written for this practice question).\nTrial 1: 0.31 s\nTrial 2: 0.27 s\nTrial 3: 0.29 s\nTrial 4: 0.45 s\nTrial 5: 0.28 s\n\nIdentify the anomalous result and suggest one reason for it.\nCalculate the mean reaction time, without the anomalous result. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `anomaly: trial 4 / 0.45 s ✓\nreason: any one from: student was distracted / not concentrating / started with hand in a different position / blinked ✓\nmean: (0.31 + 0.27 + 0.29 + 0.28) ÷ 4 = 1.15 ÷ 4 ✓\n= 0.29 (s) ✓\nALLOW 0.2875 / 0.288 for 1 mark (not rounded to 2 s.f.)\nIf anomaly included, mean = 0.32 s, award 1 mark for the calculation only` },
      { q: `In a withdrawal reflex, the impulse travels a total distance of 1.6 m along the neurones of the reflex arc. The impulses travel at 80 m/s. The whole reflex, from stimulus to the start of the muscle contracting, was measured as 0.025 s (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the time taken for the impulse to travel along the neurones.\nExplain why the measured time for the reflex is longer than this.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1.6 ÷ 80 ✓\n= 0.02 (s) ✓\nthe extra 0.005 s / extra time is taken at the synapses ✓\n(because) neurotransmitter has to diffuse across the gap / chemical transmission is slower than impulses along neurones ✓\nALLOW time for the receptor to detect the stimulus / muscle to respond for the third mark` },
      { q: `* A student wants to find out whether practising improves reaction time.\nShe has a 30 cm ruler, a partner and a conversion table that turns the distance the ruler falls into a reaction time.\n\nDescribe how the student could carry out this investigation to produce valid results.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to measure reaction time\n• the variables that need to be controlled\n• how she should process her results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method for measuring reaction time AND controlling variables AND processing results to show whether practice changes reaction time. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method for measuring reaction time AND either control of variables OR processing results. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Some relevant points, e.g. drop a ruler and catch it. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points:\nMeasuring\n• partner holds ruler vertically with zero level with the top of the student's thumb / finger\n• student's hand open, a set distance apart\n• partner drops ruler without warning / at random time\n• read distance at top of thumb where ruler is caught\n• convert distance to reaction time using the table\nControl variables\n• same person catching / same hand (dominant hand)\n• same person dropping / same ruler\n• same starting position / gap between finger and thumb\n• arm resting on the edge of a table\n• same time of day / no caffeine / same level of tiredness / no distractions\nPractice and processing\n• measure reaction time at the start, then practise a set number of times, e.g. 10 catches a day for a week\n• repeat measurement several times on each occasion, e.g. 5 trials\n• identify and remove anomalies, calculate mean\n• compare mean before and after practice / plot mean reaction time against day\n• repeat with other people to see if pattern is the same` },
      { q: `* In 1848, a railway worker called Phineas Gage survived an accident in which an iron rod passed through the front of his brain. His memory and movement were mostly unaffected, but people who knew him said his personality had changed.\nToday, doctors use MRI scans and electrical stimulation to study the brain, and spinal cord injuries and brain damage are still very difficult to treat.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to investigate how the brain works, and why it is difficult to treat damage to the brain and spinal cord.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains difficulties of investigating brain function AND explains limitations of treating damage to the brain and spinal cord, with clear links to the structure of the nervous system. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains difficulties of investigating the brain OR of treating damage, with some reference to the other. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives simple statements about the brain being complex or hard to treat. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points:\nInvestigating the brain\n• brain is very complex / has billions of neurones with many connections\n• functions are spread across several regions / regions interact\n• brain is protected by the skull so is hard to access\n• unethical to damage healthy brains in experiments\n• case studies like Gage are rare / one person / damage not to just one region / accounts of personality are not objective\n• scans show which areas are active, not exactly what they do; expensive\n• electrical stimulation only possible during surgery\nTreating damage\n• neurones do not repair / regenerate easily so damage is often permanent\n• hard to reach without damaging other tissue\n• surgery risks loss of other functions / infection / bleeding\n• spinal cord damage cuts off impulses between brain and body below the injury\n• difficult to reconnect neurones correctly\n• drugs may not reach the brain / may have side effects on other parts of the nervous system\n• stem cell treatments still being researched / not yet proven safe` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.2': {
    name: 'Coordination — Endocrine System',
    questions: [
      { q: `Thyroxine is a hormone released by the thyroid gland in the neck. The release of thyroxine is controlled by thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH), which is released by a gland at the base of the brain.\n\nName the gland that releases TSH.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Pituitary (gland) (1)\nIgnore: anterior\nDo not allow: hypothalamus / thyroid / adrenal` },
      { q: `A sprinter waits on the starting blocks before a race. Her heart begins to beat faster and her breathing rate increases even before the race starts. This is caused by a hormone released from glands above her kidneys.\n\nName the glands that release this hormone.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Adrenal glands (1)\nIgnore: naming the hormone (adrenaline)\nDo not allow: kidneys / pituitary` },
      { q: `Bananas are picked from the plant while they are still green and hard. They are transported in cool ships. When they arrive, they are stored in warehouses and exposed to a gas that makes them ripen.\n\nName the plant hormone used to ripen the bananas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Ethene (1)\nAllow: ethylene\nDo not allow: auxin / gibberellin / ethane` },
      { q: `A student puts a pot of cress seedlings on a windowsill. After three days, all the shoots are bending towards the window.\n\nWhich term describes this response?\n\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\n\nnegative gravitropism        negative phototropism        positive gravitropism        positive phototropism`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• positive phototropism (1)\nIf more than one answer is ringed, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `A student investigates the growth of maize seedlings grown in the dark. She measures the length of the shoot of four seedlings after 5 days.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nSeedling | Shoot length (mm)\nA | 12\nB | 15\nC | 13\nD | 16\n\nCalculate the mean shoot length.\n\nMean shoot length = ______ mm`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 14 (mm) (1)\n(12 + 15 + 13 + 16 = 56; 56 ÷ 4 = 14)` },
      { q: `A woman records the levels of hormones in her blood during one menstrual cycle. The cycle is 28 days long and day 1 is the first day of menstruation.\n\nThe level of LH stays low from day 1 to day 11. It then rises sharply to a peak on day 13 and falls back to a low level by day 16.\n\nOn which day is ovulation most likely to have happened?`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Day 13 / day 14 (1)\nAllow: any day from 13 to 15\nIgnore: day 12` },
      { q: `Adrenaline is a hormone. It is carried in the blood plasma to all parts of the body. Adrenaline makes heart muscle cells contract faster, but it has no effect on many other cells, such as the cells in the lens of the eye.\n\nExplain why adrenaline affects heart muscle cells but not the cells of the lens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Heart muscle cells have receptors for adrenaline (on their cell membrane) (1)\n• Lens cells do not have these receptors / adrenaline cannot bind to lens cells (1)\nAllow: heart is a target organ for adrenaline (for 1 mark only if no reference to receptors)\nIgnore: adrenaline does not reach the lens` },
      { q: `The body uses two systems to coordinate responses: the nervous system and the endocrine system.\n\nComplete the table to compare the two systems.\n\nFeature | Nervous system | Endocrine system\nHow the message travels | ______ | ______\nHow long the effect lasts | ______ | ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• How the message travels: (electrical) impulses along neurones AND hormones / chemicals carried in the blood (1)\n• How long the effect lasts: short / brief AND long / longer lasting (1)\nBoth cells in a row needed for each mark.\nIgnore: references to speed` },
      { q: `The endocrine system is made of glands that release hormones directly into the blood.\n\nComplete the table to show the main hormone released by each gland.\n\nGland | Hormone released\novaries | ______\ntestes | ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• ovaries: oestrogen (1)\nAllow: progesterone\n• testes: testosterone (1)\nIgnore: spelling errors where the hormone is clearly identified` },
      { q: `Thyroxine is a hormone that regulates the basal metabolic rate. A doctor sees a patient whose thyroid gland releases too little thyroxine. The patient has been gaining weight and often feels cold.\n\nExplain why the patient gains weight.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Low thyroxine means the (basal) metabolic rate is lower / slower (1)\n• So less (glucose / fat) is used in respiration (and more is stored as fat) (1)\nAllow: fewer chemical reactions in cells / less energy released\nIgnore: eats more` },
      { q: `A sprinter's adrenal glands release adrenaline just before a race. Adrenaline increases her heart rate.\n\nExplain why an increased heart rate helps the sprinter during the race.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• More (blood carrying) oxygen and glucose is delivered to the (leg) muscles (1)\n• For (more / faster) respiration, releasing more energy for muscle contraction (1)\nAllow: carbon dioxide is removed faster\nDo not allow: energy is made / produced` },
      { q: `A couple use condoms as their method of contraception. Condoms are a non-hormonal method of contraception.\n\nGive two advantages of using condoms rather than the combined contraceptive pill.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Protect against sexually transmitted infections (STIs) / named STI e.g. HIV (1)\n• No hormonal side effects / do not affect the woman's hormone levels (1)\n• Only need to be used at the time of sex / no need to remember to take a pill every day (1)\n• Easy to obtain / available without seeing a doctor (1)\n• The man can take responsibility for contraception (1)\nIgnore: cheaper` },
      { q: `A diaphragm is a soft dome made of silicone. A woman places it over her cervix before sexual intercourse. It is usually used with a spermicide cream.\n\nExplain how the diaphragm and spermicide prevent pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The diaphragm is a barrier that stops sperm reaching the uterus / egg (1)\n• Spermicide kills sperm (1)\nAllow: so fertilisation cannot happen (for 1 mark if neither point given)` },
      { q: `A contraceptive implant is a small flexible rod placed under the skin of the upper arm. It slowly releases progesterone into the blood for up to three years.\n\nExplain how the implant prevents pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Progesterone inhibits the release of FSH / LH (from the pituitary) (1)\n• So eggs do not mature / ovulation does not happen (1)\n• Progesterone thickens the mucus in the cervix so sperm cannot get through (1)\nIgnore: kills sperm` },
      { q: `Some women are infertile because their ovaries do not release eggs. A doctor may prescribe a drug called clomifene.\n\nClomifene stops oestrogen from inhibiting the pituitary gland.\n\nExplain how clomifene can help the woman to become pregnant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• More FSH and LH are released (from the pituitary) (1)\n• So eggs mature (FSH) and ovulation is triggered (LH) / an egg is released that can be fertilised (1)\nAllow: more FSH / more LH for the first mark` },
      { q: `In the UK, doctors usually transfer only one embryo into the uterus during IVF treatment. In the past, three or more embryos were often transferred at the same time.\n\nSuggest two reasons why doctors now usually transfer only one embryo.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• To reduce the chance of multiple births / twins / triplets (1)\n• Multiple pregnancies are a greater risk to the health of the mother (1)\n• Babies from multiple births are more likely to be born early / underweight / have health problems (1)\n• Spare embryos can be frozen for a later attempt (1)` },
      { q: `Gardeners take cuttings from plants to produce new plants. Before planting a cutting, they dip the cut end into rooting powder. Rooting powder contains a plant hormone.\n\nName the plant hormone in rooting powder and explain why it is used.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Auxin (1)\n• It stimulates / speeds up the growth of roots (from the cut stem), so the cutting grows into a new plant faster / more cuttings survive (1)\nDo not allow: gibberellin / ethene for the first mark` },
      { q: `A groundskeeper sprays a lawn with a selective weedkiller. The weedkiller contains a synthetic auxin. After two weeks, the broad-leaved weeds such as dandelions have died, but the grass is still growing well.\n\nExplain how the weedkiller kills the weeds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Broad-leaved weeds absorb more of the auxin (than narrow-leaved grass) / grass is not affected by this concentration of auxin (1)\n• Auxin causes rapid / uncontrolled growth, so the weeds cannot support themselves / use up their food stores and die (1)\nIgnore: weedkiller is poisonous / toxic` },
      { q: `Gibberellins are plant hormones. Some fruit growers spray their crops with gibberellins.\n\nGive two commercial uses of gibberellins.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• To end seed dormancy / make seeds germinate (e.g. barley for brewing) (1)\n• To promote flowering / make plants flower at a chosen time (1)\n• To increase fruit size (e.g. seedless grapes) (1)\n• To produce seedless fruit (1)\nDo not allow: ripening fruit / rooting cuttings` },
      { q: `Bananas are picked while they are unripe and green. They are ripened later using ethene gas in warehouses near the shops.\n\nSuggest two advantages to the banana supplier of ripening the bananas this way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Unripe fruit is firmer, so it is less likely to be damaged / bruised during transport (1)\n• Fruit does not over-ripen / rot during the journey, so less is wasted (1)\n• Fruit can be ripened when it is needed / ready to sell at the right time (1)\n• All the fruit ripens at the same time / uniform ripeness (1)` },
      { q: `A student grows a bean seedling in a dark box. She then turns the box so that the seedling lies horizontally. After two days the root has curved downwards.\n\nIn roots, high concentrations of auxin inhibit cell elongation.\n\nExplain why the root curves downwards.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Auxin collects on the lower side of the root (due to gravity) (1)\n• Cells on the lower side elongate less / cells on the upper side elongate more, so the root bends down (1)\nDo not allow: auxin moves to the upper side\nDo not allow: auxin kills cells` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of light from one side on the growth of cress seedlings. She grows seedlings in a box with a slit on one side. Light from a lamp shines through the slit.\n\nGive two variables the student should control in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Temperature (1)\n• Volume of water given / moisture of the soil (1)\n• Type / species / age of seedling (1)\n• Number of seeds per dish / type of soil or compost (1)\n• Distance of the lamp from the slit / brightness of the lamp (1)\n• Time allowed for growth (1)\nIgnore: amount of light` },
      { q: `In an investigation, the tips of some oat seedlings were covered with foil caps. The seedlings were then lit from one side. The seedlings with covered tips grew straight up. Seedlings with uncovered tips bent towards the light.\n\nExplain why the seedlings with covered tips did not bend towards the light.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The tip is the part that detects light / the foil stops light reaching the tip (1)\n• So auxin is not redistributed / is spread evenly, so both sides of the shoot grow at the same rate (1)\nAllow: no more auxin on the shaded side` },
      { q: `A student places a plant shoot horizontally in a dark cupboard. She measures the angle the tip of the shoot has turned upwards using a protractor. After 6 hours the shoot tip has turned upwards by 45°.\n\nCalculate the mean rate at which the shoot turned upwards. Give the unit.\n\nRate = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 45 ÷ 6 = 7.5 (1)\n• degrees per hour / ° h⁻¹ / °/h (1)\nAllow: 0.125 degrees per minute for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Oestrogen is a hormone released by the ovaries during the menstrual cycle. Its level rises during the first two weeks of the cycle.\n\nDescribe two roles of oestrogen in the menstrual cycle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Causes the lining of the uterus to (repair and) thicken / grow (1)\n• Inhibits the release of FSH (from the pituitary) (1)\n• Stimulates the release of LH (from the pituitary) (1)\nIgnore: maintains the lining` },
      { q: `The level of thyroxine in the blood is kept fairly constant by negative feedback.\n\nComplete the sentences.\n\nWhen the level of thyroxine in the blood rises, the pituitary gland releases ______ TSH.\n\nThis causes the thyroid gland to release ______ thyroxine, so the level returns to normal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• less (1)\n• less (1)\nAllow: no TSH for the first gap` },
      { q: `Iodine is needed to make thyroxine. In some parts of the world, people's diets contain very little iodine. These people can develop a goitre, which is a swelling of the thyroid gland caused by high levels of TSH.\n\nExplain why people with an iodine-deficient diet have high levels of TSH.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Without iodine, the thyroid cannot make (enough) thyroxine / thyroxine levels are low (1)\n• Low thyroxine is detected (by the pituitary / brain) (1)\n• TSH release is no longer inhibited / negative feedback causes more TSH to be released (to try to raise thyroxine) (1)\nAllow: TSH keeps being released because thyroxine never rises` },
      { q: `A man is walking home at night when a dog suddenly barks at him. A few seconds later his heart is pounding, he is breathing faster and his liver is releasing glucose into his blood.\n\nExplain how adrenaline prepares the man's body for 'fight or flight'.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• Adrenaline is released from the adrenal glands (into the blood) (1)\n• Increases heart rate / breathing rate (1)\n• Liver converts glycogen to glucose, raising blood glucose (1)\n• Blood is diverted to the muscles / away from the gut and skin (1)\n• So more oxygen and glucose reach the muscles for respiration, releasing more energy for movement (1)\nDo not allow: energy produced / made` },
      { q: `A woman has a 28-day menstrual cycle. Her progesterone level is low from day 1 to day 14. It rises from day 15 to a peak on day 21. If she is not pregnant, the progesterone level falls from day 22 and is low again by day 28.\n\nExplain what happens in the woman's body when the progesterone level falls at the end of the cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Progesterone maintains the lining of the uterus (1)\n• So when it falls, the lining breaks down / is shed / menstruation begins (1)\n• FSH (and LH) are no longer inhibited, so FSH rises and a new egg begins to mature / the next cycle starts (1)` },
      { q: `A couple have been unable to have a baby for several years. They decide to have IVF treatment.\n\nDescribe the main stages of IVF treatment.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• The woman is given (injections of) FSH and LH / fertility hormones so that several eggs mature (1)\n• Eggs are collected from the ovaries (1)\n• Eggs are fertilised with sperm (from the man) in a lab / outside the body (1)\n• The fertilised eggs develop into embryos (in the lab) (1)\n• One or two embryos are placed (inserted) into the woman's uterus (1)\nIgnore: test tube baby` },
      { q: `A health clinic compared three methods of contraception. Each method was used by 1500 women for one year. The table shows how many of the women became pregnant.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nMethod | Number of women who became pregnant\ncontraceptive implant | 1\ncombined pill | 135\ncopper IUD | 6\n\nCalculate the percentage of women using the combined pill who became pregnant, and suggest why this method was less effective than the implant.\n\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 135 ÷ 1500 × 100 (1)\n• = 9 (%) (1)\n• The pill must be taken every day / women may forget to take a pill (or take it late, or vomit), whereas the implant works without the user doing anything (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student places a young plant on its side in a dark cupboard. After 24 hours the shoot has bent so that its tip is growing upwards.\n\nUse ideas about auxin to explain why the shoot bends upwards.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Auxin is made in the shoot tip (1)\n• Gravity causes auxin to collect on the lower side of the shoot (1)\n• In shoots, auxin stimulates cell elongation, so cells on the lower side grow longer / faster and the shoot bends up (1)\nAllow: negative gravitropism for credit only with an explanation\nDo not allow: auxin moves towards light / auxin causes cell division only` },
      { q: `Dwarf pea plants grow to a smaller height than normal pea plants. A scientist sprayed 20 dwarf pea plants with gibberellin solution and 20 dwarf pea plants with water. After four weeks she measured their heights.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nTreatment | Mean height after four weeks (cm)\nwater | 18.4\ngibberellin | 47.2\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean height caused by gibberellin. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Increase = 47.2 − 18.4 = 28.8 (cm) (1)\n• 28.8 ÷ 18.4 × 100 = 156.52… (1)\n• = 157 (%) to 3 significant figures (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 3 marks\nAllow: 156.5 for 2 marks (not 3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `A gardener grows tomato seedlings on a windowsill. Light only comes from the window, on one side. After a few days the shoots bend towards the window.\n\nExplain how auxin causes the shoots to bend towards the light.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Auxin (made in the tip) moves to the shaded side of the shoot (1)\n• Auxin causes cells to elongate (more) on the shaded side (1)\n• So the shaded side grows faster / longer than the lit side and the shoot bends towards the light (1)\nDo not allow: auxin moves towards the light / auxin is destroyed by light (for the first mark)` },
      { q: `Withdrawing your hand from a hot plate is controlled by the nervous system. The changes in the uterus during the menstrual cycle are controlled by hormones.\n\nExplain why hormones are more suitable than nerves for controlling the menstrual cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• The menstrual cycle involves changes over a long period of time / about 28 days (1)\n• Hormonal effects are slower but longer lasting (whereas nerve impulses are fast and short-lived) (1)\n• Hormones are carried in the blood so can reach several target organs at once / e.g. ovaries and uterus (1)` },
      { q: `Four hormones control the menstrual cycle: FSH, LH, oestrogen and progesterone.\n\nComplete the table to show where each hormone is released and one of its roles.\n\nHormone | Released by | One role\nFSH | pituitary gland | ______\nLH | ______ | ______\noestrogen | ovaries | ______\nprogesterone | ovaries | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• FSH: causes an egg to mature (in a follicle) / stimulates ovaries to release oestrogen (1)\n• LH: pituitary gland AND triggers / stimulates ovulation (release of an egg) (1)\n• Oestrogen: causes the uterus lining to thicken / inhibits FSH / stimulates LH (1)\n• Progesterone: maintains the uterus lining / inhibits FSH and LH (1)\nBoth cells needed for the LH mark.` },
      { q: `A woman is choosing a method of contraception. She is in a long-term relationship, does not want children for at least five years, and often forgets to take medicines. Her doctor suggests either a copper IUD or the combined contraceptive pill.\n\nThe copper IUD is a small T-shaped device placed in the uterus by a doctor. It can stay in place for up to ten years. The combined pill contains oestrogen and progesterone and must be taken every day.\n\nEvaluate the two methods for this woman.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• Pill: contains hormones that inhibit FSH (and LH), so no eggs mature / no ovulation (1)\n• Pill: must be taken daily, so may fail if she forgets / less effective for her (1)\n• Pill: may cause side effects e.g. mood changes, headaches, raised blood pressure / blood clots (1)\n• IUD: works for many years without her having to remember anything (1)\n• IUD: non-hormonal, so no hormonal side effects (1)\n• IUD: must be fitted / removed by a doctor; may cause heavier periods / risk of infection when fitted (1)\n• Neither protects against STIs (1)\n• Conclusion: IUD more suitable as she forgets medicines and wants long-term contraception (1)\nTo gain 4 marks, a conclusion linked to her situation must be given.` },
      { q: `A gardener tests a rooting powder. She dips stem cuttings into rooting powders with different concentrations of auxin and counts the number of roots on each cutting after three weeks.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nConcentration of auxin (mg per g of powder) | Mean number of roots per cutting\n0 | 8\n0.1 | 14\n1.0 | 21\n10.0 | 5\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the mean number of roots when cuttings are treated with 1.0 mg per g of auxin compared with no auxin.\n\nDescribe what the results show about the effect of auxin concentration on root growth.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (21 − 8) ÷ 8 × 100 (1)\n• = 162.5 (%) (1)\nAllow: 163 / 162\n• As auxin concentration increases up to 1.0 mg per g, the number of roots increases (1)\n• At 10.0 mg per g the number of roots falls below that with no auxin / high concentrations inhibit root growth (1)\nAllow: optimum concentration is about 1.0 mg per g (from these data)` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the levels of FSH, LH, oestrogen and progesterone in the blood of a woman during one 28-day menstrual cycle.\n\n• FSH rises in the first few days, then falls.\n• Oestrogen rises steadily to a peak on day 12.\n• LH rises sharply to a peak on day 13.\n• Progesterone rises after day 14, peaks on day 21 and falls by day 28.\n\nThe combined contraceptive pill contains oestrogen and progesterone.\n\n* Explain how the four hormones interact to control the menstrual cycle, and explain how the combined pill uses these interactions to prevent pregnancy.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of how the hormones interact, including at least two feedback effects, AND a clear explanation of how the pill prevents ovulation. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes the roles of most hormones with at least one interaction, AND some explanation of how the pill works. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives some roles of hormones OR a simple statement of how the pill works. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nInteractions in the cycle\n• FSH (from the pituitary) causes an egg to mature in a follicle\n• FSH stimulates the ovaries to release oestrogen\n• Oestrogen makes the uterus lining thicken\n• Rising oestrogen inhibits FSH (explains FSH falling)\n• High oestrogen stimulates the release of LH (peak day 13)\n• LH triggers ovulation (about day 14)\n• Progesterone (from the ovary / corpus luteum) maintains the uterus lining\n• Progesterone inhibits FSH and LH\n• Progesterone falls at the end of the cycle, so the lining is shed and FSH is no longer inhibited\nThe pill\n• Oestrogen and progesterone in the pill keep their levels high\n• This inhibits FSH (and LH) release from the pituitary\n• So no egg matures and no ovulation occurs, so fertilisation cannot happen\n• Progesterone also thickens cervical mucus` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate how light shining from one side affects the growth of seedlings. She has:\n• dishes of cotton wool\n• cress seeds\n• cardboard boxes, some with a slit cut in one side\n• a lamp\n• a ruler and a protractor.\n\n* Describe how the student could carry out this investigation, and explain the results she would expect.\n\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how she would set up the investigation, including a control\n• the variables she should keep the same\n• the measurements she should take\n• the results she would expect and why, using ideas about auxin.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that includes a control and controlled variables and gives suitable measurements, AND a correct explanation of the expected results in terms of auxin. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method with some detail, including either a control or controlled variables, AND some description of expected results with some reference to auxin. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple method OR a simple statement of expected results. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• Germinate the same number of cress seeds on damp cotton wool in several dishes\n• Place one dish in a box with a slit facing the lamp (light from one side)\n• Control: a dish in a box with no slit (dark) / a dish lit evenly from above\n• Repeat with several dishes for each condition\nControlled variables\n• Same temperature, volume of water, number and age of seeds, distance and brightness of lamp, time\nMeasurements\n• After a few days, measure the angle of bending with a protractor\n• Measure shoot length with a ruler\n• Calculate means\nExpected results and explanation\n• Seedlings lit from one side bend towards the slit / light (positive phototropism)\n• Seedlings in the dark grow straight up and taller / pale\n• Auxin is made in the shoot tip and moves to the shaded side\n• Auxin causes cell elongation on the shaded side, so the shaded side grows faster and the shoot bends towards the light\n• This helps the plant absorb more light for photosynthesis` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.3': {
    name: 'Maintaining Internal Environments',
    questions: [
      { q: `A long-distance runner's body temperature rises during a race on a hot day. The body detects this change and responds to bring the temperature back to about 37 °C.\n\nWhich part of the brain contains the thermoregulatory centre?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `Hypothalamus ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW pituitary gland / medulla / cerebellum` },
      { q: `ADH is a hormone that helps to control the water content of the blood. It travels in the blood to the kidneys.\n\nName the gland that releases ADH.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Pituitary (gland) ✓\nIGNORE hypothalamus\nDO NOT ALLOW adrenal / thyroid` },
      { q: `Insulin and glucagon are hormones that control the concentration of glucose in the blood.\n\nWhich organ releases insulin?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nkidney / liver / pancreas / stomach`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `pancreas ✓\nIf more than one option is ringed, award 0` },
      { q: `Each human kidney contains about 1 000 000 nephrons (kidney tubules).\n\nGive the total number of nephrons in two kidneys in standard form.\nNumber of nephrons = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × 10⁶ ✓\nALLOW 2.0 × 10⁶\nDO NOT ALLOW 20 × 10⁵ (not standard form)` },
      { q: `A doctor compares the liquid filtered out of the blood into the kidney tubule (filtrate) with the urine of a healthy person (data written for this practice question).\n\nSubstance | In filtrate? | In urine?\nwater | yes | yes\nurea | yes | yes\nglucose | yes | no\nsalts | yes | yes\n\nWhich substance is completely reabsorbed back into the blood?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `Glucose ✓` },
      { q: `A scientist takes a sample of red blood cells and places them in a beaker of distilled (pure) water. After a few minutes she looks at the cells using a light microscope.\n\nWhat happens to the red blood cells?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `(Cells) swell and burst ✓\nALLOW lyse / haemolyse\nIGNORE get bigger on its own\nDO NOT ALLOW become turgid / become plasmolysed` },
      { q: `A group of hikers walks across a mountain in very cold, windy weather. One hiker gets lost and her body temperature falls to 33 °C.\n\nExplain why it is important for the body to maintain a constant temperature of about 37 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Enzymes work best / have an optimum at about 37 °C ✓\nAt lower temperatures reactions (e.g. respiration) become too slow / at higher temperatures enzymes denature ✓\nALLOW metabolism slows down / cells cannot function properly for second marking point\nIGNORE ‘to stay healthy’` },
      { q: `After a game of tennis on a hot day, a student notices that the skin on his face looks red.\n\nExplain why his skin looks red.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Vasodilation / arterioles (supplying the skin capillaries) widen ✓\nMore blood flows near the surface of the skin (so more heat is lost by radiation) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW capillaries widen / capillaries move nearer the surface\nIGNORE veins` },
      { q: `On a hot day a builder sweats a lot. His shirt becomes wet, but his skin feels cooler when a breeze blows.\n\nExplain how sweating helps to cool the body.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Water in sweat evaporates (from the skin) ✓\nEvaporation uses / transfers (heat) energy from the skin (so the skin cools) ✓\nIGNORE ‘sweat cools you down’ without evaporation\nDO NOT ALLOW sweat is cold` },
      { q: `A student weighs a runner before and after a one-hour run on a hot day. The runner does not drink during the run (data written for this practice question).\n\nMass before run = 70.0 kg\nMass after run = 68.6 kg\n\nCalculate the percentage of body mass lost during the run.\nPercentage of body mass lost = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mass lost = 70.0 − 68.6 = 1.4 kg ✓\n(1.4 ÷ 70.0) × 100 = 2(.0) % ✓\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nALLOW 1 mark for 98 %` },
      { q: `A swimmer climbs out of a cold lake and starts to shiver.\n\nExplain how shivering helps to raise body temperature.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Muscles contract (rapidly / automatically) ✓\nMore respiration (in muscle cells) releases (heat) energy ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW respiration produces / makes energy\nIGNORE friction` },
      { q: `The kidneys filter about 180 dm³ of liquid out of the blood each day. A healthy person produces about 1.8 dm³ of urine each day.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the filtered liquid that is reabsorbed back into the blood.\nPercentage reabsorbed = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Volume reabsorbed = 180 − 1.8 = 178.2 (dm³) ✓\n(178.2 ÷ 180) × 100 = 99 % ✓\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nALLOW 1 mark for 1 % (percentage not reabsorbed)` },
      { q: `A student measures her blood glucose concentration before and after eating a breakfast cereal (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe concentration rises from 4.8 mmol/dm³ to 8.4 mmol/dm³ in the 30 minutes after eating.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of increase in blood glucose concentration.\nRate = ______ mmol/dm³ per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Increase = 8.4 − 4.8 = 3.6 (mmol/dm³) ✓\n3.6 ÷ 30 = 0.12 (mmol/dm³ per minute) ✓\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A person has not eaten for 12 hours. Their blood glucose concentration starts to fall below normal, so the pancreas releases more glucagon.\n\nDescribe how glucagon causes blood glucose concentration to return to normal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Glucagon travels (in the blood) to the liver ✓\n(Liver) converts / breaks down glycogen into glucose (which is released into the blood) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW glucagon is converted into glucose\nDO NOT ALLOW confusion of glucagon and glycogen` },
      { q: `Two patients have diabetes. Patient A was diagnosed at the age of 9. Patient B was diagnosed at the age of 58 and is overweight.\n\nComplete the table to show one difference in the cause of each type of diabetes.\nType 1 (Patient A): ______\nType 2 (Patient B): ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Type 1: pancreas does not produce (enough) insulin / insulin-secreting cells destroyed ✓\nType 2: (body) cells no longer respond to insulin / insulin resistance ✓\nALLOW pancreas produces too little insulin for type 2\nIGNORE references to age or weight` },
      { q: `A doctor diagnoses a patient with type 2 diabetes. The patient has a BMI of 33 and rarely exercises.\n\nSuggest two changes to the patient's lifestyle that the doctor might recommend.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nEat a diet low in sugar / carbohydrate / fat ✓\nEat a balanced diet / reduce portion sizes / lose weight ✓\nTake more (regular) exercise ✓\nIGNORE ‘eat healthily’ unqualified\nIGNORE take medication / inject insulin (not lifestyle changes)` },
      { q: `People with type 1 diabetes must inject insulin. Insulin is a protein.\n\nSuggest why insulin cannot be taken as a tablet that is swallowed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Insulin would be digested / broken down (in the stomach / small intestine) ✓\nBy protease (enzymes) / into amino acids ✓\nALLOW so would not reach the blood (in its active form) as an alternative to the first point` },
      { q: `A scientist places red blood cells in a concentrated salt solution. After 10 minutes the cells look smaller and wrinkled.\n\nExplain why the red blood cells change shape.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Water moves out of the cells by osmosis ✓\nFrom a dilute solution / higher water potential (inside the cell) to a more concentrated solution / lower water potential (outside) ✓\nALLOW across a partially permeable membrane\nDO NOT ALLOW salt moves into / out of the cells` },
      { q: `Blood is filtered under high pressure in the kidney. The filtrate collected from the start of a healthy kidney tubule contains water, glucose, salts and urea but no protein or red blood cells.\n\nExplain why the filtrate contains no protein or red blood cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Proteins / red blood cells are too large ✓\nTo pass through (the filter / walls of the capillaries / tubule) into the tubule ✓\nIGNORE ‘they are needed by the body’` },
      { q: `A patient with untreated type 1 diabetes has a urine test. The test shows glucose in the patient's urine. A healthy person has no glucose in their urine.\n\nExplain why there is glucose in the patient's urine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Blood glucose concentration is (very) high ✓\n(So) not all the glucose in the filtrate can be reabsorbed (into the blood) ✓\nALLOW too much glucose is filtered for the kidney to reabsorb it all` },
      { q: `A soldier carries out a training march in a desert. She sweats a lot and the water content of her blood falls. Her pituitary gland releases more ADH.\n\nDescribe the effect of ADH on the kidney tubules.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(ADH) makes the (collecting duct / tubule) more permeable to water ✓\nSo more water is reabsorbed into the blood ✓\nALLOW less water lost in urine / urine more concentrated for second point\nDO NOT ALLOW ADH absorbs water` },
      { q: `A student records the volume of urine he produces on two days (data written for this practice question). He drinks the same volume of water on both days.\n\nDay | Air temperature (°C) | Volume of urine (dm³)\nMonday | 14 | 1.9\nTuesday | 31 | 1.1\n\nSuggest why he produced less urine on Tuesday.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `More water lost by sweating (on Tuesday) ✓\n(So) more ADH released / more water reabsorbed by the kidneys ✓\nIGNORE ‘he was dehydrated’ unqualified` },
      { q: `A patient with kidney failure has dialysis three times a week. During dialysis, the patient's blood flows past a partially permeable membrane. Dialysis fluid flows on the other side of the membrane. The dialysis fluid contains the same concentration of glucose as normal blood but no urea.\n\nExplain why the dialysis fluid contains glucose but no urea.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `No urea so that urea diffuses out of the blood (into the fluid) / urea moves down its concentration gradient ✓\nSame glucose concentration so there is no net diffusion / no glucose is lost from the blood ✓` },
      { q: `A patient with kidney failure is offered a kidney transplant instead of dialysis. The donor kidney must have a close tissue match with the patient.\n\nGive two disadvantages of a kidney transplant compared with dialysis.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nRisk of rejection (by the immune system) ✓\nMust take immunosuppressant drugs (for life) ✓\nIncreased risk of infection (due to immunosuppressants) ✓\nShortage of donors / long waiting time ✓\nRisks of surgery ✓\nIGNORE cost` },
      { q: `When blood glucose concentration rises, the pancreas releases insulin. As the blood glucose concentration falls back to normal, less insulin is released. This is an example of negative feedback.\n\nExplain what is meant by negative feedback.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `A change (from the normal / set level) is detected ✓\nThe response reverses / opposes the change (returning conditions to normal) ✓\nALLOW correct description using the blood glucose example` },
      { q: `The body's temperature is monitored by temperature receptors. Information from these receptors is sent to the thermoregulatory centre.\n\nGive two places in the body where temperature receptors are found.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Skin ✓\nHypothalamus / brain (detects temperature of blood) ✓\nIGNORE thermometer / blood vessels` },
      { q: `A student eats a large meal containing a lot of carbohydrate. One hour later her blood glucose concentration has risen, then it slowly returns to normal.\n\nDescribe how insulin causes the blood glucose concentration to return to normal.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\nPancreas detects the rise (in blood glucose) ✓\n(And) releases (more) insulin into the blood ✓\nInsulin causes (body / muscle / liver) cells to take up glucose (from the blood) ✓\nInsulin causes the liver / muscles to convert glucose into glycogen (for storage) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW insulin converts glucose into glycogen (insulin is not an enzyme)\nDO NOT ALLOW glucagon for glycogen` },
      { q: `A student writes this statement in her revision notes:\n‘When we are cold, the capillaries move further away from the skin surface so we lose less heat.’\n\nThe statement is incorrect. Explain how blood flow in the skin is changed to reduce heat loss.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Vasoconstriction / arterioles (supplying skin capillaries) narrow ✓\n(Because) muscles in the arteriole walls contract ✓\nLess blood flows through the capillaries near the skin surface (so less heat is lost by radiation) ✓\nALLOW blood is diverted through shunt vessels / away from the skin surface\nDO NOT ALLOW capillaries constrict / move` },
      { q: `A hospital compares the cost of treating kidney failure by dialysis and by kidney transplant (data written for this practice question).\n\n• Dialysis costs £30 000 per year.\n• A transplant operation costs £17 000, then drugs to prevent rejection cost £5 000 per year.\n\nCalculate how much money the hospital saves over 5 years by giving one patient a transplant rather than dialysis.\nMoney saved = £ ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Dialysis = 5 × 30 000 = £150 000 ✓\nTransplant = 17 000 + (5 × 5 000) = £42 000 ✓\nSaving = 150 000 − 42 000 = £108 000 ✓\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working\nECF from incorrect totals` },
      { q: `A student drinks 1 dm³ of water. On a different day he drinks 1 dm³ of a salty sports drink. He measures the volume of urine he produces in the 3 hours after each drink (data written for this practice question).\n\nDrink | Volume of urine in 3 hours (cm³)\nwater | 850\nsalty sports drink | 240\n\nExplain the difference in the volume of urine produced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Water makes the blood (more) dilute / the salty drink keeps the blood (more) concentrated ✓\nLess ADH is released after drinking water / more ADH after the salty drink ✓\nSo (kidney tubules are) less permeable / less water is reabsorbed after water (so more urine) ✓\nALLOW reverse arguments for the salty drink` },
      { q: `A person has been working outside in the sun without drinking for several hours.\n\nComplete the sentences to explain how the body responds.\nThe ______ in the brain detects that the water content of the blood is too low.\nThe pituitary gland releases ______ ADH into the blood.\nThe kidneys produce a ______ volume of concentrated urine.`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `hypothalamus ✓\nmore ✓\nsmall / low ✓\nALLOW ‘receptors’ for first gap only if qualified as in the hypothalamus` },
      { q: `The table shows the concentrations of three substances at different places in a healthy kidney (data written for this practice question).\n\nSubstance | Blood plasma entering kidney (g/dm³) | Filtrate at start of tubule (g/dm³) | Urine (g/dm³)\nprotein | 75 | 0 | 0\nglucose | 1.0 | 1.0 | 0\nurea | 0.3 | 0.3 | 20.0\n\nExplain the results for glucose and urea.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Glucose: (small enough so) filtered into the tubule (so same concentration as plasma) ✓\nGlucose: all reabsorbed (into the blood) so none in urine ✓\nUrea: concentration higher in urine because water is reabsorbed / urea is not reabsorbed ✓\nALLOW selective reabsorption for second point` },
      { q: `Doctors give a person with type 2 diabetes and a person without diabetes a drink containing 75 g of glucose. They measure blood glucose concentration over 2 hours.\n\nThe graph is described here: for the person without diabetes, glucose rises from 4.5 mmol/dm³ to a peak of 7.5 mmol/dm³ at 30 minutes and falls back to 5.0 mmol/dm³ by 2 hours. For the person with type 2 diabetes, glucose rises from 7.0 mmol/dm³ to a peak of 13.0 mmol/dm³ at 60 minutes and is still 11.5 mmol/dm³ at 2 hours (data written for this practice question).\n\nCompare the results and explain the difference.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Person with diabetes has a higher starting / peak concentration ✓\nPeak is later / concentration falls more slowly / does not return to starting level ✓\nBecause their body cells do not respond to insulin (so glucose is not taken up from the blood) ✓\nALLOW correct use of figures for comparison marks\nDO NOT ALLOW ‘does not make insulin’ for type 2` },
      { q: `During a marathon, a runner drinks very large volumes of plain water. Her blood becomes much more dilute than normal and she develops a headache because her brain cells swell.\n\nExplain why her brain cells swell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Blood / tissue fluid is more dilute than the cytoplasm / has a higher water potential than the cells ✓\nWater moves into the (brain) cells ✓\nBy osmosis (across the partially permeable membrane) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW salt moves out of cells as the explanation` },
      { q: `In a country with a population of 64 million, 3.0 million people had diabetes in 2010. By 2024 the population had risen to 67 million and 4.4 million people had diabetes (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage of the population with diabetes in 2024, and suggest one reason why the number of people with type 2 diabetes is increasing.\nPercentage = ______ %\nReason: ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(4.4 ÷ 67) × 100 ✓\n= 6.6 % ✓\nALLOW 6.57 %\nReason: more people are overweight / obese / less active / eat more sugary food ✓\nIGNORE ageing population unless linked to higher risk\nALLOW better diagnosis / screening` },
      { q: `A man falls into the sea in winter. The water temperature is 8 °C. He is rescued after 20 minutes.\n\nDescribe and explain how his body responds to try to keep his body temperature at 37 °C.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any four from:\nTemperature receptors (in skin) / hypothalamus detect the fall ✓\nThermoregulatory centre / hypothalamus sends (nerve) impulses to effectors ✓\nShivering: muscles contract, more respiration releases heat ✓\nVasoconstriction: arterioles to the skin narrow ✓\nSo less blood near the skin surface / less heat lost (by radiation) ✓\nLess / no sweat produced so less heat lost by evaporation ✓\nIGNORE hairs stand up (little effect in water)` },
      { q: `A kidney tubule (nephron) is described in words. Blood enters a knot of capillaries (glomerulus) surrounded by a cup-shaped structure (Bowman's capsule) at region A. The tubule then forms a coiled section (region B), a loop (region C), and a collecting duct (region D) that carries liquid to the ureter.\n\nDescribe what happens to the liquid in regions A, B and D.\nA: ______\nB: ______\nD: ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `A: (ultra)filtration of blood under high pressure ✓\nA: small molecules (water, glucose, urea, salts) pass into the tubule / large molecules (proteins) and cells stay in the blood ✓\nB: (selective) reabsorption of all glucose / useful substances (into the blood) ✓\nD: water reabsorbed (into the blood) / amount controlled by ADH ✓\nALLOW salts / water reabsorbed in B` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentrations of glucose, insulin and glucagon in the blood of a cyclist during a 90-minute ride without food (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime (min) | Blood glucose (mmol/dm³) | Insulin (arbitrary units) | Glucagon (arbitrary units)\n0 | 5.2 | 10 | 20\n30 | 4.6 | 6 | 45\n60 | 4.4 | 4 | 70\n90 | 4.4 | 3 | 85\n\nDescribe and explain the changes in the concentrations of insulin and glucagon.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Insulin decreases and glucagon increases ✓\nGlucose used in respiration by (contracting) muscles (so blood glucose falls) ✓\nLess insulin so less glucose taken up by cells / less converted to glycogen ✓\nMore glucagon so liver converts (more) glycogen into glucose (released into the blood) ✓\nSo blood glucose concentration is kept (nearly) steady from 60 minutes ✓\nMax 4 marks\nALLOW use of data to support the description` },
      { q: `A diabetes clinic shows patients this information (data written for this practice question).\n\nFeature | Type 1 diabetes | Type 2 diabetes\nUsual age of onset | childhood / teenage years | usually over 40\nPercentage of UK cases | 8 % | 90 %\nLinked to obesity? | no | yes\n\n*Compare type 1 and type 2 diabetes.\nIn your answer, explain the causes of each type and how each type can be treated.`, marks: 6, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Clear comparison of the causes of both types AND explains treatments for both, linked to the effect on blood glucose.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes the cause of both types AND gives a treatment for at least one type, OR describes the cause of one type and treatments for both.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a cause OR a treatment for one type of diabetes.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nCauses\n• Type 1: pancreas does not produce (enough) insulin\n• Type 1: insulin-secreting cells destroyed (by the immune system)\n• Type 2: body cells no longer respond to insulin / insulin resistance\n• Type 2: risk increased by obesity / poor diet / lack of exercise\n• Both lead to high blood glucose concentration after meals\nTreatments\n• Type 1: regular insulin injections / insulin pump\n• Type 1: monitor blood glucose and match insulin dose to diet and activity\n• Type 1: insulin causes cells to take up glucose / liver to store glycogen\n• Type 2: controlled diet (low sugar / carbohydrate), exercise, weight loss\n• Type 2: medication that increases cells' response to insulin / reduces glucose release from the liver\n• Type 2: may need insulin later\nUse of data\n• Type 2 much more common (90 % vs 8 %); type 1 starts younger; only type 2 linked to obesity` },
      { q: `A cyclist takes part in a long race on a hot day. During the first half of the race she sweats heavily and drinks nothing. In the second half she drinks a very large volume of plain water.\n\n*Explain how her body controls the water content of her blood during the race, and the effects on her body cells if this control did not happen.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains the responses to BOTH dehydration AND too much water, including the role of the hypothalamus, ADH and the kidney tubule, AND explains effects on cells using osmosis.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains the response to ONE challenge including ADH and the kidney, AND gives some effect on cells, OR describes responses to both challenges without explanation.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes a change in urine volume OR mentions ADH OR states an effect on cells.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nFirst half (dehydration)\n• Water lost in sweat; blood becomes more concentrated\n• Detected by receptors in the hypothalamus\n• Pituitary gland releases more ADH\n• ADH makes collecting ducts more permeable to water\n• More water reabsorbed into the blood; small volume of concentrated urine\nSecond half (too much water)\n• Blood becomes more dilute; less ADH released\n• Collecting ducts less permeable; less water reabsorbed\n• Large volume of dilute urine\n• Negative feedback returns water content to normal\nEffects on cells\n• Concentrated blood: water leaves cells by osmosis; cells shrink / crenate\n• Dilute blood: water enters cells by osmosis; cells swell / may burst\n• Cells cannot function properly / enzymes / reactions affected` }
    ]
  },
  'B4.1': {
    name: 'Ecosystems',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student surveys a rocky shore. She counts all the limpets, barnacles, mussels and seaweeds living there. She also measures the temperature and salt concentration of the rock pools.\n\nComplete the sentence.\nAll the limpets living on the rocky shore make up a ______ .`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• population (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW community / species / ecosystem` },
      { q: `Clover plants have swellings on their roots called root nodules. Bacteria live inside the nodules. The bacteria give the clover nitrogen compounds and the clover gives the bacteria sugars.\n\nWhich term describes the relationship between the clover and the bacteria?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\ncompetition / mutualism / parasitism / predation`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• mutualism (1)\nALLOW mutualistic\nMore than one option ringed = 0` },
      { q: `This is a food chain from a meadow.\ngrass → grasshopper → shrew → barn owl\n\nWhich trophic level is the shrew at?`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• trophic level 3 (1)\nALLOW third (trophic level) / secondary consumer\nIGNORE carnivore / predator on its own` },
      { q: `Decomposers are important in all ecosystems. Scientists estimate that there are about 4 000 000 000 bacteria in 1 g of fertile soil (data written for this practice question).\n\nGive this number in standard form.\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• 4 × 10⁹ (1)\nALLOW 4.0 × 10⁹\nDO NOT ALLOW 40 × 10⁸` },
      { q: `A student uses capture–recapture to estimate the number of banded snails in a garden. He marks the shell of each captured snail with a small dot of paint before releasing it.\n\nSuggest why the paint mark must not make the snails easier for thrushes to see.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• (otherwise) marked snails would be more likely to be eaten so fewer marked snails would be recaptured / estimate would be inaccurate (1)\nALLOW marked and unmarked snails must have the same chance of survival` },
      { q: `In a rainforest, a large tree can release hundreds of litres of water vapour into the air each day. The water vapour comes out through the stomata in its leaves.\n\nName the process that releases water vapour from leaves.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• transpiration (1)\nALLOW evaporation (from leaves)\nIGNORE respiration` },
      { q: `A student is writing notes about levels of organisation in a pond ecosystem.\n\nPut these terms in order from the smallest level to the largest level.\ncommunity   ecosystem   individual organism   population\n\nsmallest ______ → ______ → ______ → ______ largest`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• individual organism → population → community → ecosystem\nAll four in the correct order (2)\nTwo terms in the correct positions (1)` },
      { q: `A group of students list factors that might affect the number of woodlice under logs in a wood.\n\nsoil pH   number of shrews   light intensity   disease   temperature   amount of dead leaves to eat\n\nComplete the table by writing each factor in the correct column.\nAbiotic factors: ______\nBiotic factors: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• abiotic: soil pH, light intensity, temperature (1)\n• biotic: number of shrews, disease, amount of dead leaves to eat (1)\nAll six correct = 2; four or five correct = 1` },
      { q: `A student counts the number of buttercup plants in eight 0.25 m² quadrats placed randomly in a field.\n\nNumber of buttercups in each quadrat: 3, 7, 0, 5, 4, 6, 2, 5\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean number of buttercups per quadrat.\nMean = ______ buttercups per quadrat`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 3 + 7 + 0 + 5 + 4 + 6 + 2 + 5 = 32 (1)\n• 32 ÷ 8 = 4 (1)\nALLOW 4.0\nCorrect answer with no working = 2\nALLOW 1 mark for 32 ÷ 7 = 4.6 (zero quadrat ignored)` },
      { q: `Students use capture–recapture to estimate the population of woodlice in a school garden.\n• On day 1 they catch 40 woodlice, mark them with a dot of non-toxic paint and release them.\n• On day 2 they catch 35 woodlice. 7 of these have a paint mark.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nEstimate the population of woodlice in the garden.\nUse the equation:\npopulation = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample\n\nPopulation = ______ woodlice`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (40 × 35) ÷ 7 (1)\n• = 200 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2\nALLOW 1 mark for 1400 seen` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the dry biomass at two trophic levels in a field.\n• producers (grass): 5000 g per m²\n• primary consumers (insects and rabbits): 450 g per m²\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the efficiency of biomass transfer from the producers to the primary consumers.\nUse the equation:\nefficiency = (biomass transferred to the next level ÷ biomass available at the previous level) × 100\n\nEfficiency = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 450 ÷ 5000 (× 100) (1)\n• = 9 (%) (1)\nALLOW 9.0\nCorrect answer with no working = 2\nALLOW 1 mark for 0.09` },
      { q: `A farmer feeds grass to beef cattle. Only a small percentage of the biomass in the grass becomes biomass in the cattle.\n\nGive two reasons why not all the biomass in the grass becomes biomass in the cattle.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• not all of the grass is eaten / roots are not eaten (1)\n• some of the grass is not digested / lost in faeces / egestion (1)\n• some is used / lost in respiration (as carbon dioxide / water) (1)\n• some is lost in urine / urea / excretion (1)\nIGNORE energy lost as heat on its own\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is used to make biomass` },
      { q: `A gardener makes a compost heap from grass cuttings and vegetable peelings. Every two weeks she turns the heap over with a fork to mix it.\n\nSuggest two reasons why turning the compost heap speeds up decay.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• adds (more) oxygen / air (1)\n• so decomposers can respire aerobically / respire faster (1)\n• mixes decomposers / microorganisms through the material / brings them into contact with more material (1)\n• moves cooler outside material to the warm centre / distributes heat (1)\nALLOW bacteria / fungi for decomposers` },
      { q: `A farmer notices that wheat grows poorly in one corner of a field. The soil in that corner is waterlogged for most of the year. Tests show the soil has very low levels of nitrates.\n\nExplain why the waterlogged soil has low levels of nitrates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• waterlogged soil has little / no oxygen / is anaerobic (1)\n• (so) denitrifying bacteria (thrive and) convert nitrates into nitrogen gas (1)\nALLOW nitrifying bacteria cannot work / cannot convert ammonium to nitrates (in the absence of oxygen) for the second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrates to nitrogen` },
      { q: `Some farmers grow clover in a field one year and then plough it into the soil before planting wheat the following year. They find they need to add less fertiliser to the wheat.\n\nExplain why growing clover means less fertiliser is needed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• clover has nitrogen-fixing bacteria (in root nodules) which convert nitrogen (gas) from the air into nitrogen compounds / ammonium / nitrates (1)\n• when the clover is ploughed in / decomposes, nitrates are released into the soil (for the wheat) (1)\nALLOW clover is a legume for nitrogen-fixing idea only if bacteria mentioned` },
      { q: `Large areas of tropical rainforest are cleared each year by cutting down and burning the trees.\n\nExplain two ways this increases the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• burning / combustion of the wood releases carbon dioxide (1)\n• fewer trees so less carbon dioxide is removed by photosynthesis (1)\nALLOW decomposition / microorganisms respiring as they decay remaining wood / roots releases carbon dioxide (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `Red squirrels are native to the UK. Grey squirrels were introduced from North America in the 1870s. In most of England, grey squirrels have replaced red squirrels. Both species live in woodland and eat nuts and seeds.\n\nSuggest two resources that red and grey squirrels compete for.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• food / nuts / seeds (1)\n• space / territory / nesting sites / shelter / trees (1)\n• mates (1) — only if clearly within a species\n• water (1)\nIGNORE light / minerals` },
      { q: `Scientists recorded the numbers of snowshoe hares and lynx in a forest in Canada over many years. Lynx eat hares. The graph showed that both populations rose and fell in cycles. Each peak in lynx numbers came one or two years after a peak in hare numbers.\n\nExplain why the peak in lynx numbers comes after the peak in hare numbers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• when there are more hares there is more food for the lynx (1)\n• it takes time for the lynx to breed / for young lynx to be born and survive (so lynx numbers rise later) (1)\nALLOW lynx numbers fall later because they run out of food after hare numbers have fallen` },
      { q: `This is a food chain from a woodland.\noak tree → caterpillars → blue tits\nOne oak tree can support thousands of caterpillars.\n\nExplain why a pyramid of biomass for this food chain is pyramid-shaped but a pyramid of numbers is not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is only one oak tree but many caterpillars, so the producer bar is narrower than the caterpillar bar in a pyramid of numbers (1)\n• the oak tree has a very large biomass / biomass decreases at each trophic level, so the producer bar is widest in a pyramid of biomass (1)\nALLOW the pyramid of numbers is inverted at the bottom / does not take size of organisms into account` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out how the numbers of limpets change from the top of a beach (high-tide mark) to the bottom (low-tide mark).\n\nExplain why the student should use a transect instead of placing quadrats randomly across the beach.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a transect lets her sample at regular intervals across / along a gradient / from high to low tide (1)\n• so she can see how numbers change with distance / with an abiotic factor such as time covered by water (1)\nALLOW random quadrats would not show a pattern / change across the beach` },
      { q: `A student wants to estimate the number of daisies on a school field. He decides to throw a quadrat over his shoulder to choose where to place it.\n\nHis teacher says he should place the quadrats randomly in a different way.\nDescribe how he could place the quadrats randomly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• lay out two tape measures at right angles / mark out a grid across the field (1)\n• use random numbers (from a calculator / table / app) as coordinates to position each quadrat (1)\nIGNORE throwing the quadrat` },
      { q: `A student puts 50.0 g of fresh grass cuttings into a mesh bag and buries it in soil. After 4 weeks she digs up the bag, dries the outside and finds the mass of grass left is 41.5 g.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in mass of the grass cuttings.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 50.0 − 41.5 = 8.5 (g) (1)\n• 8.5 ÷ 50.0 × 100 = 17 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2\nALLOW 1 mark for 41.5 ÷ 50.0 × 100 = 83 (%)` },
      { q: `Water from a lake can fall again as rain on hills many kilometres away.\n\nDescribe how water from the lake ends up falling as rain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (heat from the Sun causes) water to evaporate from the lake / forms water vapour (1)\n• water vapour rises, cools and condenses to form clouds, which fall as rain / precipitation (1)\nALLOW evaporation and condensation named in correct sequence for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A food chain in the sea is:\nphytoplankton → zooplankton → herring → tuna → killer whale\nFood chains like this rarely have more than five trophic levels.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• biomass / energy is lost at each trophic level (e.g. respiration, egestion, excretion) (1)\n• so there is not enough biomass / energy left to support another trophic level (1)\nALLOW only about 10% is transferred to the next level` },
      { q: `In some areas of China, pesticides have killed most of the bees. Farmers now have to pollinate their pear trees by hand. Birds in the area eat pear seeds and insects.\n\nSuggest two effects on the ecosystem if farmers stopped hand pollinating the pear trees.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• fewer pears / fruits / seeds produced (1)\n• fewer new pear trees grow (1)\n• less food for seed-eating birds so bird numbers decrease (1)\n• birds eat more insects so insect numbers decrease (1)\n• birds compete more with other animals for food (1)` },
      { q: `In autumn, leaves fall from the trees in a woodland. By the next summer, most of the leaves on the woodland floor have disappeared.\n\nExplain how carbon in the fallen leaves is returned to the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• decomposers / bacteria / fungi / microorganisms feed on / break down the leaves (1)\n• they respire, releasing carbon dioxide (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW leaves release carbon dioxide by photosynthesis` },
      { q: `A student estimates the population of buttercups in a field. The field is 60 m long and 40 m wide. She uses 0.25 m² quadrats and finds a mean of 3.2 buttercups per quadrat.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nEstimate the total number of buttercups in the field.\nNumber of buttercups = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• area of field = 60 × 40 = 2400 (m²) (1)\n• number per m² = 3.2 ÷ 0.25 = 12.8 / number of quadrats that fit = 2400 ÷ 0.25 = 9600 (1)\n• 12.8 × 2400 = 30 720 / 3.2 × 9600 = 30 720 (1)\nALLOW 31 000 / 30 700\nCorrect answer with no working = 3\nECF from incorrect area` },
      { q: `Students measured the dry biomass of organisms in a pond.\n• algae: 820 g per m²\n• water fleas: 96 g per m²\n• sticklebacks: 11 g per m²\n• perch: 1.5 g per m²\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDraw a pyramid of biomass for this pond. Label each bar with the name of the organism.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• four horizontal stacked bars with algae at the bottom and perch at the top (1)\n• bars correctly labelled with the organisms (1)\n• bar widths decrease upwards and are roughly proportional to the biomass / a stated scale used (1)\nALLOW a scale where the top bars are very thin but visible\nDO NOT ALLOW a pyramid drawn as a triangle or a food chain with arrows` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of water on the decay of leaves. She puts 20 g of dead leaves into each of three mesh bags. She buries one bag in dry soil, one in moist soil and one in waterlogged soil. After 8 weeks she measures the percentage decrease in mass of the leaves.\n\ndry soil: 8%   moist soil: 41%   waterlogged soil: 12%\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDescribe and explain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• most decay / greatest mass loss in moist soil and least in dry soil (1)\n• in dry soil there is too little water for decomposers to grow / reproduce / for enzymes / reactions (1)\n• in waterlogged soil there is little oxygen so decomposers cannot respire aerobically / respire more slowly (1)\nALLOW moist soil has both water and oxygen for the decomposers for the third mark if neither of the last two scored\nALLOW bacteria / fungi / microorganisms for decomposers` },
      { q: `Students use capture–recapture to estimate the number of banded snails in a garden.\n• On the first day they catch 60 snails, mark each shell with a dot of paint and release them.\n• Two days later they catch 48 snails. 12 of these have a paint mark.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nIt rained heavily between the two samples and some paint washed off the shells.\nCalculate the estimate of the population and explain how the rain is likely to have affected this estimate.\nUse the equation:\npopulation = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (60 × 48) ÷ 12 (1)\n• = 240 (snails) (1)\n• estimate is too high / an overestimate because fewer marked snails were recognised in the second sample (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks for calculation\nDO NOT ALLOW 'inaccurate' on its own for the third mark` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the dry biomass at three trophic levels in a grassland.\n• producers: 8200 g per m²\n• primary consumers: 740 g per m²\n• secondary consumers: 52 g per m²\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nA student says the efficiency of biomass transfer from primary consumers to secondary consumers is lower than the efficiency of transfer from producers to primary consumers.\nUse calculations to show whether the student is correct.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• producers to primary consumers: 740 ÷ 8200 × 100 = 9.0 (%) (1)\n• primary to secondary consumers: 52 ÷ 740 × 100 = 7.0 (%) (1)\n• student is correct, because 7.0% is less than 9.0% (1)\nALLOW answers between 9.0 and 9.02 and between 7.0 and 7.03\nThe conclusion mark requires both calculations to be attempted\nECF for the conclusion from incorrect calculated values` },
      { q: `A student uses a transect to study grass growing from the edge of a woodland into the wood. At each point she measures light intensity with a light meter and estimates the percentage cover of grass in a quadrat.\n\ndistance into wood (m): 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\nlight intensity (lux): 22 000, 15 000, 8000, 3500, 1200\npercentage cover of grass (%): 85, 60, 30, 10, 2\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDescribe and explain the pattern shown by these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• as distance into the wood increases, light intensity and percentage cover of grass both decrease (1)\n• (so) as light intensity decreases, grass cover decreases / positive correlation between light intensity and grass cover (1)\n• less light means less photosynthesis so grass grows less / makes less glucose (1)\nALLOW trees block light / shade the ground\nIGNORE grass needs light on its own for the third mark` },
      { q: `A dead sheep lies in a field. Its body contains nitrogen in proteins. Some months later, the nitrogen is in the leaves of the grass growing nearby.\n\nDescribe how the nitrogen in the sheep's proteins becomes available to the grass.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• decomposers / bacteria / fungi break down the proteins into ammonium (compounds) / ammonia (1)\n• nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium into nitrates / nitrites then nitrates (1)\n• nitrates are absorbed by the grass roots (by active transport) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW nitrogen-fixing bacteria for nitrifying bacteria\nDO NOT ALLOW grass absorbs nitrogen gas` },
      { q: `Peat bogs are found on cold, wet uplands in the UK. The ground is waterlogged and the soil is acidic. Dead moss that fell into one bog 5000 years ago has hardly decayed.\n\nExplain why dead plant material builds up in peat bogs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• waterlogged so little / no oxygen (1)\n• (so) decomposers cannot respire aerobically / few decomposers can survive (1)\n• low temperature so enzymes in decomposers work slowly (1)\n• acidic conditions denature / inhibit enzymes of decomposers / fewer microorganisms survive (1)\n• so the rate of decay is slower than the rate at which plant material is added (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `The white-clawed crayfish is the only crayfish native to UK rivers. The American signal crayfish was brought to the UK in the 1970s. Signal crayfish are larger, grow faster and carry a disease called crayfish plague, which does not harm them but kills white-clawed crayfish.\n\nExplain why the number of white-clawed crayfish has decreased in rivers where signal crayfish now live.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• signal crayfish compete with white-clawed crayfish for food / shelter / space (1)\n• signal crayfish are larger / grow faster so are better competitors / get more of the resources (1)\n• crayfish plague is passed to white-clawed crayfish and kills them (1)\nALLOW signal crayfish may eat white-clawed crayfish\nIGNORE references to evolution` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on the decay of grass cuttings. She puts 40.0 g of grass cuttings into each of four containers with the same mass of moist soil. She keeps each container at a different temperature for 6 weeks and then measures the mass of grass left.\n\ntemperature (°C): 10, 20, 30, 40\nmass of grass left after 6 weeks (g): 36.4, 31.2, 25.6, 30.8\npercentage decrease in mass (%): 9, 22, X, 23\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate value X. Then describe and explain the effect of temperature on the rate of decay.\nX = ______ %`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (40.0 − 25.6) ÷ 40.0 × 100 (1)\n• X = 36 (%) (1)\n• rate of decay increases from 10 °C to 30 °C (and then decreases at 40 °C) (1)\n• higher temperature, enzymes (in decomposers) work faster / more collisions / decomposers respire and reproduce faster; above 30 °C enzymes begin to denature (1)\nALLOW optimum (in this investigation) is about 30 °C\nCorrect value of X with no working = 2` },
      { q: `A carbon atom in a carbon dioxide molecule in the air can become part of the muscle of a fox. Later it can return to the air.\n\nDescribe how this happens.\nUse these organisms in your answer: grass, rabbit, fox, decomposers.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• grass takes in carbon dioxide and uses it in photosynthesis to make glucose / carbohydrates / proteins (1)\n• rabbit eats the grass and the carbon becomes part of the rabbit's tissues / biomass (after digestion) (1)\n• fox eats the rabbit and uses the carbon compounds to make muscle / protein (1)\n• when the fox dies (or in its waste), decomposers break down the body and release carbon dioxide by respiration (1)\nALLOW fox releases carbon dioxide by respiration as an alternative route to the air for the last mark only if decomposers named elsewhere` },
      { q: `Ecologists want to estimate the number of ground beetles in a meadow. They set pitfall traps overnight.\n• Night 1: they catch 84 beetles, mark each one with a small dot of correction fluid under the wing case and release them.\n• Night 3: they catch 90 beetles. 14 of these are marked.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(i) Estimate the population of ground beetles in the meadow.\nUse the equation:\npopulation = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample\n(ii) Give two assumptions the ecologists must make for this estimate to be valid.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i)\n• (84 × 90) ÷ 14 (1)\n• = 540 (beetles) (1)\n(ii) Any two from:\n• no / few beetles are born, die, immigrate or emigrate between the two samples (1)\n• marked beetles mix randomly / evenly back into the population (1)\n• the mark does not affect survival / behaviour / chance of being caught (1)\n• marks do not rub off (1)\nCorrect answer for (i) with no working = 2` },
      { q: `* A student wants to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of bread.\nShe has: slices of bread from the same loaf, sealable plastic bags, a top-pan balance, incubators and a fridge, a water spray and a grid of 1 cm squares printed on clear plastic.\n\nDescribe a method she could use to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of bread.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how to change the temperature\n• the variables that need to be kept constant\n• the measurements that should be taken and how to process them.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that would give valid results, including how temperature is changed (at least three temperatures), at least two control variables, and a clear measurement with how the rate is found. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method including how temperature is changed and either control variables or a suitable measurement. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Some relevant steps, e.g. putting bread at different temperatures and seeing which goes mouldy first. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nChanging temperature\n• use the fridge (about 5 °C), room temperature and incubators at e.g. 25 °C, 30 °C and 35 °C\n• place one bag at each temperature; repeat with three bags at each temperature\nControl variables\n• same size / mass / type of bread from the same loaf\n• same amount of water sprayed onto each slice / same moisture\n• same time left exposed to the air before sealing / same amount of air in each bag\n• same length of time / same light conditions\nMeasurements and processing\n• each day count the number of 1 cm squares covered by mould using the clear grid\n• or measure the mass of the bread at the start and end and calculate percentage decrease in mass\n• calculate a mean for each temperature\n• rate = area of mould (or % mass loss) ÷ number of days\n• plot a graph of rate against temperature\nSafety\n• do not open the bags once mould has grown / dispose of sealed bags safely (spores may cause allergic reactions)` },
      { q: `* An ecologist uses a transect from the sea inland across a sand dune system. The table shows her results.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\ndistance from sea (m): 0, 50, 100, 150, 200\nwind speed (m/s): 9.0, 7.5, 4.0, 2.5, 1.5\nsoil water content (%): 2, 4, 9, 14, 18\nsoil organic matter (%): 0.2, 0.8, 3.5, 7.0, 12.0\nsoil pH: 8.5, 8.0, 7.0, 5.5, 4.5\nmain plants found: marram grass (0–50 m); grasses and wild flowers (100 m); heather and gorse (150–200 m)\n\nMarram grass has long roots, rolled leaves and grows quickly when sand covers it. Heather grows well in acidic soils.\n\nExplain how abiotic and biotic factors could cause the change in the plants found along the transect.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Uses data to describe the changes in at least three abiotic factors along the transect AND explains how both abiotic and biotic factors affect the distribution of the named plants. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes changes in some abiotic factors using the data AND gives some explanation of the distribution of plants. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes a simple trend in one or more factors OR states where plants are found. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nAbiotic factors (use of data)\n• wind speed falls from 9.0 to 1.5 m/s moving inland\n• soil water content rises from 2% to 18%\n• organic matter rises from 0.2% to 12.0%\n• pH falls from 8.5 (alkaline) to 4.5 (acidic)\nExplanation near the sea\n• few plants can survive strong wind, moving sand and dry soil with few minerals\n• marram grass is adapted: long roots reach water, rolled leaves reduce water loss, grows through sand\nExplanation further inland\n• when marram grass dies and decays it adds organic matter / humus / minerals and holds more water\n• so other grasses and flowers can grow (and they add more organic matter)\n• decay of plant material makes the soil more acidic\n• heather grows well in acidic, moist soil with more organic matter\nBiotic factors\n• inland, other plants out-compete marram grass for light / water / minerals\n• heather and gorse are taller and shade smaller plants\n• marram grass is a poor competitor so is only found where other plants cannot survive\n• animals (e.g. rabbits grazing) / decomposers in soil may also affect the plants` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.1': {
    name: 'Inheritance',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about cell division in humans.\n\nA human skin cell contains 46 chromosomes. Sperm cells are made in the testes.\n\nHow many chromosomes are in a human sperm cell?\n\nNumber of chromosomes = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'How many', markScheme: `23 (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 23 pairs` },
      { q: `In the 1850s and 1860s Gregor Mendel bred pea plants. When he crossed pure-breeding plants with round seeds with pure-breeding plants with wrinkled seeds, all of the offspring had round seeds.\n\nWhich word describes the allele for wrinkled seeds?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\ndominant / heterozygous / phenotype / recessive`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `recessive (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `The Human Genome Project was completed in 2003. Scientists worked out the order of bases in the DNA taken from human cells.\n\nWhat is meant by the genome of an organism?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `(all of) the genetic material / DNA of an organism (1)\nALLOW all the genes of an organism / the entire base sequence of its DNA\nIGNORE references to chromosomes alone` },
      { q: `The fruit fly, Drosophila, is often used in genetics research. A body cell of a fruit fly contains 8 chromosomes, arranged in 4 pairs. Its gametes contain 4 chromosomes.\n\nWhich term describes a cell, like the body cell, that contains two sets of chromosomes?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `diploid (1)\nIGNORE 2n\nDO NOT ALLOW haploid` },
      { q: `Mendel published the results of his pea plant experiments in 1866. His ideas were not accepted by most scientists until about 1900, sixteen years after his death.\n\nSuggest one reason why Mendel's ideas were not accepted during his lifetime.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any one from: (1)\nchromosomes / genes / DNA had not been discovered (so there was no mechanism to explain his 'factors')\nhe was a monk / not a well-known scientist / not part of the scientific community\nhis work was published in a little-known journal / few scientists read it\nother scientists did not understand the mathematics / statistics he used\nhis ideas did not fit the accepted idea of blending inheritance` },
      { q: `A couple have three children. All three children are girls.\n\nWhat is the probability that their next child will be a boy?\nGive your answer as a percentage.\n\nProbability = ______ %`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `50 (%) (1)\nALLOW 0.5 / ½ / 1 in 2 / 1:1\nIGNORE working` },
      { q: `In cats, the allele for short fur (L) is dominant to the allele for long fur (l).\n\nA cat has the genotype Ll.\n\nGive the phenotype of this cat and state whether it is homozygous or heterozygous.\n\nPhenotype ______\nHomozygous or heterozygous ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `short fur (1)\nheterozygous (1)\nIGNORE long-haired carrier\nDO NOT ALLOW Ll as the phenotype` },
      { q: `This question is about the words used in genetics.\n\nComplete the sentences using words from the list.\n\nallele   chromosome   gamete   gene   genotype   phenotype\n\nA ______ is a long molecule of DNA found in the nucleus.\nA section of DNA that codes for one protein is called a ______.\nA different version of a gene is called an ______.\nThe alleles an organism has for a characteristic are called its ______.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `chromosome; gene; allele; genotype\nall four correct (2)\ntwo or three correct (1)` },
      { q: `Identical twins come from the same fertilised egg, so they have the same alleles.\n\nTwo identical twin sisters were separated at birth and brought up in different countries. At age 30, one twin has a mass of 58 kg and the other has a mass of 71 kg.\n\nExplain why the twins have different masses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(twins have) the same genotype / DNA / genes (so the difference is not genetic) (1)\ndifference is caused by the environment, e.g. diet / exercise / lifestyle (1)\nALLOW phenotype is the result of genes interacting with the environment for 1 mark only if neither point is given` },
      { q: `Sex in humans is determined by one pair of chromosomes, the sex chromosomes.\n\nThe Punnett square shows a cross between a mother and a father. It is incomplete.\n\n            mother: X     mother: X\nfather: X     XX           ______\nfather: Y     ______       XY\n\nComplete the Punnett square and give the percentage chance that a child of this couple will be female.\n\nPercentage chance of a female child = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `XX and XY in the correct boxes (top right XX, bottom left XY) (1)\n50 (%) (1)\nALLOW 0.5 / ½ / 1 in 2\nALLOW ECF for the percentage from an incorrect square` },
      { q: `Mendel crossed pea plants that were heterozygous for seed shape. Round seed (R) is dominant to wrinkled seed (r).\n\nHe counted 5474 round seeds and 1850 wrinkled seeds in the offspring.\n\nCalculate the ratio of round seeds to wrinkled seeds.\nGive your answer in the form x : 1, to 2 decimal places.\n\nRatio = ______ : 1`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `5474 ÷ 1850 (1)\n2.96 (: 1) (2)\nALLOW 3.0 : 1 or 2.959 for 1 mark only (incorrect rounding)` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disorder caused by a recessive allele (f). The dominant allele is F.\n\nA man and a woman are both unaffected by cystic fibrosis. Their first child has cystic fibrosis.\n\nGive the genotype of the father and explain how you know.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Ff (1)\nthe child must be ff so received an f allele from each parent / from the father AND he is unaffected so he must also have an F allele (1)\nALLOW 'he is a carrier' for the genotype mark only if Ff is not given` },
      { q: `Gametes are made by meiosis. In humans, a sperm cell fertilises an egg cell to form a zygote.\n\nExplain why it is important that gametes are haploid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(so that at fertilisation) the zygote / embryo / offspring has the diploid / full / normal number of chromosomes (1)\nthe chromosome number does not double every generation (1)\nALLOW 46 / 23 pairs for diploid number in humans\nALLOW each gamete has half the chromosome number of a body cell / one chromosome from each pair (1) if neither point given` },
      { q: `A cell in the testis divides by meiosis to make sperm cells.\n\nGive two ways in which the cells produced by meiosis are different from the cell that divided.\n\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from: (2)\nthey contain half the number of chromosomes / are haploid (parent cell is diploid)\nthey contain only one chromosome from each pair\nthey are genetically different (from the parent cell / from each other)\nthey are smaller / have a tail (sperm)\nIGNORE four cells are made (this is not a difference between the cells)` },
      { q: `Strawberry plants can reproduce asexually by growing runners. A runner is a long stem that grows along the ground and forms a new plant where it touches the soil. Strawberry plants can also reproduce sexually by producing flowers and seeds.\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage to a strawberry plant of reproducing asexually.\n\nAdvantage ______\nDisadvantage ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `advantage, any one from: (1)\nonly one parent needed / no need for pollination / pollinators\nfaster / produces many offspring quickly\noffspring have the same (successful) characteristics / suited to the same environment\nuses less energy (than producing flowers / seeds)\n\ndisadvantage, any one from: (1)\nno (genetic) variation / offspring are clones\nall offspring may be killed by the same disease / change in environment\noffspring grow near the parent so compete for resources\nIGNORE cheaper` },
      { q: `Aphids are small insects that feed on plants. During the summer, female aphids reproduce asexually and give birth to large numbers of young. In the autumn, the aphids reproduce sexually and lay eggs that survive the winter.\n\nSuggest why it is an advantage for aphids to reproduce sexually before the winter.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `sexual reproduction produces (genetic) variation in the offspring (1)\n(so) some offspring / eggs are more likely to survive the cold / changing conditions / new diseases (1)\nALLOW natural selection can take place for the second mark` },
      { q: `Human height is affected by more than 700 different genes. A person's height is also affected by their diet during childhood.\n\nExplain why there is a wide range of heights in the human population rather than a small number of height groups.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `height is controlled by many genes / is not single gene inheritance (so many combinations of alleles are possible) (1)\nthe environment / diet (also) affects height / phenotype (1)\nALLOW height shows continuous variation for 1 mark if neither point is made` },
      { q: `Phenylketonuria (PKU) is a genetic disorder. People with PKU have a genetic variant in the gene for an enzyme called PAH. In people with PKU, the PAH enzyme does not work.\n\nThe genetic variant changes one base in the coding DNA of the gene.\n\nExplain how a change of one base could stop the PAH enzyme working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `changes the (sequence of) amino acid(s) in the protein / enzyme (1)\nchanges the (3D) shape of the active site (so the substrate no longer fits) (1)\nALLOW changes the way the protein folds\nALLOW a stop codon / triplet may be produced so the protein is incomplete (1) for the first marking point` },
      { q: `Most mammals stop making the enzyme lactase after they are weaned, so adults cannot digest lactose in milk. Many people in northern Europe have a genetic variant that means they keep making lactase as adults.\n\nThis variant is in non-coding DNA near the lactase gene. It does not change the base sequence of the lactase gene itself.\n\nExplain how a variant in non-coding DNA can change a person's phenotype.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `non-coding DNA controls whether a gene is expressed / switched on or off (1)\nso the variant changes how much protein / lactase is made (but not the structure of the protein) (1)\nALLOW it affects binding of transcription factors / RNA polymerase to the DNA\nIGNORE changes the shape of lactase` },
      { q: `In guinea pigs, the allele for black fur (B) is dominant to the allele for white fur (b).\n\nA breeder crosses two heterozygous black guinea pigs, Bb × Bb, several times. The crosses produce 24 offspring in total.\n\nCalculate the number of offspring that would be expected to have white fur.\n\nNumber with white fur = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `probability of bb = ¼ / 0.25 / 25% / ratio 3 : 1 (1)\n6 (2)\nALLOW 24 ÷ 4 for the first mark` },
      { q: `In Labrador dogs, the allele for black coat (B) is dominant to the allele for chocolate coat (b).\n\nA breeder has a black male Labrador. She does not know if his genotype is BB or Bb.\n\nSuggest how the breeder could use breeding to find out the genotype of the black dog.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `breed / cross him with a chocolate / bb / homozygous recessive female (1)\nif any puppies are chocolate he is Bb / if all puppies are black (in a large number of puppies) he is (probably) BB (1)\nALLOW cross with a known heterozygous Bb female and look for chocolate puppies for full marks` },
      { q: `Polydactyly is an inherited condition that causes extra fingers or toes. It is caused by a dominant allele (D). The recessive allele is d.\n\nA father has polydactyly and his genotype is Dd. The mother does not have polydactyly.\n\nWhat is the probability that their first child will have polydactyly?\nShow your working.\n\nProbability = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'What', markScheme: `working: mother is dd / gametes D, d and d / Punnett square showing Dd, Dd, dd, dd (1)\n0.5 / ½ / 50% / 1 in 2 (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 1 : 1 as a probability\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect genotype for the mother` },
      { q: `Mendel carried out his experiments on more than 28 000 pea plants over eight years.\n\nExplain why using a large number of plants made his conclusions more valid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reduces the effect of chance / random fertilisation / anomalies (1)\nso the ratios obtained are closer to the expected (3 : 1) ratio / patterns are easier to see / results are more repeatable (1)\nIGNORE 'more accurate' on its own` },
      { q: `A student crosses two heterozygous tall pea plants (Tt × Tt). The expected ratio of tall to short plants is 3 : 1. The student grows 8 seeds from the cross. Five plants are tall and three are short.\n\nExplain why the results do not match the expected ratio.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `fertilisation / which gametes fuse is random / ratios are probabilities (1)\nthe sample size / number of offspring is (too) small (1)\nALLOW with a larger number of plants the ratio would be closer to 3 : 1` },
      { q: `Huntington's disease is an inherited disorder of the nervous system caused by a dominant allele. The symptoms usually do not appear until a person is between 30 and 50 years old.\n\nExplain why the allele for Huntington's disease is often passed on to children.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `people often have children before (they know) they have the disease / before symptoms appear (1)\nonly one copy / one allele is needed (so each child has a 50% chance of inheriting it) (1)` },
      { q: `Mendel described characteristics as being controlled by 'inherited factors' that were passed from parents to offspring in pairs. He had no idea what these factors were made of.\n\nGive the modern name for Mendel's 'inherited factors' and name the structures in the nucleus where they are found.\n\nModern name ______\nStructures ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `genes / alleles (1)\nchromosomes (1)\nALLOW DNA for the second mark\nIGNORE nucleus` },
      { q: `In cats, short fur (L) is dominant to long fur (l).\n\nA heterozygous short-furred cat is crossed with a long-furred cat.\n\nDraw a Punnett square to show this cross. Use it to give the expected ratio of short-furred to long-furred kittens.\n\nRatio = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `parental gametes correctly shown: L and l (from heterozygous parent), l and l (from long-furred parent) (1)\noffspring genotypes Ll, Ll, ll, ll (1)\n1 : 1 (short : long) (1)\nALLOW 50% short, 50% long / 2 : 2\nALLOW ECF for the ratio from an incorrect Punnett square` },
      { q: `The table shows some of Mendel's results when he crossed heterozygous pea plants. (Results from Mendel's 1866 paper.)\n\nCharacteristic | Dominant phenotype (number) | Recessive phenotype (number)\nSeed colour | yellow (6022) | green (2001)\nStem length | tall (787) | dwarf (277)\n\nMendel concluded that each characteristic was inherited in a ratio of approximately 3 : 1.\n\nCalculate the ratio for each characteristic, in the form x : 1 to 2 decimal places, and state whether the data support Mendel's conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `seed colour 3.01 : 1 (1)\nstem length 2.84 : 1 (1)\nyes, both are close to 3 : 1 / seed colour is closer because the sample is larger (1)\nALLOW 6022 ÷ 2001 and 787 ÷ 277 seen for 1 mark if neither ratio is correct\nALLOW ECF for the conclusion` },
      { q: `Albinism is an inherited condition in which the body cannot make the pigment melanin. A family tree shows:\n\n• Tom and Ruth do not have albinism.\n• Tom and Ruth have three children: Ali and Ben do not have albinism, and Cara has albinism.\n\nExplain how the family tree shows that albinism is caused by a recessive allele.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cara has albinism but neither parent does / two unaffected parents have an affected child (1)\n(so) both parents must carry the allele / be heterozygous / carriers (1)\nthe allele is not expressed when a dominant allele is present / Cara must be homozygous recessive / has two copies (1)\nALLOW 'if it were dominant one of the parents would have albinism' for the third mark` },
      { q: `Brothers and sisters with the same parents usually look different from each other.\n\nExplain how meiosis and fertilisation cause children with the same parents to be genetically different.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from: (3)\nmeiosis produces gametes that are genetically different (from each other)\neach gamete gets one chromosome from each pair / chromosomes are shared out randomly (independent assortment)\n(Higher) crossing over / exchange of alleles between chromosomes in a pair\nwhich sperm fertilises which egg is random / fertilisation is random\nthe offspring gets a (new) combination of alleles from two parents / half the alleles from each parent` },
      { q: `Sickle cell anaemia is caused by a recessive allele (a). The dominant allele is A.\n\nTwo parents do not have sickle cell anaemia. Their first child has sickle cell anaemia. Their second child does not have sickle cell anaemia.\n\nCalculate the probability that the second child is a carrier (Aa) of the allele.\nShow your working.\n\nProbability = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `both parents are Aa / cross Aa × Aa gives AA, Aa, Aa, aa (1)\nthe child is not aa so only AA, Aa, Aa are possible (1)\n⅔ / 0.67 / 67% / 2 in 3 (1)\nALLOW ½ / 50% for 1 mark only (ignores the information that the child is unaffected)` },
      { q: `Scientists compared the DNA of many people. They found that most people have millions of small differences (genetic variants) in their DNA compared with other people, but most of these variants have no effect on phenotype.\n\nExplain why most genetic variants have no effect on phenotype.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from: (3)\nmost DNA is non-coding / most variants are in non-coding DNA\n(Higher) the variant may not affect how / whether a gene is expressed\na change in a base may still code for the same amino acid / the protein produced is the same\nthe amino acid change may not affect the shape / function of the protein / not in the active site\nthe variant may be in a recessive allele (so is not expressed when a dominant allele is present)` },
      { q: `In Ireland in the 1840s most farmers grew one variety of potato called the Lumper. New potato plants were grown from tubers (part of the parent plant), which is a form of asexual reproduction. Between 1845 and 1849 a disease called potato blight destroyed most of the potato crop.\n\nExplain why growing potatoes by asexual reproduction allowed blight to destroy most of the crop.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `asexual reproduction produces offspring that are genetically identical / clones (1)\nso there was no variation / no plants had resistance to blight (1)\nso all the plants were equally susceptible / if one was infected all could be infected (1)\nALLOW no plants had the alleles to survive / no natural selection could occur` },
      { q: `A breeder recorded the coat colours of all the puppies born when heterozygous black Labradors (Bb) were bred together. (Data written for this practice question.)\n\nThe crosses produced 64 puppies: 47 black and 17 chocolate.\n\nCalculate the ratio of black to chocolate puppies in the form x : 1, and compare it to the ratio expected from a genetic cross.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `47 ÷ 17 (1)\n2.76 (: 1) (1)\nALLOW 2.8 : 1\nexpected ratio is 3 : 1 so the result is close to / slightly lower than expected / 16 chocolate expected and 17 observed (1)\nALLOW ECF for the comparison` },
      { q: `Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes. One pair are the sex chromosomes.\n\nUse a genetic diagram to explain why, in each pregnancy, there is an equal chance of having a boy or a girl.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mother is XX and father is XY (1)\nmother's eggs all contain X; father's sperm contain X or Y in equal numbers / gametes shown correctly (1)\noffspring shown as 2 XX and 2 XY / 50% XX (girl) and 50% XY (boy) (1)\nALLOW the sex of the child depends on whether an X or Y sperm fertilises the egg` },
      { q: `Mendel crossed pure-breeding tall pea plants (TT) with pure-breeding short pea plants (tt). All of the first generation (F1) plants were tall. He then crossed two F1 plants together. In the second generation (F2), about three-quarters of the plants were tall and one-quarter were short.\n\nUse genetic diagrams to explain these results.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `first cross: TT × tt gives all Tt (1)\nall F1 are tall because the tall allele / T is dominant (1)\nsecond cross: Tt × Tt with gametes T, t and T, t / Punnett square shown (1)\nTT, Tt, Tt, tt so 3 tall : 1 short / only tt plants are short (1)\nALLOW any suitable letters if a key is given\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect first cross` },
      { q: `Polydactyly is caused by a dominant allele (D). A family tree shows three generations:\n\n• Generation 1: Jack has polydactyly; his wife Emma does not.\n• Generation 2: Jack and Emma have two children. Liam has polydactyly; Sophie does not.\n• Liam marries Priya, who does not have polydactyly.\n• Generation 3: Liam and Priya have one son, Noah, who does not have polydactyly.\n\nGive the genotypes of Liam and Noah and calculate the probability that Liam and Priya's next child will have polydactyly. Explain your answers.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Noah is dd because he does not have polydactyly / does not have the dominant allele (1)\nLiam is Dd (1)\nbecause he has polydactyly but passed a d allele to Noah / because his mother Emma is dd so he must have a d allele (1)\nprobability = ½ / 0.5 / 50% (Dd × dd) (1)\nALLOW ECF for the probability from an incorrect genotype for Liam` },
      { q: `A student compares two types of cell division in humans: the division of skin cells to replace damaged skin, and the division of cells in the ovary to make egg cells.\n\nComplete the table to compare the two types of cell division.\n\nFeature | Skin cell division | Ovary cell division to make eggs\nName of type of cell division | ______ | ______\nNumber of cell divisions | ______ | ______\nNumber of cells produced from one cell | ______ | ______\nNumber of chromosomes in each cell produced | ______ | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `mitosis | meiosis (1)\n1 / one | 2 / two (1)\n2 | 4 (1)\n46 | 23 (1)\neach row must be fully correct for the mark\nALLOW diploid | haploid for the last row\nIGNORE in humans only one of the four cells becomes an egg` },
      { q: `Our understanding of inheritance has developed over the last 160 years.\n\n• In the 1860s Mendel bred pea plants and counted the offspring with different characteristics.\n• In the early 1900s scientists observed chromosomes behaving in pairs during the formation of gametes.\n• In 1953 the structure of DNA was described, and in 2003 the Human Genome Project was completed.\n\nDescribe Mendel's work and explain how later discoveries have developed our understanding of inheritance.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed description of Mendel's method AND results with clear explanation of how later discoveries support and extend his ideas. Reasoning is clear and logically structured, with relevant information well supported.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of Mendel's work AND some explanation of how later discoveries developed understanding, OR a detailed description of Mendel's work only. Reasoning has some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Some relevant points about Mendel's work or later discoveries. Little structure.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMendel's work\n• bred / crossed pea plants with contrasting characteristics, e.g. tall and short, round and wrinkled seeds\n• used pure-breeding plants and controlled pollination\n• counted large numbers of offspring over several generations\n• F1 all showed one characteristic; F2 showed a 3 : 1 ratio\n• concluded characteristics are determined by 'inherited factors' that come in pairs, one from each parent\n• some factors are dominant / hide others (recessive); factors are not blended\n• his work was not recognised until after his death\nLater discoveries\n• chromosomes are found in pairs and separate in meiosis, matching Mendel's pairs of factors\n• Mendel's factors are genes on chromosomes; different versions are alleles\n• genes are sections of DNA; structure of DNA explains how genes are copied and passed on\n• base sequence of DNA codes for proteins, which determine phenotype\n• genome sequencing shows most characteristics are controlled by many genes, not single genes\n• genetic variants / mutations in DNA explain new alleles\n• allows identification of genes linked to inherited disorders` },
      { q: `A commercial grower produces strawberries. Strawberry plants can reproduce in two ways:\n\n• asexually, by runners that grow into new plants\n• sexually, by flowers that are pollinated by insects and produce seeds.\n\nThe grower wants to keep a high-yielding variety and also to develop new varieties that are resistant to a fungal disease.\n\nEvaluate the use of asexual and sexual reproduction by the grower.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Gives advantages AND disadvantages of BOTH types of reproduction and reaches a justified conclusion linked to the grower's two aims. Reasoning is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Gives advantages and disadvantages of one type of reproduction, OR advantages of both types, with some link to the grower's aims. Reasoning has some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives at least one advantage or disadvantage of either type of reproduction. Little structure.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nAsexual reproduction\n• offspring are genetically identical / clones of the parent\n• so high-yield characteristics are kept in every plant\n• quick; many new plants from one parent; no pollinators needed\n• crop is uniform, e.g. fruit ripens at the same time\n• no variation, so all plants are equally susceptible to the fungus / a new disease\n• cannot produce new varieties\nSexual reproduction\n• meiosis and random fertilisation produce genetic variation\n• some offspring may have alleles for resistance to the fungus\n• resistant plants can be selected and bred (selective breeding)\n• slower; needs pollinators / two parents; uses more energy\n• offspring may not have the high-yield characteristics\nConclusion\n• use sexual reproduction to produce new resistant varieties, then asexual reproduction (runners) to multiply the best plants as clones` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.2': {
    name: 'Natural Selection & Evolution',
    questions: [
      { q: `The snow leopard lives in the mountains of central Asia. Its binomial name is Panthera uncia.\n\nThe lion has the binomial name Panthera leo.\n\nWhat is the genus of the snow leopard?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `Panthera ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW uncia\nIGNORE Panthera uncia` },
      { q: `In 1990 Carl Woese suggested that all living organisms should be classified into three domains.\n\nA mushroom is a fungus. Its cells have a nucleus.\n\nWhich domain does the mushroom belong to?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\nArchaea / Bacteria / Eukaryota / Prokaryota`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `Eukaryota ✓\nALLOW eukaryotes\nIf more than one option ringed, 0 marks` },
      { q: `A student records four characteristics of people in their class.\n\n• blood group\n• a scar on the knee\n• body mass\n• language spoken at home\n\nWhich characteristic is caused only by genes?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\n\nblood group / scar on the knee / body mass / language spoken at home`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `blood group ✓\nIf more than one option ringed, 0 marks` },
      { q: `Bacteria reproduce very quickly. A sample taken from an infected wound contained 4 500 000 000 bacteria. Some of these bacteria were resistant to the antibiotic methicillin.\n\nGive the number of bacteria in the sample in standard form.\n\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `4.5 × 10⁹ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 45 × 10⁸` },
      { q: `Fossils of an animal called Archaeopteryx were found in limestone rocks in Germany. The fossils show an animal with feathers, teeth and a long bony tail.\n\nGive one way that fossils can be formed.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\nhard parts (e.g. bones / shells / teeth) are gradually replaced by minerals ✓\nparts of the organism do not decay because conditions needed for decay are absent ✓\nALLOW preserved in ice / amber / peat bog / tar\nthe organism leaves traces (e.g. footprints / burrows / imprints) which are covered by sediment and become rock ✓\nIGNORE 'buried' unqualified` },
      { q: `In 1858 Charles Darwin and another scientist presented a joint paper about the theory of evolution by natural selection. The other scientist had studied animals in South America and South-East Asia and had reached the same conclusions as Darwin independently.\n\nName the other scientist.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `(Alfred Russel) Wallace ✓\nALLOW phonetic spelling` },
      { q: `The peppered moth rests on tree trunks during the day. It can be pale or dark. Birds eat the moths that they can see most easily.\n\nIn the 1800s, smoke from factories near Manchester killed the pale lichens on tree trunks and covered the bark with black soot.\n\nThe table shows the percentage of dark moths caught near Manchester.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Percentage of dark moths (%)\n1850 | 2\n1870 | 35\n1890 | 80\n1910 | 96\n\nDescribe the trend shown by the data. Use numbers from the table in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `percentage of dark moths increases (over time / from 1850 to 1910) ✓\nquoting data with years, e.g. from 2% in 1850 to 96% in 1910 ✓\nALLOW increases fastest between 1850 and 1890 / slows between 1890 and 1910 for the second mark if supported by data` },
      { q: `Scientists studied pairs of twins. Identical twins have the same genes. Non-identical twins share about half of their genes.\n\nThe table shows the mean difference in height between the twins in each pair.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nType of twins | Mean difference in height (cm)\nidentical twins raised together | 1.7\nidentical twins raised apart | 1.8\nnon-identical twins raised together | 4.4\n\nWhat do the results show about the causes of variation in height?\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `height is mainly controlled by genes / genetic variation ✓\nbecause identical twins have a smaller difference (than non-identical twins) even when raised apart ✓\nALLOW environment has only a small effect on height because identical twins raised apart differ by only 0.1 cm more than those raised together\nALLOW height is affected by both genes and environment for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student measured the length of six leaves taken from the same holly tree.\n\nLeaf | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6\nLength (mm) | 42 | 38 | 45 | 51 | 39 | 44\n\nCalculate the mean leaf length.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nMean leaf length = ______ mm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK THE ANSWER ON THE ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 43.2 (mm) award 2 marks\n259 ÷ 6 ✓\n= 43.2 (mm) ✓\nALLOW 43.17 / 43.166… for 1 mark (not to 1 decimal place)` },
      { q: `Leaves on the same holly tree can be different lengths. All the cells in the tree contain the same genes.\n\nSuggest two environmental factors that could cause the leaves on the tree to grow to different lengths.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nlight intensity / amount of shade ✓\navailability of water ✓\navailability of mineral ions / nutrients ✓\ntemperature ✓\ndamage by animals / disease / wind ✓\nIGNORE weather / soil unqualified\nIGNORE genes / mutation` },
      { q: `A hospital tested samples of the bacterium E. coli taken from patients with urinary infections. The table shows how many samples were resistant to the antibiotic trimethoprim.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Number of samples tested | Number of resistant samples\n2015 | 1500 | 120\n2020 | 1500 | 210\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the number of resistant samples from 2015 to 2020.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK THE ANSWER ON THE ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 75 (%) award 2 marks\n(210 – 120) ÷ 120 (× 100) ✓\n= 75 (%) ✓\nALLOW 90 ÷ 120 for the first mark` },
      { q: `Carl Linnaeus classified organisms in the 1700s. He used features that he could see, such as the number of legs or the shape of flowers.\n\nToday, scientists also compare the DNA base sequences of organisms.\n\nGive two reasons why using DNA sequences gives a more accurate classification than using observable features alone.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nsimilar features may evolve in unrelated organisms (e.g. wings in bats and birds) ✓\nDNA shows how closely related organisms are / shows evolutionary relationships / common ancestors ✓\nDNA differences can be measured / counted (so comparisons are objective / quantitative) ✓\nmicroorganisms have few observable features to compare ✓\nALLOW more similar DNA = more closely related` },
      { q: `Scientists compared the DNA base sequences of humans and other apes. The table shows the percentage of DNA bases that are different from human DNA.\n\nSpecies | Difference from human DNA (%)\nchimpanzee | 1.2\ngorilla | 1.6\norangutan | 3.1\n\nWhich species is most closely related to humans?\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `chimpanzee ✓\n(because) it has the smallest difference in DNA / most similar DNA (so shares the most recent common ancestor with humans) ✓\nThe second mark is dependent on chimpanzee` },
      { q: `Bats and birds both have wings and can fly. In an artificial classification system they could be placed in the same group. In a natural classification system they are placed in different groups. Bats are mammals and birds are not.\n\nDescribe the difference between an artificial classification system and a natural classification system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `artificial classification groups organisms using one or a few observable features (chosen for convenience) ✓\nnatural classification groups organisms by evolutionary relationships / common ancestors / many shared features ✓\nALLOW natural classification uses DNA / molecular evidence` },
      { q: `Scientists have found fossils of many different species of horse-like animals. However, there are gaps in the fossil record, so scientists cannot be certain how all the species are related.\n\nGive two reasons why there are gaps in the fossil record.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nmost organisms decay (completely) before they can fossilise ✓\nsoft-bodied organisms / soft tissue rarely fossilise ✓\nconditions needed for fossil formation are rare ✓\nfossils have been destroyed by geological activity / erosion / movement of rock ✓\nmany fossils have not been found / discovered yet ✓` },
      { q: `Ensatina salamanders live in the hills around the Central Valley in California. Two populations, one from the east and one from the west of the valley, look very different.\n\nScientists want to find out whether the two populations are the same species or different species.\n\nDescribe how scientists could find this out.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `breed / mate a salamander from one population with a salamander from the other population ✓\nif they cannot produce (offspring that are) fertile offspring they are different species ✓\nALLOW if they produce fertile offspring they are the same species\nALLOW compare DNA sequences for 1 mark if no breeding idea given` },
      { q: `In the 1950s a virus that causes the disease myxomatosis killed over 99% of the rabbits in Britain. A few rabbits survived because they had a different version of a gene that gave them resistance to the virus.\n\nExplain how the different version of the gene first appeared in the rabbit population.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(random) mutation ✓\nchange in the DNA base sequence (produces a new allele) ✓\nALLOW change in the genetic code / DNA\nDO NOT ALLOW mutation caused by the virus / rabbits mutated in order to survive` },
      { q: `Charles Darwin visited the Galápagos Islands in 1835. He collected many specimens and recorded what he saw.\n\nGive two pieces of evidence that Darwin used to develop his theory of evolution by natural selection.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nfinches / tortoises / mockingbirds on different islands had different features ✓\nALLOW finches had different beak shapes on different islands linked to different food\nfeatures of organisms were suited to their environment ✓\nfossils (of extinct animals similar to living species) ✓\nresults of selective breeding (e.g. of pigeons) ✓\nindividuals in a species show variation ✓\nmore offspring are produced than survive ✓` },
      { q: `Darwin published his book 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859. Many people did not accept his theory of evolution by natural selection at first.\n\nSuggest two reasons why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nit challenged religious beliefs / the idea that God made all living things ✓\nthere was not enough evidence at the time ✓\nthe mechanism of inheritance / genes / DNA was not known ✓\nevolution is too slow to be observed in a lifetime ✓` },
      { q: `Alfred Russel Wallace studied butterflies in South America. Some butterflies that taste unpleasant to birds have very bright colours. Wallace suggested that the bright colours are a warning to predators.\n\nExplain how natural selection could have led to most of these butterflies having bright colours.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `brightly coloured butterflies are less likely to be eaten (as birds learn to avoid them) / more likely to survive ✓\nso they reproduce and pass on the allele(s) for bright colour (to their offspring) ✓\nIGNORE butterflies change colour to survive` },
      { q: `A single bacterium resistant to an antibiotic gets into a wound. Under these conditions the bacteria divide once every 30 minutes.\n\nCalculate how many resistant bacteria there will be after 3 hours.\nAssume that none of the bacteria die.\n\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK THE ANSWER ON THE ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 64 award 2 marks\n6 divisions / 2⁶ ✓\n= 64 ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 12 (from 6 × 2) or 128 (from 7 divisions)` },
      { q: `The table shows the binomial names of four animals.\n\nCommon name | Binomial name\nbrown bear | Ursus arctos\npolar bear | Ursus maritimus\ngiant panda | Ailuropoda melanoleuca\nred fox | Vulpes vulpes\n\nWhich two animals are most closely related?\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `brown bear and polar bear ✓\n(because) they are in the same genus / both have the genus Ursus ✓\nThe second mark is dependent on the correct pair` },
      { q: `The table shows how the grey wolf is classified.\n\nKingdom | Animalia\nPhylum | Chordata\nClass | Mammalia\n______ | Carnivora\nFamily | Canidae\n______ | Canis\nSpecies | lupus\n\nComplete the table by writing the two missing names of the classification groups.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Order ✓\nGenus ✓\nmust be in the correct positions` },
      { q: `Woese compared the base sequences of ribosomal RNA in many different microorganisms. Before this, all prokaryotes were placed together in one kingdom.\n\nWoese placed the Archaea in a separate domain from the Bacteria.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the RNA / genetic sequences of archaea were very different from those of bacteria ✓\nso archaea are not closely related to bacteria / have a different evolutionary history even though both are prokaryotes ✓\nALLOW archaea are as different from bacteria as they are from eukaryotes\nIGNORE references to living in extreme environments` },
      { q: `Hydrangea plants can be grown as clones from cuttings. Two clones of the same hydrangea plant were planted in different gardens. One clone had blue flowers and the other had pink flowers.\n\nSuggest why the two clones had different coloured flowers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the clones have the same genes / genetic information ✓\nso the difference must be caused by the environment, e.g. different soil pH ✓\nALLOW different minerals / nutrients in the soil` },
      { q: `Cavendish bananas are grown on farms all over the world. All Cavendish banana plants are clones, so there is almost no genetic variation between them. A fungal disease called Panama disease is spreading through banana farms.\n\nExplain why a lack of genetic variation puts the Cavendish banana at risk from the disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `if one plant is not resistant, none of them are resistant / no plants have an allele for resistance ✓\nso all plants could be killed / no plants survive to reproduce (so natural selection cannot occur) ✓\nALLOW the whole crop could be wiped out` },
      { q: `The Clean Air Act was passed in 1956. After this, there was less soot from factories and pale lichens grew back on the tree trunks near Manchester.\n\nThe table shows the percentage of dark peppered moths caught near Manchester after 1956.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Percentage of dark moths (%)\n1960 | 94\n1980 | 60\n2000 | 12\n\nUse ideas about natural selection to explain the change shown in the table.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(on pale tree trunks) dark moths are more visible to birds / pale moths are camouflaged ✓\nso pale moths are more likely to survive and reproduce / dark moths are more likely to be eaten ✓\npale moths pass on the allele for pale colour to their offspring (so the proportion of pale moths increases over generations) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW moths change colour to match the bark` },
      { q: `MRSA is a strain of the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to many antibiotics. The table shows the percentage of S. aureus samples from blood infections in one country that were MRSA.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Percentage of samples that were MRSA (%)\n1990 | 2\n1995 | 15\n2000 | 41\n\nExplain how the use of antibiotics led to the change shown in the table.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a (random) mutation produced bacteria that were resistant (to the antibiotic) ✓\nwhen the antibiotic was used, non-resistant bacteria were killed but resistant bacteria survived ✓\nresistant bacteria reproduced / multiplied and passed on the resistance (allele / gene) ✓\nALLOW resistant bacteria have less competition\nDO NOT ALLOW bacteria become immune / antibiotics cause the mutation` },
      { q: `The table shows information about fossils of some ancestors of the modern horse.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nAnimal | Age of fossils (million years) | Height at shoulder (m) | Number of toes on each front foot\nHyracotherium | 55 | 0.4 | 4\nMesohippus | 35 | 0.6 | 3\nMerychippus | 15 | 1.0 | 3\nEquus (modern horse) | 0 | 1.6 | 1\n\nDescribe the changes shown in the table and explain how these fossils provide evidence for evolution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `height increases (over time) / from 0.4 m to 1.6 m ✓\nnumber of toes decreases (over time) / from 4 to 1 ✓\nshows gradual change in a group of organisms over millions of years / shows that modern horses developed from earlier (different) species ✓\nALLOW fossils in older rocks are more different from the modern horse` },
      { q: `The Grand Canyon in Arizona is a deep valley formed over millions of years by the Colorado River. Kaibab squirrels live only on the north side of the canyon. Abert's squirrels live on the south side. Scientists think that both types of squirrel evolved from one population of squirrels.\n\nExplain how two different species of squirrel could have formed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the population was split / isolated (by the canyon / river) so the two groups could not interbreed ✓\nthere were different conditions / selection pressures on each side, so different variants survived and reproduced (by natural selection) ✓\nover a long time the populations became so different that they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring ✓\nALLOW different mutations occur in each population for the second mark` },
      { q: `Warfarin is a poison used to kill rats. In some areas, rats have become resistant to warfarin. Scientists trapped and tested 400 rats from the same area each year.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Number of rats resistant to warfarin (out of 400)\n1 | 12\n3 | 88\n6 | 260\n\nCalculate the percentage of rats that were resistant to warfarin in year 6.\nSuggest why the percentage of resistant rats increased so quickly.\n\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `260 ÷ 400 × 100 = 65 (%) ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 260 ÷ 400 / 0.65\nwarfarin kills non-resistant rats so resistant rats survive and breed / pass on the allele for resistance (and rats have many offspring / a short generation time) ✓` },
      { q: `Darwin and Wallace both developed the theory of evolution by natural selection in the mid-1800s.\n\nDescribe how Darwin and Wallace developed the theory.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\nDarwin made observations / collected specimens on the voyage of the Beagle / in the Galápagos Islands ✓\nWallace collected specimens / made observations in South America / South-East Asia / the Malay Archipelago ✓\nboth noticed variation within species and that organisms are suited to their environment ✓\nWallace sent his ideas to Darwin / they worked independently and reached the same conclusion ✓\nthey published / presented a joint paper (in 1858) ✓\nDarwin published 'On the Origin of Species' (in 1859) ✓\nDarwin also used evidence from selective breeding / fossils ✓` },
      { q: `Cytochrome c is a protein found in the cells of many organisms. Scientists counted the number of differences in the amino acid sequence of cytochrome c between humans and other species.\n(data written for this practice question, based on published values)\n\nSpecies | Number of amino acid differences from human cytochrome c\nrhesus monkey | 1\ndog | 11\nhorse | 12\nchicken | 13\ntuna | 21\nyeast | 44\n\nExplain what these data show about the evolutionary relationships between humans and these organisms.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `rhesus monkey is the most closely related to humans / yeast is the least closely related ✓\nfewer differences means a more recent common ancestor ✓\nbecause there has been less time for mutations to change the DNA / gene coding for the protein ✓\nALLOW dog, horse and chicken are about equally related to humans\nALLOW all share a common ancestor (as all have cytochrome c)` },
      { q: `Elephant shrews are small African mammals with long noses. They look like shrews and were once classified in the same group as shrews.\n\nScientists compared the DNA of elephant shrews with other mammals. They found that elephant shrews are more closely related to elephants than to shrews. Elephant shrews have now been reclassified.\n\nExplain why the elephant shrew was first classified with shrews and why it has now been reclassified.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `it was first classified using observable features / it looks like a shrew ✓\nDNA shows the elephant shrew shares a more recent common ancestor with elephants ✓\nsimilar features can evolve in organisms that are not closely related / classification now reflects evolutionary relationships (natural classification) ✓` },
      { q: `A student reads this statement on a website:\n\n'Giraffes have long necks because each giraffe stretched its neck to reach leaves, and then passed its longer neck on to its offspring.'\n\nThe student says this is not how evolution happens.\n\nExplain how giraffes with long necks evolved by natural selection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `there was variation in neck length (caused by genes / mutation) in the population ✓\ngiraffes with longer necks could reach more food so were more likely to survive (and reproduce) ✓\nthey passed on the allele(s) for long necks to their offspring, so over many generations more giraffes had long necks ✓\nIGNORE references to stretching` },
      { q: `Rats that are resistant to warfarin have an allele called R. The table shows information about two types of rat.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nType of rat | Survival after eating warfarin (%) | Daily vitamin K needed compared to normal rats\nnormal (no R allele) | 5 | 1 ×\nresistant (has R allele) | 95 | 10 ×\n\nIn one area, warfarin was used for many years and most rats became resistant. When warfarin was no longer used, the percentage of resistant rats fell again.\n\nUse the table to explain both changes in the percentage of resistant rats.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `when warfarin was used:\nresistant rats were much more likely to survive (95% compared with 5%) ✓\nso they reproduced and passed on the R allele / the R allele became more common ✓\nwhen warfarin stopped:\nresistant rats need 10 times more vitamin K so are at a disadvantage / less likely to survive when food is short ✓\nso normal rats survive and reproduce more / pass on the normal allele, so the proportion of resistant rats falls ✓` },
      { q: `During a drought in 1977 on Daphne Major, one of the Galápagos Islands, most of the small, soft seeds were eaten. Only large, hard seeds were left. Many medium ground finches died.\n\nScientists measured the beak depth of finches before the drought and of the birds that survived the drought.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nGroup of finches | Mean beak depth (mm)\nbefore drought | 9.2\nsurvivors of drought | 9.7\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean beak depth.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nExplain why the mean beak depth of the survivors was greater.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(9.7 – 9.2) ÷ 9.2 × 100 ✓\n= 5.4 (%) ✓\nALLOW 5.43 / 5.434… for 1 mark (not 2 significant figures)\nfinches with deeper beaks could crack / eat the large, hard seeds ✓\nso they were more likely to survive (and reproduce), while finches with shallower beaks starved ✓` },
      { q: `A student is asked to compare two ways of classifying organisms.\n\nMethod A: grouping organisms by observable features, such as body shape or number of legs.\nMethod B: comparing the DNA base sequences of organisms (molecular phylogenetics).\n\nEvaluate the two methods of classifying organisms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any four from:\nMethod A:\nadvantage: quick / cheap / can be done in the field / can be used on fossils ✓\ndisadvantage: similar features can evolve in unrelated organisms, so organisms may be grouped wrongly ✓\ndisadvantage: microorganisms have few features to compare / features may be affected by the environment ✓\nMethod B:\nadvantage: shows evolutionary relationships / how recently organisms shared a common ancestor ✓\nadvantage: differences can be measured / counted so it is more objective ✓\ndisadvantage: expensive / needs specialist equipment / DNA may not be available from fossils / extinct species ✓\nFor 4 marks there must be at least one point about each method` },
      { q: `*Scientists collected data about antibiotic use and antibiotic resistance in one country.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Antibiotic prescriptions (millions) | E. coli samples resistant to the antibiotic ciprofloxacin (%)\n2005 | 34 | 7\n2010 | 39 | 13\n2015 | 42 | 18\n2020 | 35 | 17\n\nExplain how bacteria become resistant to antibiotics and use the data to evaluate how the country could reduce the spread of antibiotic resistance.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Please refer to the marking instructions for Level of Response questions.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks)\nA detailed explanation of how resistance develops by natural selection AND uses the data to evaluate at least two ways of reducing resistance.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nAn explanation of how resistance develops AND refers to the data OR suggests ways of reducing resistance.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nA simple statement about how resistance develops OR a way of reducing resistance.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nHow resistance develops\n• random mutation in bacterial DNA\n• some bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic\n• the antibiotic kills non-resistant bacteria\n• resistant bacteria survive and reproduce\n• resistant bacteria pass on the resistance allele / gene\n• the proportion of resistant bacteria increases over generations\n• bacteria reproduce rapidly so this happens quickly\nUse of data\n• as prescriptions increased from 34 to 42 million, resistance rose from 7% to 18%\n• positive correlation between prescriptions and resistance\n• when prescriptions fell to 35 million in 2020, resistance fell only slightly, to 17%\n• resistant bacteria remain in the population once they have spread\n• correlation does not prove causation / other factors may be involved\nReducing resistance\n• only prescribe antibiotics for bacterial infections, not viral ones\n• reduce the number of prescriptions overall (data suggest this slows the increase)\n• patients complete the course of antibiotics\n• reduce use of antibiotics in farming\n• good hygiene in hospitals to stop resistant bacteria spreading\n• develop new antibiotics` },
      { q: `*Classification systems have changed over time.\n\n• In the 1700s, Linnaeus grouped organisms into kingdoms and smaller groups and gave each species a two-part (binomial) name.\n• In the 1960s, a five-kingdom system was used: animals, plants, fungi, protoctists and prokaryotes.\n• In 1990, Woese used evidence from comparing genetic sequences to propose three domains: Archaea, Bacteria and Eukaryota.\n\nExplain how and why classification systems have changed since the time of Linnaeus.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Please refer to the marking instructions for Level of Response questions.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks)\nDescribes the Linnaean system AND explains in detail how new evidence (microscopy and/or DNA / RNA sequencing) led to changes, including the three-domain system.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes the Linnaean system AND gives a reason for a change in classification.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nDescribes a feature of one classification system OR gives a reason for a change.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nLinnaeus\n• based on observable features / structure\n• hierarchy: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species\n• binomial name: genus then species, e.g. Homo sapiens\n• gives a universal name used by scientists in all countries\nWhy systems changed\n• improved microscopes showed internal cell structure, e.g. presence or absence of a nucleus\n• discovery of many new microorganisms\n• observable features can be misleading: similar features may evolve in unrelated organisms\n• Darwin's theory led to classification based on evolutionary relationships (natural classification)\n• DNA / RNA sequencing (molecular phylogenetics) shows how closely related organisms are\n• more similar sequences means a more recent common ancestor\nThree domains\n• Woese found archaea were genetically very different from bacteria\n• so prokaryotes were split into two domains, Archaea and Bacteria\n• all organisms with a nucleus are in the domain Eukaryota\n• some organisms have been reclassified as a result of new evidence` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.1': {
    name: 'Monitoring & Maintaining the Environment',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student wants to find out which invertebrates live on the floor of a wood.\nThe student digs a hole and puts a jar in it so that the rim of the jar is level with the soil surface. A flat stone is propped up over the top of the jar to keep out rain. The student checks the jar the next morning.\n\nWhat is the name of this sampling method?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `pitfall trap (1)\nIGNORE jar / trap on its own` },
      { q: `A student is estimating the number of daisy plants on a school field.\nThe student lays two long tape measures along two edges of the field, at right angles to each other.\n\nDescribe how the student can use the tape measures to place each quadrat at random.`, marks: 1, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `use a random number generator / random number table to give (two) coordinates along the tape measures and place the quadrat where they meet (1)\nALLOW pick numbers out of a hat for coordinates\nDO NOT ALLOW throwing the quadrat` },
      { q: `Grey squirrels were brought to the UK from North America in the 19th century. Since then the number of red squirrels in the UK has fallen.\n\nWhich term describes the grey squirrel in the UK?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nendangered species / indigenous species / non-indigenous species / producer`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `non-indigenous species (1)` },
      { q: `A report estimates that 10 000 000 hectares of forest are cut down in the world each year.\n\nGive this number in standard form.\nArea = ______ hectares`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 × 10⁷ (1)\nALLOW 1.0 × 10⁷` },
      { q: `A student wants to sample the insects that live on the stems and leaves of tall grass in a meadow.\n\nWhich piece of equipment should the student use?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\npitfall trap / pooter / quadrat / sweep net`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `sweep net (1)` },
      { q: `The Millennium Seed Bank in the UK stores seeds from thousands of wild plant species. The seeds are dried and then stored at −20 °C.\n\nGive one reason why the seeds are dried and kept at −20 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• so the seeds do not germinate (1)\n• so the seeds stay alive / viable for a long time (1)\n• to slow down (enzyme activity / respiration in) the seeds so they do not use up their food stores (1)\n• to stop the growth of fungi / microorganisms that could decay the seeds (1)\nIGNORE to preserve them unqualified` },
      { q: `Some students estimate the number of dandelions on a school field. The area of the field is 750 m².\nThey place 8 quadrats at random. Each quadrat has an area of 1 m².\nThe table shows their results (data written for this practice question).\nQuadrat: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\nNumber of dandelions: 4, 7, 2, 0, 5, 3, 6, 5\n\nCalculate the estimated number of dandelions on the whole field.\nNumber of dandelions = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean = 32 ÷ 8 = 4 (dandelions per m²) (1)\n4 × 750 = 3000 (1)\nALLOW 3000 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect mean × 750` },
      { q: `A student estimates the population of woodlice under logs in a garden.\n• On the first day the student catches 45 woodlice, marks each one with a small dot of non-toxic paint and releases them.\n• Two days later the student catches 52 woodlice. 9 of these have a paint mark.\n\nCalculate the estimated population of woodlice in the garden.\nUse the formula: population = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample\nPopulation = ______ woodlice`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(45 × 52) ÷ 9 (1)\n= 260 (1)\nALLOW 260 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student uses capture–recapture to estimate the number of snails in a garden. The student marks each snail with a dot of paint on the underside of its shell, not on the top.\n\nExplain why the student puts the mark on the underside of the shell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `so the marked snails are not more easily seen by predators / birds (1)\nso marked snails are not more likely to be eaten than unmarked snails / so the estimate is not affected (1)\nALLOW so the mark does not affect the survival of the snail for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A group of students investigates the seaweeds on a rocky shore. They want to find out how the types of seaweed change from the low tide mark to the high tide mark.\nThey decide to place quadrats along a transect line rather than placing quadrats at random over the whole shore.\n\nExplain why a transect is more suitable than random sampling for this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the students want to see how the distribution changes with distance up the shore / along a gradient (1)\nrandom quadrats would not show the change in distribution / would not be placed at regular positions from low to high tide (1)\nALLOW abiotic factors such as time out of water change across the shore for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student estimates the percentage cover of moss on a lawn.\nThe student uses a quadrat divided into 25 equal squares.\nIn one quadrat, moss covers 9 squares completely and covers about half of each of another 6 squares. The rest of the quadrat has no moss.\n\nCalculate the percentage cover of moss in this quadrat.\nPercentage cover = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `9 + (6 ÷ 2) = 12 squares (1)\n(12 ÷ 25) × 100 = 48 (%) (1)\nALLOW 48 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Large areas of the Amazon rainforest in Brazil have been cut down. The land is used for cattle ranching and for growing soya.\n\nGive two ways in which this deforestation reduces biodiversity.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• habitats are destroyed (1)\n• animals lose their food sources / food chains are broken (1)\n• many tree / plant species are removed and replaced by one crop / grass (monoculture) (1)\n• species may become extinct (1)\n• animals are forced to move away (1)\nIGNORE less oxygen / more carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Between 1950 and 1990 many farmers in the UK removed hedgerows to make their fields bigger. Now some farmers are paid to plant new hedgerows and to leave wild flowers growing around the edges of their fields.\n\nExplain how planting hedgerows can increase biodiversity on farmland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `provide habitats / food / nesting sites (1)\nfor a wider range of species e.g. birds / insects / small mammals (1)\nALLOW act as corridors so animals can move between areas of woodland / habitats for 1 mark` },
      { q: `The number of cod in the North Sea fell sharply in the 1990s. Fishing boats now have to use nets with a larger mesh size (bigger holes in the net).\n\nExplain how using a larger mesh size helps the cod population to recover.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `young / small cod can escape through the net (1)\nso they survive to breed / reach reproductive age (1)\nIGNORE fewer fish caught unqualified` },
      { q: `This table shows the most northerly place in the UK where a species of butterfly was recorded breeding (data written for this practice question).\n1980: northern England\n2000: southern Scotland\n2020: central Scotland\nOver the same time, the mean temperature in the UK increased.\n\nSuggest why the range of the butterfly has moved further north.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `global warming / climate change has made northern areas warmer (1)\nso conditions in the north are now suitable for the butterfly / its food plants (to survive and breed) (1)\nALLOW the butterfly can only survive within a certain temperature range for the second mark` },
      { q: `In 1935, about 100 cane toads were released in Queensland, Australia, to control beetles that damage sugar cane crops. There are now many millions of cane toads in Australia.\nCane toads produce a poison in their skin.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the cane toad population increased so much.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• no / few natural predators in Australia (1)\n• the poison kills animals that try to eat them (1)\n• plenty of food / they eat many different types of food (1)\n• few competitors (1)\n• no diseases / parasites (that affect them in Australia) (1)\n• suitable climate / habitat (1)\n• they lay large numbers of eggs (1)` },
      { q: `Przewalski's horse is a type of wild horse. It became extinct in the wild in the 20th century. All the Przewalski's horses alive today are descended from a small number of horses that were kept in zoos. Some have now been bred in zoos and released into Mongolia.\n\nGive two problems with using captive breeding programmes to conserve a species.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• low genetic variation / inbreeding (1)\n• so the population is more likely to be wiped out by a disease / change in environment (1)\n• animals released may not survive in the wild / may not know how to find food or avoid predators (1)\n• the natural habitat may have been destroyed (1)\n• expensive / needs a lot of money and space (1)` },
      { q: `Seed banks store seeds from many different varieties of each plant species, including rare wild species.\n\nExplain how seed banks help to maintain biodiversity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `if a species becomes extinct (in the wild), seeds can be grown and the plants reintroduced (1)\nthey store genetic variation / many different alleles within a species (1)\nALLOW they store seeds of species that are endangered for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student estimates the number of buttercups on a field that is 60 m long and 40 m wide. The student places 2 quadrats on the field and counts the buttercups in each.\nA teacher says that the estimate is unlikely to be accurate.\n\nExplain why the estimate is unlikely to be accurate and how the student could improve it.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 quadrats are too few to be representative of the whole field / buttercups may not be evenly spread (1)\nuse more quadrats (e.g. at least 10) placed at random and calculate a mean (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists use capture–recapture to estimate the size of a population of field voles in a meadow.\n\nGive two assumptions the scientists make when they use this method.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• no voles move into or out of the meadow between the two samples / no migration (1)\n• no (or very few) births or deaths between the two samples (1)\n• the marks do not come off (1)\n• the marked voles mix fully with the rest of the population (1)\n• marking does not affect the voles' chance of survival / being caught again (1)` },
      { q: `A charity is planting trees on land in Scotland that was cleared of forest hundreds of years ago. This is called reforestation.\n\nExplain how reforestation can help to reduce global warming.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `trees absorb carbon dioxide (from the air) for photosynthesis (1)\ncarbon is stored in the wood / biomass so there is less (of this greenhouse gas) carbon dioxide in the atmosphere (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists say that protecting tropical rainforests is important for global biodiversity. Many of these rainforests are in countries where a lot of people are poor.\n\nSuggest two reasons why it is difficult to stop deforestation in these countries.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• people need the land to grow food / for farming (1)\n• money can be made by selling the timber / land / crops such as palm oil (1)\n• deforestation provides jobs / income for local people (1)\n• it is difficult / expensive to monitor or police large areas of forest (1)\n• it is difficult to get agreement between countries / governments (1)\n• demand from other countries for wood / beef / soya / palm oil (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists estimated the population of a species of beetle on an island using capture–recapture. They estimated the population to be 600 beetles.\nThey had marked and released 50 beetles in the first sample. In the second sample they caught 84 beetles.\n\nCalculate how many of the beetles in the second sample were marked.\nUse the formula: population = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample\nNumber of marked beetles = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `number marked = (50 × 84) ÷ 600 (1)\n= 7 (1)\nALLOW 7 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `The table shows the total length of hedgerow on a farm (data written for this practice question).\n1950: 800 km\n2000: 520 km\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the length of hedgerow between 1950 and 2000.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `800 − 520 = 280 (km) (1)\n(280 ÷ 800) × 100 = 35 (%) (1)\nALLOW 35 with no working for 2 marks\nDO NOT ALLOW 54 (dividing by 520)` },
      { q: `Lichens are organisms that grow on tree bark and walls. Many species of lichen cannot survive where there is sulfur dioxide in the air.\nA student counted the number of lichen species on trees at different distances from the centre of a city (data written for this practice question).\nDistance from city centre in km: 0, 2, 4, 8\nNumber of lichen species: 1, 3, 6, 12\n\nDescribe the pattern in the data and suggest a reason for it.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `the number of lichen species increases as distance from the city centre increases (1)\nless sulfur dioxide / air pollution further from the city centre (from vehicles / burning fossil fuels) (1)\nALLOW less pollution away from the city for the second mark\nIGNORE better air quality unqualified` },
      { q: `Two students compare the number of insects in a meadow that is mown every week with a meadow that is left uncut.\nIn each meadow, they sweep a sweep net through the grass and count the insects caught.\nOne student sweeps the net 10 times in the mown meadow. The other student sweeps the net 25 times in the uncut meadow.\n\nExplain why this is not a fair comparison and how the method should be changed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `more sweeps will catch more insects / the number of insects depends on the number of sweeps (1)\nuse the same number of sweeps (and same student / same sweeping technique) in each meadow (1)\nALLOW calculate the mean number of insects per sweep for each meadow for the second mark` },
      { q: `A farmer spreads fertiliser containing nitrates on a field next to a river. After heavy rain, some of the fertiliser is washed into the river. A few weeks later, many fish in the river die.\n\nExplain why the fish die.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• nitrates cause algae / water plants to grow quickly (algal bloom) (1)\n• algae block light so plants (below) cannot photosynthesise and die (1)\n• bacteria / decomposers break down the dead plants / algae (1)\n• bacteria respire and use up the oxygen in the water (1)\n• fish cannot get enough oxygen for (aerobic) respiration (1)\nALLOW eutrophication for the first mark if linked to algae\nDO NOT ALLOW fertiliser poisons the fish` },
      { q: `A student estimates the number of clover plants on a field. The field is 50 m long and 40 m wide.\nThe student uses quadrats that are 0.5 m × 0.5 m. The numbers of clover plants in 10 random quadrats were (data written for this practice question):\n3, 5, 0, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3, 2, 4\n\nCalculate the estimated number of clover plants on the field.\nNumber of clover plants = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean per quadrat = 30 ÷ 10 = 3 (1)\narea of field = 2000 m² and area of quadrat = 0.25 m² OR 3 ÷ 0.25 = 12 per m² (1)\n12 × 2000 = 24 000 (1)\nALLOW 2000 ÷ 0.25 = 8000 quadrats, 8000 × 3 = 24 000\nALLOW 24 000 with no working for 3 marks\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect mean` },
      { q: `Some students want to estimate the population of ground beetles in a small wood.\nThey have pitfall traps and a pot of non-toxic paint.\n\nDescribe how the students could use capture–recapture to estimate the population of ground beetles.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `catch a sample of beetles (using pitfall traps), count them and mark each one with paint (1)\nrelease them back where they were caught and wait (e.g. a day) to allow them to mix with the population (1)\ncatch a second sample, count the total number and the number that are marked (1)\nAND\npopulation = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample\nALLOW the formula as a replacement for any one of the first three points\nmax 3` },
      { q: `Grey squirrels were introduced to the UK from North America. Grey squirrels are larger than red squirrels and are better able to digest acorns. Grey squirrels carry the squirrelpox virus. The virus does not usually harm grey squirrels but it kills red squirrels.\n\nExplain why the number of red squirrels has decreased in areas where grey squirrels live.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• grey and red squirrels compete for the same food / habitat / nesting sites (1)\n• grey squirrels are better competitors / get more of the food because they can eat acorns (1)\n• red squirrels have less food so fewer survive / breed (1)\n• grey squirrels pass squirrelpox to red squirrels (1)\n• red squirrels have no immunity / have not evolved resistance to the virus so die (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigates the distribution of grass along a transect from the trunk of a large tree out into an open field. The student measures the light intensity and the percentage cover of grass at each point (data written for this practice question).\nDistance from tree trunk in m: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8\nLight intensity in arbitrary units: 5, 12, 30, 58, 60\nPercentage cover of grass: 2, 10, 35, 80, 85\n\nDescribe the relationship between the light intensity and the percentage cover of grass. Explain the relationship.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `as light intensity increases the percentage cover of grass increases (1)\nuses data e.g. light intensity 5 gives 2% cover but light intensity 60 gives 85% cover (1)\nmore light means more photosynthesis so more glucose for growth (1)\nALLOW the tree's leaves shade the grass near the trunk for the explanation mark if linked to less photosynthesis` },
      { q: `Eurasian beavers became extinct in Britain hundreds of years ago. They have now been reintroduced to some rivers in England and Scotland.\nBeavers cut down small trees and build dams across streams. This creates ponds and wetland areas.\nSome farmers whose land is next to these rivers object to the reintroduction.\n\nSuggest the benefits and the problems of reintroducing beavers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any three from, with at least one benefit and one problem:\nBenefits:\n• the ponds / wetlands provide new habitats (1)\n• increase biodiversity / more species of plants, insects, fish, birds (1)\n• dams slow the flow of water so reduce flooding downstream (1)\nProblems:\n• dams may flood farmland / crops (1)\n• beavers cut down / damage trees on farmland (1)\n• may block the movement of fish such as salmon (1)\nmax 2 for benefits only or problems only` },
      { q: `Scientists use capture–recapture to estimate a population of newts in a pond. They mark the newts with a dye.\nBetween the first and second samples, the dye wears off some of the marked newts.\n\nExplain how this would affect the scientists' estimate of the population.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `fewer marked newts are counted / recognised in the second sample (1)\nso the number divided by in the formula is smaller (1)\nso the population is overestimated / estimate is too large (1)` },
      { q: `The area of forest on an island was 2.4 × 10⁴ km² in 1990. In 2020 the area of forest was 1.5 × 10⁴ km² (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the mean area of forest lost per year between 1990 and 2020.\nMean area lost = ______ km² per year`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `area lost = 2.4 × 10⁴ − 1.5 × 10⁴ = 9.0 × 10³ / 9000 (km²) (1)\nnumber of years = 30 (1)\n9000 ÷ 30 = 300 (km² per year) (1)\nALLOW 300 with no working for 3 marks\nALLOW 3 × 10²` },
      { q: `A student wants to compare the number of daisies growing on an open, sunny part of a lawn with the number on a shaded part of the lawn under some trees.\nThe student has a 1 m × 1 m quadrat and two tape measures.\n\nDescribe how the student should sample the two areas so that the comparison is valid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• place quadrats at random in each area (using random coordinates) (1)\n• use the same size quadrat in both areas (1)\n• use the same number of quadrats in each area, e.g. at least 10 (1)\n• count the daisies in each quadrat (1)\n• calculate the mean number of daisies per quadrat for each area (1)\n• sample both areas on the same day / at the same time of year (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists use capture–recapture to compare the populations of a species of mouse in two woods (data written for this practice question).\nWood A: area 0.5 hectares; 40 mice caught and marked; second sample 35 mice, of which 7 were marked.\nWood B: area 1.2 hectares; 30 mice caught and marked; second sample 48 mice, of which 4 were marked.\n\nThe scientists say that the population density of mice (number per hectare) is greater in Wood A.\nUse calculations to show whether the scientists are correct.\nUse the formula: population = (number in first sample × number in second sample) ÷ number marked in second sample`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Wood A population = (40 × 35) ÷ 7 = 200 (1)\nWood B population = (30 × 48) ÷ 4 = 360 (1)\ndensity A = 200 ÷ 0.5 = 400 per hectare AND density B = 360 ÷ 1.2 = 300 per hectare (1)\nthe scientists are correct / Wood A has the greater density (1)\nALLOW ECF from incorrect population estimates\nconclusion mark must be supported by calculated densities` },
      { q: `The comma butterfly feeds on nettles. Its range in Britain has spread north over the last 50 years as the climate has become warmer. Scientists predict that the mean temperature in the UK will continue to rise.\nSome species of plants and animals that live on the tops of mountains in Scotland can only survive in cold conditions.\n\nExplain how global warming may affect the distribution of species and biodiversity in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any four from:\n• species that need warmer conditions can spread north / to higher ground (1)\n• so new species may arrive in the UK from Europe / biodiversity in some areas may increase (1)\n• species adapted to cold (e.g. on mountain tops) have nowhere colder to move to (1)\n• so these species may decrease / become extinct (in the UK) (1)\n• new species may compete with / prey on / bring diseases to native species (1)\n• food plants / prey may change distribution so animals that depend on them are affected (1)\n• changes in rainfall / flooding / drought may destroy habitats (1)` },
      { q: `In Indonesia, large areas of rainforest have been cut down to grow oil palm trees. Palm oil is used in many foods and cosmetics.\n• Indonesia is one of the largest producers of palm oil in the world.\n• Many people in Indonesia work on oil palm plantations.\n• Orangutans live only in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra and are critically endangered.\n\nEvaluate the use of rainforest land to grow oil palm trees.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any three from, with at least one benefit and one drawback:\nBenefits:\n• provides jobs / income for local people (1)\n• the country earns money from exports (1)\n• provides a useful product / food / fuel (1)\nDrawbacks:\n• loss of habitat for orangutans / other species (1)\n• reduces biodiversity / many tree species replaced by one (monoculture) (1)\n• species such as orangutans may become extinct (1)\n• burning / decay of trees releases carbon dioxide / fewer trees to absorb carbon dioxide, increasing global warming (1)\nAND\na conclusion that is supported by the points given, e.g. palm oil should be grown only on land that is already cleared / with sustainable certification (1)` },
      { q: `*A group of students wants to investigate how the distribution and abundance of seaweeds change from the low tide mark to the high tide mark on a rocky shore.\nThe students have a 30 m tape measure, a 0.5 m × 0.5 m quadrat divided into 25 squares, and an identification key for seaweeds.\n\nDescribe a method the students could use. Include how they would make their results valid and how they would process their results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Describes a detailed method using a transect with quadrats at regular intervals, including how the seaweeds are recorded, AND explains how results are made valid AND how they are processed.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes a method using a transect and quadrats, with some detail on recording AND either validity or processing.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Makes simple statements about using quadrats or a tape measure to sample seaweeds.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• lay the tape measure from the low tide mark to the high tide mark (a line transect)\n• place the quadrat at regular intervals along the tape, e.g. every 2 m\n• use the key to identify the seaweed species in each quadrat\n• count the squares covered by each species to estimate percentage cover\nValidity\n• use the same size quadrat at each point\n• repeat with more transects at different places along the shore\n• carry out the sampling at the same time / on the same day (at low tide)\n• safety: check tide times / work at low tide / wear suitable footwear\nProcessing\n• calculate the mean percentage cover of each species at each distance\n• plot a graph / kite diagram of percentage cover against distance up the shore\n• describe how the distribution changes and link to time out of water / abiotic factors` },
      { q: `*The giant panda lives only in mountain forests in China. It feeds almost entirely on bamboo.\n• In the past, many forests were cut down for farming and roads, splitting the panda's habitat into small, separate areas.\n• China has set up more than 60 nature reserves for pandas and has planted bamboo forest corridors to link some of the reserves.\n• Zoos in China run captive breeding programmes. Some pandas bred in captivity have been released into the wild.\n• In 2016 the giant panda was moved from the 'endangered' category to the less serious 'vulnerable' category.\n\nExplain the benefits and challenges of the methods used to conserve the giant panda.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains benefits AND challenges of BOTH habitat protection (reserves / corridors) AND captive breeding.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains benefits AND challenges of one method OR gives benefits of both methods.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives simple statements about a benefit or a challenge of one method.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nNature reserves and corridors – benefits\n• protect the habitat and bamboo (food) from being cut down\n• protect pandas from hunting / poaching\n• corridors let pandas move between areas to find food and mates\n• increases gene flow / genetic variation between small populations\n• protects many other species in the same forests, increasing biodiversity\nNature reserves and corridors – challenges\n• land cannot be used for farming / development, so conflict with local people\n• expensive to set up and to monitor / patrol\n• bamboo flowers and dies off over large areas at intervals, so pandas in small reserves may starve\nCaptive breeding – benefits\n• increases panda numbers / prevents extinction\n• allows pandas to be released to boost wild populations\n• raises public awareness / money for conservation\nCaptive breeding – challenges\n• small number of breeding animals so low genetic variation / inbreeding\n• pandas breed slowly / are difficult to breed in captivity\n• released pandas may not survive / not know how to find food or avoid danger\n• very expensive\n• no use without suitable habitat to release them into` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.2': {
    name: 'Feeding the Human Race',
    questions: [
      { q: `Aphids are small insects that feed on the sap of crop plants. They reduce the yield of the crop.\nFarmers can use different methods to reduce the number of aphids.\n\nWhich method is an example of biological control?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nadding nitrate fertiliser / growing the plants in a nutrient solution / releasing ladybirds that eat aphids / spraying the crop with insecticide`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `releasing ladybirds that eat aphids ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `Lettuces can be grown using hydroponics. The roots of the lettuces hang in water that contains dissolved mineral ions. No soil is used.\nThe farmer adds nitrate ions to the water.\n\nWhy do the lettuce plants need nitrate ions?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `to make amino acids / proteins (for growth) ✓\nALLOW to make DNA / nucleic acids\nIGNORE for energy / for food / to make chlorophyll` },
      { q: `The United Nations estimates that the world's human population will reach 9 700 000 000 in the year 2050.\nAn increasing human population is one of the biggest threats to food security.\n\nGive the estimated population in 2050 in standard form.\nPopulation = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `9.7 × 10⁹ ✓\nALLOW 9.70 × 10⁹\nDO NOT ALLOW 97 × 10⁸` },
      { q: `Holstein cows are kept on dairy farms because they produce large volumes of milk.\nFor many years, farmers chose the cows that produced the most milk and bred them with bulls whose mothers also produced a lot of milk. They repeated this with the offspring for many generations.\n\nName this process.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `selective breeding ✓\nALLOW artificial selection\nDO NOT ALLOW natural selection / genetic engineering` },
      { q: `Scientists can use genetic engineering to insert a human gene into a bacterial plasmid.\nThe plasmid and the human gene are both cut with the same restriction enzyme.\n\nName the enzyme that is then used to join the human gene into the plasmid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `(DNA) ligase ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW restriction enzyme / polymerase` },
      { q: `The North Sea cod population fell to very low numbers in the early 2000s because of overfishing.\nOne rule introduced to help the cod population recover was that fishing boats must use nets with a larger mesh size (bigger holes).\n\nSuggest why using nets with a larger mesh size helps the cod population to recover.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `young / small fish escape (so they can grow and) breed ✓\nALLOW so fish survive to reproductive age\nIGNORE fewer fish are caught (unqualified)` },
      { q: `The United Nations estimated that the world's human population was 8.0 billion in 2022. The population is predicted to be 9.7 billion in 2050.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the world's population between 2022 and 2050.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 21 (%) award 2 marks\n(9.7 – 8.0) ÷ 8.0 (× 100) ✓\n= 21 (%) ✓\nALLOW 21.25 / 21.3 for 1 mark (not 2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `The table shows the area of land needed to produce 100 g of protein from different foods (data written for this practice question).\n\nFood | Land needed to produce 100 g of protein (m²)\nBeef | 164\nPork | 11\nChicken | 7.1\nPeas | 3.4\n\nCalculate how many times more land is needed to produce 100 g of protein from beef than from peas.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 48 award 2 marks\n164 ÷ 3.4 ✓\n= 48 ✓\nALLOW 48.2 for 1 mark (not rounded to nearest whole number)` },
      { q: `Wheat stem rust is a disease of wheat caused by a fungus. In 1999 a new strain of the fungus, called Ug99, was found in Uganda. Most of the wheat varieties grown around the world have no resistance to Ug99. The fungus produces millions of spores that are carried long distances by the wind.\n\nExplain why new pathogens such as Ug99 are a threat to global food security.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\nspores spread (quickly) to other countries / over large areas ✓\nmost crops / wheat varieties are not resistant so many plants are infected ✓\n(infected plants) give a lower yield / die so less food is produced ✓\nwheat is a staple food for many people / large areas grow the same variety ✓\nIGNORE it is a disease (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Some farmers grow tomatoes using hydroponics in large greenhouses. The plants grow in a nutrient solution instead of soil. Pumps circulate the solution and computers control the concentration of mineral ions.\n\nSuggest two disadvantages of growing tomatoes using hydroponics.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nexpensive to set up / buy equipment ✓\nneeds electricity / high energy costs (for pumps / lighting / heating) ✓\nif the pump / power fails the plants could die quickly ✓\nneeds skilled workers / monitoring ✓\npathogens can spread (quickly) through the shared solution ✓\nnutrient solution must be bought / replaced (costly) ✓\nIGNORE no soil` },
      { q: `A grower counted the number of aphids on 20 pepper plants in a greenhouse. Ladybirds were then released into the greenhouse.\nThe table shows the results (data written for this practice question).\n\nWeek | Mean number of aphids per plant\n0 (ladybirds released) | 480\n2 | 310\n4 | 140\n6 | 60\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the mean number of aphids per plant between week 0 and week 6.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 87.5 award 2 marks\n(480 – 60) ÷ 480 (× 100) ✓\n= 87.5 (%) ✓\nALLOW 88 / 87 (%)\nALLOW 420 ÷ 480 for the first mark` },
      { q: `In 1935, about 100 cane toads were released in Queensland, Australia, to control beetles that were eating sugar cane crops. The cane toads did not reduce the number of beetles. Today there are more than 200 million cane toads in Australia. Cane toads are poisonous to many animals that try to eat them.\n\nExplain why using the cane toad for biological control caused problems in Australia.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\ncane toads had no (natural) predators (so population increased rapidly) ✓\ncane toads ate / competed with other (native) species instead of the beetles ✓\nnative predators that ate the toads were poisoned / died ✓\nso numbers of native species / biodiversity decreased ✓\nALLOW cane toads became an invasive species / spread over large areas for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Farmers add fertilisers containing nitrate ions to the soil in their fields.\n\nExplain how adding nitrate ions increases the yield of a wheat crop.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `nitrate (ions) used to make amino acids / proteins ✓\n(proteins needed) for growth / to make new cells so the plants grow bigger / produce more grain ✓\nIGNORE nitrates give the plant energy / are food for the plant\nALLOW to make chlorophyll / DNA for first marking point only if linked to nitrogen` },
      { q: `A farmer investigated the effect of adding different masses of nitrogen fertiliser on the yield of wheat. The table shows the results (data written for this practice question).\n\nMass of nitrogen added (kg per hectare) | Yield of wheat (tonnes per hectare)\n0 | 4.2\n50 | 6.0\n100 | 7.5\n150 | 8.3\n200 | 8.5\n250 | 8.4\n\nDescribe the relationship between the mass of nitrogen added and the yield of wheat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `as the mass of nitrogen increases the yield increases (up to 200 kg per hectare) ✓\nthe increase gets smaller / yield levels off above 150 / 200 kg per hectare ✓\nALLOW yield falls slightly / stays the same at 250 kg per hectare\nALLOW correct use of data to support either point` },
      { q: `A farmer grows wheat. Adding 250 kg of nitrogen fertiliser per hectare does not increase the yield any more than adding 200 kg per hectare.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the farmer should not add more than 200 kg of nitrogen fertiliser per hectare.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nwastes money / costs more but no increase in yield / profit ✓\nexcess fertiliser washed / leached into rivers / lakes ✓\ncausing eutrophication / growth of algae / death of fish ✓\nsome other factor (e.g. water / light / another mineral) is now limiting growth ✓\nIGNORE pollution (unqualified)` },
      { q: `The table shows the mean milk yield of dairy cows on UK farms (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Mean milk yield per cow (litres per year)\n1960 | 3500\n1980 | 4800\n2000 | 6500\n2020 | 8200\n\nMuch of this increase is the result of selective breeding.\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean milk yield between 1960 and 2020.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 134 award 2 marks\n(8200 – 3500) ÷ 3500 (× 100) ✓\n= 134 (%) ✓\nALLOW 134.3 for 1 mark (not rounded)\nALLOW 4700 ÷ 3500 for the first mark` },
      { q: `Most dairy cows in the UK are Holsteins. Many of them are descended from a small number of bulls that were selectively bred for high milk yield.\n\nExplain one disadvantage of selectively breeding dairy cows in this way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reduces genetic variation / increases inbreeding ✓\nso the whole herd / population may be susceptible to a new disease / cannot adapt to change ✓\nOR\nincreases chance of inheriting harmful recessive alleles / genetic disorders ✓\nso more cows have health problems ✓\nALLOW welfare problems (e.g. lameness / udder infection) linked to high yield for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Bt maize is a genetically modified crop. It contains a gene from the bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis. The gene codes for a protein that is toxic to the caterpillars of some insects that feed on maize.\n\nExplain how growing Bt maize could increase the yield of maize.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `caterpillars / insect pests that eat the maize are killed ✓\nso less damage to leaves / plants (so more photosynthesis / growth) ✓\nALLOW fewer insects eat the crop for 1 mark\nIGNORE plant is resistant to disease` },
      { q: `Some oilseed rape plants have been genetically modified to be resistant to a herbicide (weedkiller). Farmers can spray the herbicide on the field and kill the weeds without killing the crop.\nOilseed rape can cross-pollinate with some wild plants that grow near the fields.\n\nExplain one possible risk to the environment of growing this GM oilseed rape.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(pollen carries) the herbicide-resistance gene to wild plants / weeds ✓\nproducing weeds that cannot be killed by the herbicide / superweeds ✓\nOR\nmore herbicide used so (fewer) weeds / wild flowers ✓\nreducing food / habitat for insects / reduces biodiversity ✓` },
      { q: `In many countries rice is the main food that people eat. Some people in these countries do not get enough vitamin A. Lack of vitamin A can cause blindness.\nScientists have produced Golden Rice. Genes have been inserted into the rice so that the rice grains make beta-carotene. The body uses beta-carotene to make vitamin A.\n\nExplain how growing Golden Rice could improve the health of people in these countries.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `people eat rice (every day) so they get beta-carotene / vitamin A from their main food ✓\nfewer people become blind / have vitamin A deficiency ✓\nIGNORE more food / better nutrition (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Scientists want to insert a gene into a plasmid. They cut the DNA containing the gene and the plasmid with the same restriction enzyme.\n\nExplain why they use the same restriction enzyme to cut both pieces of DNA.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(the same enzyme) produces the same / matching / complementary sticky ends ✓\nso the bases on the sticky ends pair up / the gene and the plasmid can join ✓\nALLOW the enzyme cuts at the same base sequence for first marking point` },
      { q: `Scientists mixed some bacteria with plasmids containing a gene for a useful protein. The plasmids also contained a gene for resistance to an antibiotic. Only a few of the bacteria took up a plasmid.\nThe scientists then grew the bacteria on agar containing the antibiotic.\n\nExplain why the bacteria were grown on agar containing the antibiotic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `only bacteria that have taken up the plasmid are resistant / survive ✓\nso these contain the useful gene / the scientists can select the modified bacteria ✓\nIGNORE to kill harmful bacteria` },
      { q: `Mycoprotein is a protein-rich food made by growing the fungus Fusarium venenatum in large fermenters. The fungus is fed on glucose.\nThe table compares mycoprotein and beef mince (data written for this practice question).\n\nFood (per 100 g) | Protein (g) | Fat (g) | Fibre (g) | Land needed to produce 1 kg (m²)\nMycoprotein | 11 | 3 | 6 | 2\nBeef mince | 19 | 16 | 0 | 160\n\nUse the data to give two advantages of eating mycoprotein instead of beef mince.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nless fat (3 g compared to 16 g) ✓\nmore / contains fibre (6 g compared to 0 g) ✓\nneeds much less land (2 m² compared to 160 m²) ✓\nALLOW less land so more land available to grow other crops\nDO NOT ALLOW more protein` },
      { q: `The table shows estimates of the mass of adult cod in the North Sea (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Mass of adult cod (tonnes)\n1970 | 270 000\n1990 | 80 000\n2006 | 40 000\n2020 | 75 000\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the mass of adult cod between 1970 and 2006.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 85 award 2 marks\n(270 000 – 40 000) ÷ 270 000 (× 100) ✓\n= 85 (%) ✓\nALLOW 85.2 for 1 mark (not 2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `Between 2020 and 2022 the price of nitrogen fertiliser in Europe rose sharply. Fertilisers, pesticides, fuel and animal feed are called agricultural inputs.\n\nSuggest how a large increase in the cost of agricultural inputs could affect food security.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `farmers use less fertiliser / fewer inputs (because they cannot afford them) ✓\nso crop yields fall / less food is produced ✓\nOR\nfood prices increase ✓\nso poorer people cannot afford enough food ✓\nALLOW any two linked points for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Some companies that make GM crop seeds hold a patent on the seeds. Farmers who grow the GM crop must buy new seed from the company every year and cannot save seed from their own harvest to plant.\n\nSuggest two reasons why some farmers in poorer countries are against growing GM crops.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nGM seed is (more) expensive / cost of buying seed every year ✓\nfarmers become dependent on large companies ✓\nmay not be able to sell the crop / some consumers / countries will not buy GM food ✓\nconcern that genes spread to their non-GM / traditional crops ✓\nmay need to buy expensive herbicides / pesticides to go with the crop ✓\nIGNORE GM food is unsafe (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A plant breeder has two varieties of wheat.\n• Variety A has a high yield but is easily damaged by a fungal disease.\n• Variety B has a low yield but is resistant to the fungal disease.\n\nDescribe how the plant breeder could use selective breeding to produce a variety of wheat with a high yield that is resistant to the disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `cross / breed variety A with variety B ✓\nselect / choose the offspring with both high yield and disease resistance ✓\nbreed these offspring together ✓\nrepeat (selecting and breeding) over many generations ✓\nmax 3\nIGNORE genetic engineering / cloning` },
      { q: `A hydroponic farm produces 12 000 kg of lettuce each year.\nGrowing lettuce in soil uses 250 litres of water for each kg of lettuce. Growing lettuce using hydroponics uses 20 litres of water for each kg of lettuce (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the volume of water the farm saves each year by using hydroponics instead of soil.\nGive your answer in standard form.\nVolume of water saved = ______ litres`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 2.76 × 10⁶ award 3 marks\n250 – 20 = 230 (litres per kg) ✓\n230 × 12 000 = 2 760 000 (litres) ✓\n2.76 × 10⁶ (litres) ✓\nALLOW ECF for standard form from an incorrect value\nALLOW (250 × 12 000) – (20 × 12 000) for the first two marks` },
      { q: `A farmer sprays the same insecticide on a potato crop every year. After several years the insecticide no longer kills most of the beetles that feed on the crop.\n\nExplain how the population of beetles has become resistant to the insecticide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(random) mutation / variation means some beetles are resistant ✓\nresistant beetles survive (the spraying) and the others die ✓\nresistant beetles reproduce / breed and pass on the allele for resistance ✓\n(over many generations) the proportion of resistant beetles increases ✓\nmax 3\nDO NOT ALLOW beetles become resistant because they are exposed to insecticide / learn to be resistant` },
      { q: `Bt cotton is a GM crop that produces a toxin that kills cotton bollworm caterpillars.\nA farmer has 40 hectares of cotton. The table shows information about growing conventional cotton and Bt cotton (data written for this practice question).\n\nType of cotton | Number of insecticide sprays per season | Cost of each spray (£ per hectare)\nConventional cotton | 12 | 18\nBt cotton | 4 | 18\n\nCalculate how much money the farmer would save on insecticide sprays in one season by growing Bt cotton on all 40 hectares.\nMoney saved = £ ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 5760 award 3 marks\n12 – 4 = 8 (fewer sprays) ✓\n8 × 18 = £144 (per hectare) ✓\n144 × 40 = £5760 ✓\nALLOW (12 × 18 × 40) – (4 × 18 × 40) = 8640 – 2880\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect intermediate value` },
      { q: `Broiler chickens are chickens bred for meat. The table shows the mean mass of two types of broiler chicken at 42 days old when both were fed the same diet (data written for this practice question).\n\nType of chicken | Mean mass at 42 days (kg)\nBreed used in 1957 | 0.8\nBreed used in 2005 | 2.7\n\nThe 2005 breed was produced by selective breeding. Many birds of the 2005 breed have leg problems and difficulty walking.\n\nExplain the impacts of the selective breeding of broiler chickens.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2005 breed is (about) 3.4 times heavier / 1.9 kg heavier (at 42 days) ✓\nso more meat (produced in less time / from the same amount of food) / cheaper food ✓\nbut rapid growth / heavy body causes leg problems / pain / poor welfare (ethical concern) ✓\nALLOW reduced genetic variation so the breed is more likely to be affected by disease\nmax 3; for full marks must include a benefit and a problem` },
      { q: `A farmer grows wheat. The table shows the yield of wheat with different masses of nitrogen fertiliser (data written for this practice question).\n\nMass of nitrogen added (kg per hectare) | Yield of wheat (tonnes per hectare)\n100 | 7.5\n150 | 8.3\n200 | 8.5\n\nThe farmer sells wheat for £200 per tonne. Nitrogen fertiliser costs £1.20 per kg.\n\nCalculate the extra profit per hectare the farmer makes by adding 150 kg of nitrogen per hectare instead of 100 kg per hectare.\nExtra profit = £ ______ per hectare`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `FIRST CHECK ANSWER ON ANSWER LINE\nIf answer = 100 award 3 marks\nextra income: (8.3 – 7.5) × 200 = £160 ✓\nextra fertiliser cost: 50 × 1.20 = £60 ✓\nextra profit = 160 – 60 = £100 ✓\nALLOW ECF from incorrect extra income or extra cost` },
      { q: `Scientists use genetic engineering to produce bacteria that make chymosin. Chymosin is an enzyme used to make cheese.\nThe first step is to identify the gene for chymosin in calf cells.\nThe other steps are shown below in the wrong order.\nP  DNA ligase joins the gene into a plasmid\nQ  bacteria are grown on agar containing an antibiotic\nR  the gene is cut out of the DNA with a restriction enzyme, leaving sticky ends\nS  the selected bacteria are grown in a large fermenter\nT  the plasmid is inserted into a host bacterium\n\nWrite the letters in the correct order.\nFirst step: identify the gene → ___ → ___ → ___ → ___ → ___`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `R  P  T  Q  S\nall five letters in the correct order ✓✓✓\nthree or four letters in the correct position ✓✓\none or two letters in the correct position ✓` },
      { q: `In wealthier countries, people eat more meat and dairy products than they did 50 years ago. Diets are also changing in this way in many countries where people are becoming wealthier.\n\nExplain why these changes in diet are a threat to global food security.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `energy is lost at each trophic level (e.g. through respiration / movement / heat / waste) ✓\nso more crops / land are needed to feed animals than to feed people directly ✓\nless land / crops available to grow food for people ✓\nso fewer people can be fed (from the same area of land) / food prices rise ✓\nmax 3\nALLOW only about 10% of energy is transferred to the next trophic level for marking point 1` },
      { q: `Tomato growers in greenhouses can control whitefly pests by spraying an insecticide or by releasing a small wasp that lays its eggs inside whitefly larvae. The table compares the two methods (data written for this practice question).\n\nFeature | Insecticide | Parasitic wasp\nCost per hectare per season (£) | 900 | 1400\nReduction in whitefly numbers after 4 weeks (%) | 95 | 80\nInsecticide residue on fruit | yes | no\nKills bees that pollinate the tomatoes | yes | no\n\nUse the data to compare the two methods of controlling whitefly.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `insecticide is cheaper (by £500 per hectare) ✓\ninsecticide reduces whitefly numbers more (95% compared to 80%) ✓\nwasp leaves no residue on the fruit (safer to eat) ✓\nwasp does not kill bees so pollination / fruit production continues ✓\nmax 3\nALLOW wasps reproduce so control can continue / whitefly cannot become resistant to the wasp (ignore if not compared)` },
      { q: `A student wrote a description of how scientists could use genetic engineering to insert a gene from a fish into bacteria.\n1 The gene is cut out of the fish DNA using a ligase enzyme.\n2 This leaves sticky ends on the gene.\n3 The plasmid is cut with a different restriction enzyme.\n4 Restriction enzymes are used to join the gene into the plasmid.\n5 The bacteria are grown on agar containing glucose so that only bacteria that took up the plasmid grow.\n\nThe description contains four errors.\nIdentify each error and correct it.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `statement 1: should be a restriction enzyme (not ligase) ✓\nstatement 3: should be the same restriction enzyme ✓\nstatement 4: (DNA) ligase joins the gene into the plasmid ✓\nstatement 5: agar should contain an antibiotic (plasmid carries an antibiotic-resistance gene / marker) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW a mark for identifying statement 2 as an error` },
      { q: `A company wants to build a vertical farm near a city to grow lettuce using hydroponics under LED lights. The table compares growing lettuce in fields and in the vertical farm (data written for this practice question).\n\nFeature | Field (soil) | Vertical farm (hydroponics)\nYield (kg per m² per year) | 4 | 40\nWater used (litres per kg) | 250 | 20\nEnergy used (kWh per kg) | 0.3 | 12\nCost to set up (£ per m²) | 10 | 600\nPesticide used | yes | no\n\nEvaluate the use of the vertical farm to grow lettuce.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any four from (max 3 for only advantages or only disadvantages):\nAdvantages:\n10 times more yield per m² / uses less land ✓\nuses 12.5 times less water / 230 litres less per kg ✓\nno pesticide so no residues / no harm to other organisms ✓\ngrown near city so less transport / fresher / grown all year round ✓\nDisadvantages:\nuses 40 times more energy / 11.7 kWh more per kg (fossil fuels / carbon dioxide emissions / high costs) ✓\nmuch more expensive to set up (£590 more per m² / 60 times more) ✓\nALLOW a reasoned conclusion supported by data as one of the four marks` },
      { q: `North Sea cod were overfished in the 1980s and 1990s. In the early 2000s strict fishing quotas were introduced. A quota limits the mass of fish each boat can catch. The table shows data about North Sea cod (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Mass of adult cod (tonnes) | Total cod allowed to be caught (tonnes)\n1990 | 80 000 | 105 000\n2000 | 50 000 | 81 000\n2006 | 40 000 | 23 000\n2015 | 90 000 | 29 000\n\nExplain how the data supports the idea that quotas helped the cod population to recover.\nSuggest one other way that the cod population could be kept sustainable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `quota reduced (from 81 000 to 23 000 tonnes / by about 70%) between 2000 and 2006 ✓\nafter this the mass of adult cod increased (from 40 000 to 90 000 tonnes by 2015) ✓\nexplanation: fewer fish caught so more fish survive to breed / reproduce (faster than they are removed) ✓\nother way: any one from:\nlarger net mesh size so young fish escape ✓\nclosing areas / seasons (e.g. breeding grounds / spawning season) to fishing ✓\nfish farming / reduce demand / limit number of fishing days or boats ✓\nALLOW correct data comparison for either of the first two marks` },
      { q: `*Bt cotton is genetically modified to produce a protein that kills bollworm caterpillars. It was first grown in India in 2002. The table shows data about cotton farming in India (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | Area of cotton that is Bt cotton (%) | Mean cotton yield (kg per hectare) | Insecticide used on cotton (thousand tonnes)\n2002 | 1 | 300 | 4.5\n2008 | 80 | 520 | 2.0\n2015 | 95 | 480 | 3.2\n\nBy 2015, scientists found that pink bollworm caterpillars in many areas were no longer killed by the Bt protein. Bt cotton seed costs farmers more than conventional seed.\n\nDiscuss the benefits and risks of growing GM crops such as Bt cotton.\nUse the data and your own knowledge. Include practical and ethical considerations.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed discussion of benefits AND risks, using data from the table, including practical AND ethical considerations, with a reasoned conclusion. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes benefits and risks with some use of data, OR a detailed account of one side using data with some reference to practical or ethical issues. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a benefit or a risk, OR quotes data with little interpretation. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nBenefits / data:\n• yield increased from 300 to 520 kg per hectare (2002–2008) as Bt cotton increased from 1% to 80%\n• insecticide use fell from 4.5 to 2.0 thousand tonnes\n• less insecticide: cheaper, less harm to non-target insects / farm workers\n• more income for farmers / food security for farming families\nRisks / data:\n• by 2015 yield fell to 480 and insecticide use rose to 3.2 thousand tonnes\n• pink bollworm evolved resistance (natural selection) so the advantage is lost\n• Bt protein may harm non-target insects / reduce biodiversity\n• genes could spread to wild relatives\nPractical / ethical:\n• seed costs more / must be bought every year / farmers in debt / dependence on companies\n• patents held by large companies\n• long-term effects not fully known / some people think it is wrong to move genes between species\n• a mix of crops / refuges of non-Bt plants could slow resistance` },
      { q: `*A farmer in a country with a fast-growing population wants to grow more tomatoes in a greenhouse. The farmer is considering three methods. The table shows the results of trials (data written for this practice question).\n\nMethod | Mean yield (kg of tomatoes per plant) | Extra cost per plant per year (£)\nSoil with no extra treatment | 4.0 | 0.00\nSoil with fertiliser added | 6.5 | 0.40\nSoil with biological control of whitefly | 5.2 | 0.30\nHydroponics | 8.0 | 1.50\n\nExplain how each method could increase the yield of tomatoes, and evaluate which method the farmer should choose.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains how ALL three methods increase yield AND evaluates using data (e.g. yield and cost compared) to reach a justified choice. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains how at least two methods increase yield AND makes some use of data in a comparison, OR explains all three with little use of data. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statement of how one method increases yield OR quotes some data. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nExplanation:\n• fertiliser supplies mineral ions, e.g. nitrate for amino acids / proteins, so more growth\n• biological control: predator / parasite reduces whitefly pests, so less damage to leaves, more photosynthesis\n• hydroponics: mineral ions controlled at optimum concentration, no soil pathogens / weeds, so faster growth\nEvaluation using data:\n• hydroponics gives highest yield (8.0 kg, double the untreated soil) but highest cost (£1.50 per plant)\n• fertiliser gives 2.5 kg extra for £0.40 = about £0.16 per extra kg\n• biological control gives 1.2 kg extra for £0.30 = £0.25 per extra kg\n• hydroponics gives 4.0 kg extra for £1.50 = about £0.38 per extra kg\n• fertiliser is the most cost-effective; hydroponics the most food per plant\n• methods could be combined (e.g. fertiliser and biological control)\n• fertiliser run-off risk of eutrophication; hydroponics needs energy / equipment / skills; biological control avoids chemical residues / resistance\n• justified conclusion, e.g. fertiliser plus biological control for a farmer with little money` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.3': {
    name: 'Monitoring & Maintaining Health',
    questions: [
      { q: `Ash dieback is a disease of ash trees. It was first found in the UK in 2012. Infected trees lose their leaves and have dark patches on their bark, and many trees die.\n\nWhich type of pathogen causes ash dieback?\nPut a ring around the correct option.\nbacterium / fungus / protist / virus`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `fungus ✓\n\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed` },
      { q: `Malaria is a disease caused by a protist. The protist is carried from an infected person to another person by female Anopheles mosquitoes when they feed on blood.\n\nWhat name is given to an organism, such as the mosquito, that carries a pathogen from one organism to another?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `vector ✓\n\nIGNORE carrier / host / parasite` },
      { q: `A doctor finds that a 58-year-old man has a high level of cholesterol in his blood. The doctor prescribes a drug that the man must take every day to lower his blood cholesterol and reduce his risk of a heart attack.\n\nName the type of drug the doctor prescribes.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `statin(s) ✓\n\nALLOW a named statin, e.g. simvastatin / atorvastatin\nIGNORE anticoagulant / beta blocker / aspirin` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system. In 2022 about 39 million people across the world were living with HIV.\n\nGive the number of people living with HIV in standard form.\nNumber of people = ______`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `3.9 × 10⁷ ✓\n\nALLOW 3.90 × 10⁷\nDO NOT ALLOW 39 × 10⁶` },
      { q: `Scientists make monoclonal antibodies by fusing a mouse lymphocyte that makes one type of antibody with a tumour cell. The fused cell divides many times and makes large amounts of the antibody.\n\nName the type of cell formed when the lymphocyte and the tumour cell are fused.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `hybridoma (cell) ✓\n\nALLOW phonetic spelling, e.g. hybridomer\nIGNORE hybrid cell` },
      { q: `Tears are made all the time to keep the surface of the eye moist. Tears contain an enzyme called lysozyme.\n\nSuggest how lysozyme protects the eye from infection.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `digests / breaks down / kills bacteria ✓\n\nALLOW breaks down bacterial cell walls\nALLOW destroys pathogens / microorganisms\nIGNORE washes away bacteria\nIGNORE kills viruses` },
      { q: `Tuberculosis (TB) is a lung disease caused by a bacterium. The World Health Organization reports that people who are infected with HIV are many times more likely to develop TB than people without HIV.\n\nExplain why people infected with HIV are more likely to develop TB.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `HIV attacks / destroys white blood cells / lymphocytes / the immune system ✓\nso the body cannot fight off / destroy the TB bacteria ✓\n\nALLOW immune system is weakened for first marking point\nALLOW fewer antibodies made against the TB bacteria for second marking point\nIGNORE HIV causes TB\nDO NOT ALLOW HIV is a bacterium` },
      { q: `Human papillomavirus (HPV) is spread by sexual contact. Some types of HPV can cause cells in the cervix to become cancerous. In the UK, children aged 12–13 are offered a vaccine against HPV.\n\nExplain how the HPV vaccination programme will reduce the number of cases of cervical cancer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(vaccinated) people become immune to / will not be infected by HPV ✓\nso fewer cervix cells are infected / changed by HPV so fewer become cancerous ✓\n\nALLOW less spread of HPV (in the population) for first marking point\nALLOW vaccine stimulates production of antibodies / memory cells against HPV for first marking point\nIGNORE vaccine kills cancer cells\nIGNORE vaccine prevents cancer unqualified` },
      { q: `After a flood, many people in a town had to live in a camp with tents. Within two weeks there was an outbreak of cholera in the camp. Cholera is caused by a bacterium that is spread in water contaminated with faeces.\n\nSuggest two ways that the spread of cholera in the camp could be reduced.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nprovide clean / treated / bottled drinking water ✓\nboil water before drinking it ✓\nprovide toilets / sanitation so that faeces do not get into the drinking water ✓\nwash hands (with soap) after using the toilet / before eating ✓\nisolate / treat people with cholera ✓\n\nALLOW chlorinate water for treated water\nALLOW give antibiotics to infected people\nIGNORE vaccinate unqualified\nIGNORE wash hands unqualified as a second answer if the first was also hygiene` },
      { q: `Athlete's foot is a disease caused by a fungus. It causes itchy, cracked skin between the toes. It is common in people who use swimming pools.\n\nExplain why athlete's foot spreads easily in the changing rooms of swimming pools.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(fungus spreads by) direct contact / contact with floors / towels touched by infected people ✓\nconditions are warm and damp / moist so the fungus grows / survives well ✓\n\nALLOW people walk barefoot for first marking point\nALLOW spores are left on the floor for first marking point\nIGNORE spread by air / water droplets` },
      { q: `A farmer grows barley. One summer, white powdery patches appear on the leaves of many plants in one corner of the field. A scientist thinks the plants have barley powdery mildew, a disease caused by a fungus.\n\nDescribe two ways the scientist could identify the pathogen that is causing the disease.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\ncompare the symptoms with a reference / identification guide / key ✓\nuse a (monoclonal) antibody / diagnostic test kit (in the field) ✓\nsend a sample to a laboratory for DNA analysis / testing ✓\nlook at the pathogen / spores under a microscope ✓\ngrow / culture the pathogen (from the leaves) in a laboratory ✓\n\nIGNORE look at the leaves unqualified\nALLOW analyse the distribution of affected plants in the field` },
      { q: `Many plants have structures that act as physical barriers to pathogens. The leaves of holly plants are covered with a thick waxy cuticle. All plant cells are surrounded by a cell wall.\n\nExplain how the waxy cuticle and the cell wall help to protect the plant from pathogens.\nWaxy cuticle: ______\nCell wall: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `waxy cuticle: (a barrier that) stops pathogens entering the leaf / reaching the cells ✓\ncell wall: (made of) cellulose that is strong / tough so pathogens cannot easily get into the cell ✓\n\nALLOW waterproof so pathogens / spores cannot grow on the surface for waxy cuticle\nALLOW prevents entry of pathogens into the cell for cell wall\nIGNORE protects the plant unqualified` },
      { q: `Barley powdery mildew spreads when spores are carried by the wind from infected plants to healthy plants. On many farms, one variety of barley is grown in very large fields year after year.\n\nSuggest why growing one variety of barley in very large fields increases the spread of powdery mildew.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `plants are close together so spores easily reach / land on healthy plants ✓\nall the plants are genetically similar / identical so if one is not resistant then none are resistant ✓\n\nALLOW lots of hosts for the fungus\nALLOW no resistant plants to slow the spread\nIGNORE plants are weak` },
      { q: `Phagocytes are white blood cells. When bacteria enter a cut on the skin, phagocytes move to the site of the infection.\n\nDescribe how phagocytes destroy the bacteria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `phagocyte engulfs / surrounds / ingests the bacteria ✓\n(phagocyte) digests / breaks down the bacteria (using enzymes) ✓\n\nALLOW phagocytosis for first marking point\nALLOW bacteria are taken into a vacuole\nDO NOT ALLOW phagocytes produce antibodies` },
      { q: `Diphtheria is a disease caused by a bacterium. The bacteria release a toxin that damages tissue in the throat and heart. When a person is infected, some lymphocytes produce antitoxins.\n\nExplain how antitoxins help to protect the person from the effects of diphtheria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `antitoxins bind to / combine with the toxin ✓\nneutralising it / so it cannot damage cells / tissues ✓\n\nALLOW antitoxins are specific to the toxin\nIGNORE antitoxins kill the bacteria\nDO NOT ALLOW antitoxins kill the toxin` },
      { q: `BMI can be used to find out if a person is underweight, a healthy weight, overweight or obese.\nBMI = mass (kg) ÷ (height (m))²\n\nBMI value: below 18.5 underweight; 18.5–24.9 healthy weight; 25.0–29.9 overweight; 30.0 and above obese.\n\nA woman has a mass of 84.5 kg and a height of 1.68 m.\n\nCalculate the woman's BMI and use the table to identify which group she is in.\nBMI = ______\nGroup = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `29.9 ✓\noverweight ✓\n\nALLOW 29.94 or 29.939… for first marking point\nALLOW ECF for group from an incorrect BMI\nDO NOT ALLOW 50.3 (84.5 ÷ 1.68)` },
      { q: `Waist-to-hip ratio is used to estimate the risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes. A woman with a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.85 has an increased risk.\n\nA woman has a waist measurement of 88 cm and a hip measurement of 102 cm.\n\nCalculate her waist-to-hip ratio and state whether she has an increased risk.\nWaist-to-hip ratio = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.86 ✓\n(yes) increased risk because the ratio is above 0.85 ✓\n\nALLOW 0.863 or 0.8627…\nALLOW ECF for conclusion from an incorrect ratio\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.16 (102 ÷ 88) for first marking point` },
      { q: `A student places a paper disc soaked in an antibiotic on an agar plate that has been spread with bacteria. After incubation, there is a circular clear zone around the disc with a diameter of 18 mm.\nArea of a circle = πr²\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.\nArea = ______ mm²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `r = 9 (mm) or π × 9² ✓\n254 (mm²) ✓\n\nALLOW 254.5 for 1 mark only (not to nearest whole number)\nALLOW 254.3 / 254 if π = 3.14 used\nan answer of 1018 (π × 18², diameter used as radius) scores 0 marks` },
      { q: `A student grows bacteria on agar plates in a school laboratory. The teacher tells the student to incubate the plates at 25 °C, not at 37 °C.\n\nExplain why the plates should be incubated at 25 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `37 °C is human body temperature ✓\nso pathogens / harmful bacteria (that could infect humans) are less likely to grow at 25 °C ✓\n\nALLOW reduces risk of growing human pathogens for second marking point\nIGNORE to stop bacteria growing too fast\nIGNORE enzymes denature at 37 °C` },
      { q: `A patient goes to the doctor with a sore throat and a high temperature. The doctor tells the patient the illness is caused by the influenza (flu) virus. The patient asks for antibiotics, but the doctor refuses.\n\nExplain why the doctor refuses to give the patient antibiotics.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `antibiotics only kill / affect bacteria ✓\nso they will not work against the flu virus ✓\n\nALLOW antibiotics do not kill viruses for first marking point\nALLOW using antibiotics unnecessarily increases antibiotic resistance (in bacteria) as an alternative for either marking point\nDO NOT ALLOW the virus becomes resistant to antibiotics` },
      { q: `Antiviral drugs are used to treat some viral diseases, such as HIV. Developing antiviral drugs that destroy viruses is much harder than developing antibiotics that destroy bacteria.\n\nSuggest why it is difficult to develop drugs that destroy viruses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `viruses live / reproduce inside the body's / host cells ✓\nso drugs that destroy viruses may also damage / kill the body's cells ✓\n\nALLOW viruses are not cells / do not have cell walls or ribosomes for antibiotics to target for first marking point\nALLOW viruses mutate rapidly so the drug may stop working as an alternative second point\nIGNORE viruses are too small` },
      { q: `A drug company is carrying out a clinical trial of a new drug to treat migraine headaches. The trial is double-blind. Some patients are given the new drug and some are given a placebo.\n\nExplain why the trial is double-blind.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `neither the patients nor the doctors know who has the drug / placebo ✓\nso the results are not biased (by the patients' or doctors' expectations) ✓\n\nALLOW doctors cannot influence the results / patients cannot influence their reported symptoms for second marking point\nALLOW to make the results valid / reliable for second marking point\nDO NOT ALLOW so patients don't know which drug they have unqualified for first marking point` },
      { q: `A home pregnancy test detects the hormone hCG in urine. hCG is only made during pregnancy. The test strip contains monoclonal antibodies that bind to hCG.\n\nExplain why the monoclonal antibodies bind to hCG but not to any other substance in the urine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `antibodies are specific ✓\n(the antibody has a) shape that is complementary to / fits only hCG ✓\n\nALLOW monoclonal antibodies are all identical / made from one clone of cells for first marking point\nALLOW binding site matches only hCG\nIGNORE same shape as hCG\nDO NOT ALLOW antibodies are enzymes / active site` },
      { q: `Doctors can now test a patient's DNA before prescribing some medicines. For example, some people have an allele that means a drug used to prevent blood clots does not work well for them.\n\nGive two advantages of using a patient's genome to choose which medicine to prescribe.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nthe patient gets a drug that will work / is effective for them ✓\nfewer side effects / adverse reactions ✓\ncorrect dose can be chosen ✓\nsaves time / money (by not trying drugs that will not work) ✓\ncan identify / predict risk of (genetic) diseases so treatment / lifestyle changes start early ✓\n\nALLOW personalised medicine for first marking point if qualified\nIGNORE cheaper unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists hope to use stem cells to treat people with type 1 diabetes. They plan to grow insulin-secreting cells from stem cells and transplant them into patients.\n\nSuggest two risks of using stem cells in this treatment.\n1 ______\n2 ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nthe cells may be rejected (by the immune system) ✓\nstem cells may divide uncontrollably / form tumours / cancer ✓\ncells may be infected / transfer a virus / infection ✓\ncells may not differentiate into the correct cell type / may not make insulin ✓\n\nALLOW patient may need immunosuppressant drugs\nIGNORE ethical objections\nIGNORE expensive` },
      { q: `Doctors often use waist-to-hip ratio as well as BMI to assess a patient's risk of cardiovascular disease. A rugby player has a BMI of 31 but a waist-to-hip ratio of 0.82.\n\nExplain why the waist-to-hip ratio may give a better indication of this rugby player's health risk than BMI.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `BMI does not distinguish between (mass of) muscle and fat ✓\nwaist-to-hip ratio shows the amount of fat stored around the abdomen / waist which is linked to (higher) risk of disease ✓\n\nALLOW rugby player has a high mass due to muscle so BMI is high for first marking point\nALLOW low ratio shows little abdominal fat so low risk for second marking point\nIGNORE BMI is inaccurate unqualified` },
      { q: `A bacterium divides by binary fission once every 30 minutes. A student puts 200 bacteria into a flask of nutrient broth at the best temperature for growth. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the number of bacteria in the flask after 4 hours. Assume that no bacteria die.\nGive your answer in standard form.\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `8 divisions / generations ✓\n200 × 2⁸ or 51 200 ✓\n5.12 × 10⁴ ✓\n\nALLOW 3 marks for correct answer in standard form with no working\nALLOW 2 marks for 51 200 not in standard form\nALLOW ECF for standard form from an incorrect number\nan answer of 3200 (200 × 2 × 8) scores 0 marks` },
      { q: `A child falls and scrapes her knee. The skin is broken and dirt gets into the wound. That evening she also breathes in dust that contains bacteria.\n\nDescribe how three non-specific defences of the body help to stop pathogens causing an infection in the child.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\nskin acts as a (physical) barrier to pathogens ✓\nplatelets cause blood clotting / form a scab to seal the wound ✓\nmucus traps pathogens / bacteria (in the airways) ✓\ncilia move / sweep mucus (with trapped pathogens) up and out of the airways / to the throat ✓\nstomach acid kills pathogens that are swallowed ✓\nphagocytes engulf / digest pathogens (in the wound) ✓\n\neach defence must be linked to how it protects\nALLOW hydrochloric acid for stomach acid\nIGNORE antibodies / vaccination (specific)\nDO NOT ALLOW cilia kill bacteria` },
      { q: `A person is vaccinated against hepatitis B. Six months later the person is infected with the hepatitis B virus. A doctor measured the concentration of antibodies against hepatitis B in the person's blood.\n\nAfter the vaccination the antibody concentration rose slowly over 2 weeks and reached a low peak. After the infection the antibody concentration rose within 2–3 days and reached a peak about ten times higher. The person did not become ill.\n\nExplain why the person did not become ill after being infected with the virus.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `vaccination produced memory cells / memory lymphocytes ✓\n(on infection) memory cells recognise the antigen (of the virus) ✓\nso antibodies are produced faster / in greater amounts (secondary response) ✓\n(so) the virus is destroyed before it can cause symptoms / damage cells ✓\n\nmax 3\nALLOW white blood cells remember the antigen for first marking point\nIGNORE immune unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW antibodies remember the virus` },
      { q: `MRSA is a strain of bacteria that is resistant to many antibiotics. It can cause serious infections in hospital patients.\n\nExplain how a population of antibiotic-resistant bacteria such as MRSA develops.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a mutation (in a bacterium) causes resistance to the antibiotic ✓\nwhen the antibiotic is used, non-resistant bacteria are killed but resistant bacteria survive ✓\nthe resistant bacteria reproduce / divide and pass on the resistance (allele / gene) ✓\n\nALLOW natural selection for second marking point if described\nALLOW resistant bacteria have less competition for third marking point\nDO NOT ALLOW bacteria become immune\nDO NOT ALLOW antibiotics cause the mutation` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of two antiseptics, X and Y, on the growth of bacteria. The student puts paper discs soaked in each antiseptic onto agar plates spread with bacteria. After two days the student measures the diameter of the clear zone around each disc.\n\nResults (data written for this practice question):\nAntiseptic X: 12 mm, 13 mm, 21 mm, 12 mm\nAntiseptic Y: 16 mm, 15 mm, 17 mm, 16 mm\n\nCalculate the mean diameter for antiseptic X, ignoring any anomalous result, and use the results to identify which antiseptic is more effective.\nMean diameter for X = ______ mm\nMore effective antiseptic = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `identifies 21 (mm) as anomalous / (12 + 13 + 12) ÷ 3 ✓\n12.3 (mm) ✓\nY because it has a larger (mean) clear zone / larger zone of inhibition ✓\n\nALLOW 12.33 or 12 for second marking point\nALLOW 14.5 (including anomaly) for 1 mark out of the first two\nALLOW Y kills more bacteria / more bacteria prevented from growing\nIGNORE Y is stronger unqualified` },
      { q: `A drug company has developed a new drug to treat high blood pressure. Preclinical tests on cells, tissues and animals show that the drug is not toxic.\n\nDescribe the stages of clinical testing that the drug must pass before doctors can prescribe it.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `drug is first tested on (a small number of) healthy volunteers ✓\n(at a) low dose to check it is safe / for side effects ✓\nthen tested on (larger numbers of) patients (with high blood pressure) ✓\nto find the optimum dose / to check it is effective / works ✓\ncompared with a placebo / existing drug in a (double-)blind trial ✓\nresults are peer reviewed (before the drug is licensed) ✓\n\nmax 3\nIGNORE tested on animals (preclinical)\nIGNORE tested on people unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists want to make monoclonal antibodies that bind to a protein found only on the surface of prostate cancer cells.\n\nDescribe how the scientists could produce these monoclonal antibodies.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `inject a mouse with the protein / antigen (from the cancer cells) ✓\nremove lymphocytes / B cells (that make the antibody) from the mouse ✓\nfuse the lymphocytes with tumour / cancer / myeloma cells to make hybridoma cells ✓\nhybridoma cells are cloned / grown (in culture) and the antibodies collected / purified ✓\n\nmax 3\nALLOW white blood cells for lymphocytes\nIGNORE stem cells\nDO NOT ALLOW fuse with prostate cancer cells` },
      { q: `Scientists studied the link between smoking and deaths from lung cancer in men. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCigarettes smoked per day → deaths from lung cancer per 100 000 men per year:\n0 → 10\n1–14 → 78\n15–24 → 127\n25 or more → 251\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in the data and explain how smoking increases the risk of lung cancer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `the more cigarettes smoked (per day), the more deaths from lung cancer ✓\nuse of data, e.g. men who smoke 25 or more a day have about 25 times the death rate of non-smokers / 251 compared with 10 ✓\ntobacco smoke / tar contains carcinogens which cause mutations / cause cells to divide uncontrollably ✓\n\nALLOW positive correlation for first marking point\nALLOW tumour forms for uncontrolled division\nIGNORE nicotine causes cancer\nIGNORE smoking damages lungs unqualified` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disorder caused by a faulty allele. Cells lining the lungs produce thick, sticky mucus. In a trial of gene therapy, patients breathed in a spray containing harmless viruses that carry a working copy of the gene into the cells lining the airways.\n\nThe patients' symptoms improved, but the treatment had to be repeated every few weeks.\n\nExplain how this gene therapy improves the symptoms and why it has to be repeated.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the working gene / allele is inserted into the (airway / lung) cells ✓\nso the cells make the correct / functional protein (and less sticky mucus is produced) ✓\nthe cells lining the airways are replaced / die (and new cells do not contain the gene) ✓\n\nALLOW virus acts as a vector to deliver the gene\nALLOW gene is not passed to new cells / not inserted into stem cells for third marking point\nIGNORE the gene wears off\nDO NOT ALLOW the faulty gene is removed` },
      { q: `A 62-year-old man has a narrowed coronary artery. His doctor discusses three possible treatments with him. (data written for this practice question)\n\nStatins: daily tablets for life; no surgery; can take months to have an effect; side effects can include muscle pain.\nStent: a mesh tube is inserted into the artery through a blood vessel in the arm; patient usually home in 1–2 days; artery can become blocked again.\nBypass surgery: a blood vessel from the leg is used to carry blood around the blockage; major open-heart surgery; 6–12 weeks to recover.\n\nEvaluate the use of a stent compared with the other two treatments for this man.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any four from:\nstent widens / holds the artery open so blood flow (to the heart muscle) improves immediately ✓\nstatins only lower cholesterol so slow further narrowing but do not widen the artery / take months ✓\nstent avoids the need to take tablets every day / avoids side effects of statins ✓\nstent has a shorter recovery time / less risk than bypass (open-heart) surgery ✓\nbut the artery can become blocked again so further treatment may be needed ✓\nstent still has risks of surgery, e.g. infection / blood clots ✓\nbypass may be better if the blockage is severe / there are several blockages ✓\n\nto gain 4 marks at least one advantage and one disadvantage of the stent must be given\nALLOW the man may still need statins / lifestyle changes alongside a stent\nIGNORE cost unqualified` },
      { q: `A drug company tested a new drug to lower blood pressure. 800 patients took part. Half were given the new drug and half were given a placebo. The trial was double-blind. After 12 weeks, the number of patients whose blood pressure fell by at least 10 mmHg was recorded. (data written for this practice question)\n\nNew drug: 312 out of 400 patients\nPlacebo: 148 out of 400 patients\n\nCalculate the percentage of patients in each group whose blood pressure fell by at least 10 mmHg. Use your answers to evaluate whether the drug is effective.\nNew drug = ______ %\nPlacebo = ______ %`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `new drug: 78 (%) ✓\nplacebo: 37 (%) ✓\nthe drug is effective because more than twice as many / 41% more patients improved with the drug than with the placebo ✓\nany one from:\nlarge sample size / double-blind so results are valid ✓\nsome patients improved on the placebo so the placebo effect is significant ✓\nonly 12 weeks so long-term effects / side effects not known ✓\nnot all patients (22%) improved so the drug does not work for everyone ✓\n\nALLOW ECF for conclusion from incorrect percentages\nIGNORE the drug works unqualified` },
      { q: `Tobacco mosaic virus (TMV) infects tomato plants. A gardener notices that some tomato plants in a large greenhouse have leaves with a pale and dark green mosaic pattern. The plants are smaller than usual and produce fewer tomatoes.\n\nDescribe how TMV causes these symptoms and how the gardener could identify that the plants are infected with TMV.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `how TMV causes symptoms, max 2:\nvirus damages / destroys chloroplasts / causes loss of chlorophyll (in parts of the leaf) ✓\nso less photosynthesis takes place ✓\nso less glucose / sugar for growth / making fruit ✓\n\nidentification, max 2:\ncompare symptoms with a reference / identification guide ✓\nuse a diagnostic test kit containing (monoclonal) antibodies against TMV ✓\nsend a sample to a laboratory for DNA / RNA analysis ✓\nlook at the distribution / pattern of infected plants (e.g. spread by contact) ✓\n\nIGNORE look at the virus with a light microscope` },
      { q: `Data from one country show that the percentage of adults who are obese increased from 15% in 1993 to 28% in 2019. Over the same period the number of people with type 2 diabetes more than doubled. Health officials say that smoking has decreased but alcohol consumption remains high. (data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain how lifestyle factors can increase the number of people with non-communicable diseases in a country, and suggest how a government could reduce the number of cases.\n\nIn your answer you should include:\n• named lifestyle factors and the diseases they are linked to\n• ways the government could reduce the number of cases.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains how at least three lifestyle factors increase the risk of named non-communicable diseases AND suggests at least two detailed government measures linked to the factors.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains how at least two lifestyle factors increase the risk of named non-communicable diseases AND suggests at least one government measure.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives at least one lifestyle factor linked to a disease OR a government measure.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way. The information is supported by limited evidence and the relationship to the evidence may not be clear.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nlifestyle factors\n• diet high in sugar / fat / energy leads to obesity\n• obesity increases risk of type 2 diabetes (body cells less responsive to insulin)\n• diet high in saturated fat / cholesterol leads to fatty deposits in arteries, cardiovascular disease / heart attack / stroke\n• high salt intake increases blood pressure\n• lack of exercise increases risk of obesity / cardiovascular disease\n• alcohol damages liver cells causing liver disease / cirrhosis; also linked to brain damage / some cancers\n• smoking: carcinogens cause lung / mouth cancer; carbon monoxide / nicotine increase risk of cardiovascular disease\n• use of data: obesity almost doubled and diabetes more than doubled, suggesting a link\ngovernment measures\n• education / advertising campaigns about healthy diet and exercise\n• tax on sugary drinks / fatty foods / alcohol / cigarettes\n• food labelling (traffic-light labels)\n• free / cheaper access to sports facilities, cycle lanes\n• screening / health checks (BMI, waist-to-hip ratio, blood pressure)\n• ban smoking in public places / raise minimum price of alcohol` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out which of three different mouthwashes is best at killing bacteria. The student has a culture of harmless bacteria, sterile nutrient agar plates, paper discs and the three mouthwashes.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the investigation safely and get valid results.\n\nIn your answer you should include:\n• the aseptic techniques the student should use and why\n• how the student should set up and incubate the plates\n• how the student should measure and compare the results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Describes a full method including several aseptic techniques with reasons, setting up a control, AND how to measure the clear zones and compare using area or diameter, with repeats.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes some aseptic techniques AND a method of setting up the plates with discs, with some reference to measuring the results.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives simple statements about aseptic technique OR about setting up the plates.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way. The information is supported by limited evidence and the relationship to the evidence may not be clear.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\naseptic techniques\n• wash hands / disinfect the bench before and after\n• flame / sterilise the inoculating loop / spreader by heating until red hot, then let it cool\n• work near a Bunsen burner flame (updraught carries microorganisms away)\n• lift the lid of the Petri dish only slightly / for as short a time as possible\n• flame the neck of the culture bottle\n• reasons: to prevent contamination by other / unwanted microorganisms, to stop pathogens growing, to keep the results valid\nsetting up and incubating\n• spread the bacteria evenly over the agar using a sterile spreader\n• soak discs of the same size in each mouthwash for the same time\n• use sterile forceps to place discs on the agar, spaced apart\n• control disc soaked in sterile water\n• tape the lid on with two or four pieces of tape, not all the way round (oxygen can enter, anaerobic pathogens do not grow)\n• label the base and store upside down (condensation does not drip onto agar)\n• incubate at 25 °C (not 37 °C) for 2–3 days\nmeasuring results\n• measure the diameter of the clear zone (zone of inhibition) around each disc with a ruler\n• calculate area using πr²\n• larger clear zone = more effective mouthwash\n• repeat with several plates and calculate a mean\n• dispose of plates safely / autoclave` }
    ]
  }
};
